// Testing/SpecTestRunner.maxon — run every active test in a specs-shv2 directory
// through the shv2 compiler and collect PASS/FAIL results.
//
// Each test is driven end-to-end through the REAL CLI as a SUBPROCESS:
// `<compilerExe> build <fragment> -o <exeBase> --emit-ir`. Spawning (rather than compiling
// in-process) is deliberate — a compiler crash is isolated to the one test that triggered it
// instead of killing the whole runner, and the actual `build` command path (arg parsing,
// output-path derivation) is exercised, not bypassed. A panic inside the compiler exits that
// build non-zero, which surfaces as a FAILED test rather than a dead runner.
//
// Trimmed HARD from v1's SpecTestRunner: no fragment cache, no per-target run-command
// building, no wasm/wasmtime path. Those return when a milestone needs them. What IS
// here: the committed `.test` goldens (`checkTestFragment`), `--update-required` to
// regenerate them, and `--filter` to scope a run to one spec or one test.
//
// The parallel worker pool — the one omission that DID come back — lives next door in
// SpecWorkerPool.maxon. This file no longer walks the spec directory itself: `runOneSpec`
// runs exactly ONE spec, because that is the unit the pool dispatches (one job, one spec,
// one worker). The pool builds the ordered spec list from `specFilePaths` and reassembles
// the workers' answers back into that order.

let SpecFileExtension = ".md"

// The extension the runner writes a SINGLE-file test's source under, and therefore the BASENAME the
// compiler sees for it (`<testName>.test`). It matches the bootstrap runner, which compiles real
// `specs/fragments/<spec>/<name>.test` files (TestRunner.cs) — so a source-path basename baked into
// output, as a P1.9 range-check panic message is (`panic at <name>.test:<line>: …`), reads `.test` on
// both compilers. The compiler accepts any extension for `build`; every diagnostic path is still
// normalized to `<fragment>` for a ```maxoncstderr block (`normalizeCompilerOutput`), so this changes
// no committed fragment. `.src`-directory multi-file staging (below) is unaffected.
let MaxonFragmentExtension = ".test"
let TempSubdirName = ".spec-tmp"

// ⭐⭐ **THE DIRECTORY EVERY TEST BINARY IS RUN FROM — `<checkoutRoot>/temp/` — AND IT IS A CONTRACT
// WITH THE CORPUS, NOT A SCRATCH LOCATION THIS RUNNER IS FREE TO PICK.**
//
// A spec case's relative paths are resolved by the OS against whatever cwd the harness gave the
// process, so the cwd is part of what a `/specs` case ASSERTS — and the corpus was written against the
// bootstrap's answer (`Program.cs`: `Path.Combine(projectDir, "temp")`, handed to
// `TestRunner.RunExecutable` as its `WorkingDirectory`). ⇒ **The name and the depth are both load
// bearing**: `specs/directory.md` reads `Directory.list(FilePath from "../bin")` to find the compiler's
// own `bin/`, which resolves only from a DIRECT CHILD of the checkout root.
//
// ⛔ **THIS RUNNER USED TO PASS NO WORKING DIRECTORY AT ALL, so a test binary inherited the SUITE's
// cwd — the checkout root — and `../bin` pointed at the checkout's PARENT.** MEASURED: four of
// `directory.md`'s nine cases answered 0 or 99 where 42 was pinned, under a compiler with nothing wrong
// with it, while the bootstrap passed all four from the same spec text and the same `stdlib/`. That is
// one corpus with two harness contracts — this project's own signature bug — and it was invisible
// because every OTHER filesystem case in the suite only ever writes and reads paths it made itself,
// which is self-consistent from ANY cwd.
//
// ⚠ It is the SAME directory the bootstrap uses rather than a private one, deliberately: a second
// gitignored scratch child of the root would be a second answer to a question the corpus asks once.
// The two suites cannot run at the same time in one checkout (`TreeLock`), which is what makes sharing
// it safe.
let RunWorkingSubdirName = "temp"

// A multi-file test's staged source DIRECTORY hangs off `<tempDir>/<name>.src`, NOT `<tempDir>/<name>`
// — because `<tempDir>/<name>` is exactly the `-o` output base (`exeBase`, prepareOneTest). With no suffix
// the two collide: on a host whose `executableExtension` is EMPTY (macOS, Linux) `build <dir> -o <dir>`
// resolves the exe path back onto the source directory, the compile reports success but writes no
// runnable file, and spawning the directory fails (126). x64-windows only escaped it because its
// `.exe` suffix made the output land beside the directory rather than on it. The suffix makes the
// source and the exe SIBLINGS on every host; it never reaches a golden (every compiled path is
// normalized to `<fragment>`), so the committed x64 fragments are byte-unchanged.
let MultiFileSourceDirSuffix = ".src"

// A `// --- stdlib-overlay:` case's private checkout hangs off `<tempDir>/<name>.stdlib`, a SIBLING of
// the `.src` directory and never a child of it: `Compiler.collectMaxonSources` recurses into every
// subdirectory of what it is pointed at, so a copy of the whole stdlib placed under `.src` would be
// compiled INTO the test's own program. It is a sibling of `exeBase` too, for `MultiFileSourceDirSuffix`'s
// reason — a distinct suffix is what keeps the source, the exe and this from landing on one path on a
// host whose `executableExtension` is empty.
let StdlibOverlayDirSuffix = ".stdlib"

let DraftStatus = "draft"
let BuildSubcommand = "build"
let OutputFlag = "-o"

// `--emit-ir` (and, for a ```RequiredRuntime case, `--emit-ir-runtime=<name>,<name>`) make the
// subprocess build write a Target-IR sidecar next to the exe (`<exeBase>.ir`); the runner reads it
// back to embed in a codegen fragment. Both spellings are the DRIVER's constants (`Main.maxon`),
// named where the parser that reads them back lives — this file only emits them, and a second copy
// here would be a flag the two halves could come to disagree about. The extension must match the
// driver's `IrFileExtension` (Compiler.maxon).
let IrFileExtension = ".ir"

// Where each test's `--metrics` TSV lands, beside its exe and its IR sidecar. Unlike those two this
// extension is nobody else's contract — the compiler writes wherever `--metrics=` points — so it is
// declared here, by the only code that names it. See `readCompileCpu`.
let MetricsFileExtension = ".metrics"

// Committed-fragment layout: `<specDir>/fragments/<target>/<specName>/<testName>.test`.
//
// ⚖ **THESE `.test` FILES ARE REFERENCE, NOT A GATE** (user ruling, 2026-08-02: *"the goldens are NOT
// supposed to be a gate, they are just for reference"*). The emitted Target IR is still compared
// against the committed golden on every run and every difference is still REPORTED — but the report
// contributes NOTHING to the failed count and NOTHING to the exit code. `--update-required`
// regenerates them, and that diff is still the review.
//
// ⛔ **IT WAS A GATE, AND THE GATE HID A REAL DEFECT.** A cross-target red was filed as *"10 stale
// golden mismatches + 9 others"*; the 9 were nine float programs exiting 1 on x64-linux (PLAN row
// `X5`), and they sat inside that filing for a day because a wall of `FAIL … codegen changed` around
// them read as bookkeeping. A suite goes red for a WRONG ANSWER — a wrong exit code, wrong stdout, a
// failed compile, a leak — and for nothing else, so that a red can only ever mean "something is
// broken" and never "something moved".
//
// ⭐ WHAT THE GOLDEN IS STILL FOR, and why it is still written and still compared. RUNNING the test
// proves the allocation is CORRECT — a value left in the wrong register computes the wrong answer, and
// the exit-code assertion catches it. The golden is the only record of whether the code got WORSE: an
// extra spill, a lost coalesce, a live range needlessly widened all still return the right answer. It
// also reaches where the run cannot — a test executes ONE path, but the golden pins every block of the
// function. That makes it the thing you READ when you change codegen, which is exactly what a
// reference is; what it never was is evidence that anything is broken.
//
// Named, not inline, because the layout + the section punctuation below are a format contract
// shared with spec review.
//
// EXPORTED because the golden-tracking REPORT asks git about the same directory (`GoldenTracking`), and
// the one directory the goldens live in must not be spelled in two files: the report would then go on
// describing a clean tree about a path nothing writes.
export let FragmentsSubdir = "fragments"
let FragmentExtension = ".test"
let FragmentTestPrefix = "// Test: "
let FragmentSectionSep = "---"
let ExitCodeLabel = "ExitCode: "

// What follows `ExitCode: ` in the fragment of a case that pins no exit code (see `fragmentExitCodeText`).
let UnpinnedExitCodeLabel = "unpinned"
let CompilerErrorLabel = "CompilerError:"

// Stand-in for the IR section when the sidecar could not be read — only reachable
// if a supposed exitCode test failed to compile (the run then FAILs regardless),
// so it is a visible signal in the fragment, never the normal path.
let MissingIrPlaceholder = "// (no Target IR: compile produced no sidecar)\n"

// The placeholder that replaces a fragment's on-disk path — in captured compiler output AND in
// the expected text — so what a ```maxoncstderr block compares is the diagnostic, not where the
// file happened to live. Both sides are rewritten by the one `normalizeCompilerOutput`.
let FragmentPathToken = "<fragment>"

// The punctuation of a positioned diagnostic: `<path>:<line>:<column>: <message>`. Splitting the
// text after the path on `:` yields `["", line, column, message…]` — three fields before the message
// begins, and the message may itself contain a `:` (`type mismatch: 'cannot compare int with bool'`),
// so everything from there on is re-joined rather than dropped.
let PositionSeparator = ":"
let MinPositionFields = 3

// DERIVED, not restated: the message begins exactly where the position's fields end. Written as its
// own `3` the two would be free to drift, and a drift would silently truncate a diagnostic's message
// or read a column as one.
let MessageFieldStart = MinPositionFields

// A diagnostic names its file exactly ONCE, so splitting the line on that path yields exactly two
// halves — what came before it, and its `:line:col: message`. Any other count is a line this
// routine does not understand, and it says so by declining to renumber rather than by guessing.
let PathSplitHalves = 2

// Compiler output is line-oriented — one diagnostic, one line — and the renumbering walks it that way.
let LineSeparator = "\n"

// How a rendered diagnostic opens: `error <code>: <message>` (`Diagnostics.render`). Two constants
// rather than one interpolation because the CODE goes between them, and the code is never written down
// here: `targetRefusalIn` takes it from the generated `ErrorCodeRegistry`, because a literal `"E3104"`
// in a source file is a fourth copy of the number space that goes on matching nothing after a renumber.
let DiagnosticLinePrefix = "error "
let DiagnosticCodeSuffix = ": "

// What a compile that REPORTED DIAGNOSTICS exits with (`Main.compileAndReport`). `targetRefusalIn`
// insists on exactly this code rather than on "non-zero", and the difference is the leak gate: a
// compiler that leaked exits 101 having printed the very same diagnostics.
let DiagnosticsReportedExitCode = 1

// How deep the compiler's own diagnostics sit under the case they refused, in the skipped-case census.
// The depth of the CASE itself is `IndentedReport.ResultNoteIndent`, shared with the golden-drift
// trailer; this one is the nesting BELOW it and is the caller's, for the reason `indentedList` states.
let RefusalIndent = "    "

// Where a CPU-fault panic line stops being a claim and starts being an address. See
// `stripFaultRipSuffix`. Named beside the other format contracts because it is one: the
// runtime writes this exact spelling and the runner recognizes exactly it.
let FaultRipSuffixMarker = " at rip=0x"

// The async-trace worker vocabulary the normalizer drops, and the ` [M=` tag it truncates at. These are the
// REFERENCE's spellings — shv2 emits none of them (it is single-M) — but the canonical spec text does, so the
// normalizer has to know them to reduce the EXPECTED side. See `normalizeAsyncTraceStderr`.
let AsyncTraceWorkerMarker = " [M="
let WorkerStartTag = "worker_start"
let WorkerParkTag = "worker_park"
let WorkerWakeTag = "worker_wake"
let WorkerExitTag = "worker_exit"
let TokenSeparator = " "

// The offset for a file whose line numbers ARE the fragment's — every single-file test, where the
// compiled file IS the fragment. Named because it is a real value in the mapping, not an absence.
let NoLineOffset = 0 as LineIndex

// The fragment directory a /specs expectation NAMES — the generic, target-free form the C#
// reference normalizes its diagnostics to before comparing (`TestRunner.cs:1380` rewrites the real
// `specs/fragments-<target>/` down to this). It is therefore the form every ```maxoncstderr block in
// the corpus was authored with, and the only form shv2 has to recognize. shv2 neither reads nor
// writes this directory — see `referenceFragmentPath`. The `/specs` corpus is the acceptance test,
// so its rendering of a path is a format contract, exactly like the fence names in SpecParser.
let ReferenceFragmentDir = "specs/fragments"

// --- The cross-compile-and-run path (restored from v1: buildWasmRunCommand / findWasmtimePath) ---
//
// ⭐ TWO INDEPENDENT QUESTIONS, AND THEY USED TO BE ONE BOOLEAN. `isCrossTarget` answered "is this
// not one of the two native targets?" and its answer was used to mean THREE different things —
// needs an external runner, emits no Target IR, is not the host. For wasm32-wasi, the only
// non-native target there had ever been, all three coincided, so nothing forced them apart.
//
// arm64-linux is the first target that splits them: an ELF cannot be spawned on macOS (so it NEEDS
// an external runner) but it goes through the whole register backend (so it DOES emit Target IR and
// MUST be pinned by a golden). Under one boolean it would have silently lost its golden gate. The
// two predicates below — `needsExternalRunner` and `emitsTargetIr` — are that split, each derived
// from the one fact it actually asks about rather than from a list of target names.
let VendorDirName = "vendor"

// The vendor SUBDIR for wasmtime, which also equals the tool's basename — so it is what
// `hostWasmToolCandidates` (WasmComponent) is handed to build the `vendor/wasmtime/wasmtime[.exe]`
// candidate paths, host-preferred. The upward walk, the host-preferred ordering, and the
// side-by-side `.exe`/extensionless spellings all live THERE now: `findWasmtimePath` reuses that one
// locator rather than growing a second copy that could disagree about which vendored binary is "the"
// one (v1 kept two and paid for it — SpecTestRunner.maxon:588).
let WasmtimeDirName = "wasmtime"

let WasmtimeRunSubcommand = "run"

// `-S cli-exit-with-code=y` enables the gated `cli-exit-with-code` proposal so the component's
// `wasi:cli/exit#exit-with-code` import resolves — the WIT marks it `@unstable(cli-exit-with-code)`,
// and without the flag wasmtime rejects the module. The scalar slice needs no other `-S` setting
// (no file or socket I/O). All `-S` flags MUST precede the module path; anything after it is
// forwarded as guest argv.
let WasmtimeSettingFlag = "-S"
let WasmtimeExitWithCodeSetting = "cli-exit-with-code=y"

// --- The shape of wasmtime's TRAP report (W3) ---
//
// When the guest traps rather than exiting, wasmtime writes its own report to the SAME stderr the
// guest was writing to, and ends the process with its own status. `wasmPanicReport` recognizes that
// report by these three spellings and by nothing else.
//
//     panic at panic.basic.test:4: something went wrong        <- the guest's own bytes
//     Error: failed to run main module `…`                     <- WasmtimeTrapReportMarker
//
//     Caused by:
//         0: failed to invoke `run` function
//         1: error while executing at wasm backtrace:
//         0:    0x36a - maxon!mrt_panic                        <- " - " then "!" then the name
//         1:    0x329 - maxon!fail
//         …
//         2: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed
//
// The marker carries its leading newline and is matched with `findLast`, which makes the split point
// EXACT rather than merely likely: wasmtime writes this line once and it is the last thing on the
// stream, so any earlier occurrence can only be text the guest itself printed. (v1's
// `convertWasmtimeStderr` takes the FIRST occurrence and would cut a panic message that quoted it.)
//
// A frame line is recognized by BOTH separators in order — the address separator, then the
// module/function `!` after it. The `!` alone is not enough: it also appears in the module PATH on
// the `failed to run main module` line, so a test whose name contained one would grow a phantom
// frame. (This is the same class of mis-parse v1's comment warns about for `!=` inside a message,
// answered structurally rather than by hoping the message is tame.)
let WasmtimeTrapReportMarker = "\nError: failed to run"
let WasmtimeFrameAddrSeparator = " - "
let WasmtimeFrameModuleSeparator = "!"

// --- OrbStack: the arm64-linux runner ---
//
// `orb run -m <machine> <exe>` executes a binary that lives on the MAC's filesystem inside a Linux
// VM, which mirrors the Mac's tree at IDENTICAL real paths. That mirroring is the whole reason an
// arm64-linux spec run works from this host with no copying, no image and no cross-mount: the ELF
// the build just wrote under `specs-shv2/.spec-tmp/` is already visible to the VM at the same path.
//
// ⚠ UNLIKE WASMTIME, `orb` IS A SYSTEM INSTALL, NOT VENDORED. There is no `vendor/` tree to walk up
// to and no in-repo copy to prefer; the locator for a system tool is PATH, which is exactly what
// `Executable.name` models. See `requireOrbMachineReachable` for the not-found behaviour.
let OrbExecutableName = "orb"
let OrbRunSubcommand = "run"
let OrbMachineFlag = "-m"
let OrbMachineName = "maxon-linux"

// The cheapest command that proves the machine can execute one — coreutils' no-op, present in every
// Linux image. See `requireOrbMachineReachable`.
let OrbProbeCommand = "true"

// --- WSL: the x64-linux runner (Windows host) ---
//
// `wsl --cd <windows-dir> -e <command>` runs a static x86-64 ELF under WSL2's genuine Linux kernel —
// the Windows twin of the OrbStack path. Because WSL2 is a real kernel, a static, raw-syscall ELF (no
// libc, no dynamic linker) runs with NOTHING installed inside the distro. Two facts shape the
// invocation:
//   * `--cd <dir>` is handed a directory as a RAW Windows path (`C:\...`); WSL translates it to its
//     `/mnt/<drive>/...` mirror itself, so this runner carries no `/mnt` mapping of its own and is
//     robust to a non-default automount root. Everything the guest then names — the binaries, the
//     script, the capture files — is named RELATIVE to that cwd, which is what keeps the path
//     translation entirely WSL's business and none of this file's.
//   * `-e` execs the command directly (no login shell), and exit codes propagate unchanged
//     (`wsl -e ./false` → 1, `./true` → 0, verified) — which is exactly what the run step reads.
// `wsl` is a SYSTEM install located by PATH (CreateProcessW + PATHEXT resolves `wsl`→`wsl.exe`), so it
// is `Executable.name`, the same as `orb` and for the same reason.
let WslExecutableName = "wsl"
let WslCwdFlag = "--cd"
let WslExecFlag = "-e"

// A binary named relative to the `--cd` directory; WSL resolves `./name` against the translated cwd.
let WslRelativePrefix = "./"

// The POSIX shell the batch script is handed to, named at its absolute path for `-e`'s reason: the flag
// execs what it is given with no guest PATH search.
let GuestShell = "/bin/sh"
let GuestShellCommandFlag = "-c"

// ⭐ **THE PREFLIGHT PROBES THE LANE, NOT MERELY THAT WSL EXISTS.** The x64-linux run step executes a
// whole spec's binaries from a generated `/bin/sh` script that bounds each one with coreutils
// `timeout` (see `runWslBatch`), so a distro without either would fail every test in the suite with
// one unexplained status — precisely the 3000-identical-failures outcome `requireRunnerAvailable`
// exists to turn into one sentence. `command -v` is a POSIX shell builtin, so ONE entry proves all
// three facts at once: a distro is reachable, it can execute `/bin/sh`, and `timeout` is on its PATH.
//
// A FUNCTION rather than a module `let`, because it INTERPOLATES: a top-level constant that allocates
// a String would run its initializer outside any test's ownership, and every other constant in this
// file is a literal for that reason.
function wslProbeScript() returns String
	return "command -v {GuestTimeoutCommand} > /dev/null"
end 'wslProbeScript'

// --- THE BATCHED RUN STEP: ONE `wsl` ENTRY PER SPEC ---------------------------------------------
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ENTRY IS THE WHOLE PRICE, SO THE ENTRY IS WHAT THIS SPENDS ONCE.** MEASURED on this host,
// 2026-08-06: a bare `wsl -e /bin/true` costs **137 ms** (20 entries = 2.733 s, almost none of it CPU
// — it is VM-entry latency), while the same **20 executions inside ONE entry cost 0.264 s**, and
// **200 inside one cost 0.139 s**. Once you are inside, the executions are free. The lane entered once
// per TEST — 3246 entries — and therefore paid ~445 s of pure entry latency, ~37 s of its 197 s wall
// at twelve workers.
//
// ⭐ **THE UNIT IS THE SPEC BECAUSE THE SCRATCH DIRECTORY ALREADY IS** (`specTempDirFor`): one worker
// owns a spec for its whole duration, every binary that spec built sits in that one directory, and
// that directory is exactly what the per-test command used to hand `--cd`. So the batch gives each
// program the SAME working directory the per-test path gave it, test for test — which is the only
// reason this is a speed change and not a behaviour change.
//
// ⭐ **THE RESULTS COME BACK THROUGH FILES, NOT A DELIMITED STDOUT STREAM.** A stream needs framing and
// escaping, and a test program emits arbitrary bytes — the worker wire protocol already had to escape
// backslash-laden diagnostics for exactly this reason. Three files per test need no framing, no
// escaping and no ordering assumption, and they are KEYED BY THE BINARY'S OWN FILENAME (`batchKeyFor`),
// which is why reading them back needs no index bookkeeping at all.
//
// ⚠ **THEY LIVE IN A SUBDIRECTORY OF THE SCRATCH DIR, NOT IN IT.** `<test>.out` beside the binaries
// would share a namespace with the compiler's own outputs, where `<name>.ir` and `<name>.metrics`
// ALREADY collide between two tests whose names differ only after their last dot (see
// `prepareOneTest`). A test named `x` and a test named `x.out` are both legal names, and the first
// one's stdout would then BE the second one's executable.
let BatchSubdirName = ".batch"
let BatchScriptName = "run.sh"
let BatchStdoutExtension = ".out"
let BatchStderrExtension = ".err"
let BatchStatusExtension = ".code"

// The shell function the script defines once and calls once per test. One letter because it is written
// once per test into a file nothing but this reads, and because it is spelled from this constant at
// both its definition and its call sites.
let BatchRunFunctionName = "r"

// The shell variable the key `batchCallLine` passes lands in. The script ASSIGNS this name and then
// REFERENCES it three times through `batchKeyShellVar`, which is derived rather than typed again — one
// word in two roles, and a rename that reached only one of them would leave the script expanding a
// variable nothing set.
let BatchKeyShellName = "n"

// The `$`-reference to it. A FUNCTION and not a `let` for `wslProbeScript`'s reason: a module constant
// must be a constant expression, and this one interpolates.
function batchKeyShellVar() returns String
	return "${BatchKeyShellName}"
end 'batchKeyShellVar'

// coreutils' per-execution bound. NAMED here rather than spelled in the script, because
// `requireWslAvailable` probes for exactly this command and the two must be the same word.
let GuestTimeoutCommand = "timeout"

// The suffix `timeout` reads its durations in. Seconds is its default unit; stating it is what keeps
// the interpolated number from being read as anything else.
let GuestTimeoutSecondsSuffix = "s"

// How long a process that ignored the TERM `timeout` sends gets before it is KILLed. A test program
// installs no signal handler at all, so this is only ever paid by something already wedged; it exists
// so that "wedged" cannot become "wedged and unkillable", which would hold the whole batch.
let BatchKillGraceSeconds = 5

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE SCRIPT WRITES WHEN AN EXECUTION NEVER GOT TO WRITE ITS OWN STATUS.**
//
// ⛔ **A NUMBER COULD NOT SAY THIS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TIMEOUT DESIGN.** `timeout` exits **124**
// when it fires — and a program that itself exits 124 is bit-for-bit indistinguishable from it, so a
// hung test would PASS a case pinning 124 and a case pinning 124 could never fail. There is no flag
// that separates them. So the status is written by the execution's OWN shell, INSIDE the process group
// `timeout` signals: either that shell wrote a number (the program ran to completion, whatever it
// exited with — 101, 124 and 139 all arrive intact), or it was killed before it could and this token is
// what the outer script finds instead.
let BatchTimedOutToken = "TIMEDOUT"

// POSIX shell quoting, and it is one rule for every `Args:` value a spec can carry: inside single
// quotes EVERYTHING is literal — a space, a `$`, a backslash, a newline — and the string ends at the
// first `'`. So an embedded quote is spelled by CLOSING the quote, escaping one bare quote, and
// REOPENING it (`'\''`), which is the only case there is.
let ShQuote = "'"
let ShBackslash = "\\"

// `FilePathArray` is NOT declared here. It is `QueryDatabase`'s export, and the top-level namespace is
// FLAT — this file's own copy was file-private and invisible to the flat-namespace check only for as
// long as it stayed unexported. Exporting it (so `HarnessSelfTest.fixtureSpecIn` could collect a
// fixture directory's spec files) turned two harmless declarations into the very thing the check
// refuses, and FAILED the bootstrap build. One declaration, read from wherever it is needed.

// PASS, or FAIL carrying a human-readable reason, or one of the TWO ways a case can end up neither.
// A union (not a `bool` + a message field) so a passing result has no vestigial empty-string message.
//
// ⚖ **THIS IS THE GATE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE GATE** (user, 2026-08-02: *"the gate is the spec tests
// passing"*). A `fail` is an assertion about the PROGRAM that did not hold — a wrong exit code, wrong
// stdout, a diagnostic that did not match, a compile that should have succeeded and did not. Nothing
// about a committed golden reaches this union; drift travels beside it as `GoldenDrift`, which is a
// different question asked of a different artifact and is REPORTED rather than judged.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO NOT-RUN ARMS ARE DIFFERENT FACTS AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED TOGETHER.** `skipped` is about
// the TARGET — the compiler has no lowering for the construct, so no binary exists. `notRunHere` is
// about the HOST — the binary was linked and is fine, and this machine cannot spawn it. Folded into
// one arm, a genuine E3104 regression would hide among three thousand host-limit entries on a cross
// lane, which is the census this project keeps paying for losing.
export union TestOutcome
	pass
	fail(reason String)
	// ⭐⭐ **NOT RUN HERE — AND THAT IS A FACT ABOUT THE TARGET, NOT AN ASSERTION ABOUT THE PROGRAM.**
	// The compiler refused the case's own construct on this target (E3104), so there is nothing to
	// assert: no binary was linked and none could be. It is neither a pass (nothing was proved) nor a
	// fail (nothing went wrong), which is exactly why it is a third arm rather than either of the two.
	// `refusals` is the compiler's own E3104 text, carried so the census can name the CONSTRUCT rather
	// than only the count — see `targetRefusalIn` for why this may never be silent.
	skipped(refusals String)
	// ⭐⭐ **COMPILED HERE, CANNOT BE EXECUTED HERE** — the case built a perfectly good binary for a
	// target whose instruction set or loader this machine has no runner for (a Mach-O on Windows). The
	// COMPILE half of the case was checked in full and its emitted code was compared against the
	// committed golden; only the execution is missing, so it is neither a pass nor a fail.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CARRIES NO REASON, DELIBERATELY.** The reason is a property of the (target, host) PAIR and
	// is identical for every case in the run — `externalRunnerFor` is the one table that states it. A
	// per-case copy would be the same sentence written down once per test and shipped over a pipe.
	notRunHere

	export function isPass() returns bool
		return match self 'p'
			pass gives true
			fail gives false
			skipped gives false
			notRunHere gives false
		end 'p'
	end 'isPass'

	// ⭐ Did a COMPILE happen whose emitted code is worth pinning against the committed golden?
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT `isPass()`, AND CONFLATING THE TWO IS WHAT MADE THE CROSS LANE WORTHLESS.** A
	// `notRunHere` case compiled cleanly and emitted a full Target-IR module; comparing that module
	// against the golden is the ONLY verification such a case can get on this host, and it is a large
	// fraction of what a cross lane is for. A `fail` is excluded because a failing test's IR is noise,
	// and a `skipped` because nothing was emitted at all.
	export function pinsEmittedCode() returns bool
		return match self 'p'
			pass gives true
			fail gives false
			skipped gives false
			notRunHere gives true
		end 'p'
	end 'pinsEmittedCode'
end 'TestOutcome'

// ⭐ What this test's committed golden had to say — the SECOND, INDEPENDENT axis, deliberately not an
// arm of `TestOutcome`.
//
// The two answer different questions about different artifacts: `TestOutcome` is *"did the program do
// what the spec says?"*, this is *"does the committed reference still describe what the compiler
// emits?"*. Folded into one union they would share a fate, and sharing a fate is precisely what the
// ruling above forbids — the report has to be able to say `0 failed` and `35 drifted` in the same
// breath. This file has paid for that conflation once already: `isCrossTarget` answered three
// different questions with one boolean until arm64-linux split them (see `needsExternalRunner`).
//
// ⚠ Only an outcome that PINS EMITTED CODE reaches this axis at all — `pass` and `notRunHere` — and
// that is by construction rather than by convention: `recordFragmentAndCleanup` skips the golden step
// entirely when behaviour already failed, because a failing test's IR is noise, and when nothing was
// emitted. Everything else carries `notPinned`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE "NO COMPARISON HAPPENED" ANSWERS ARE SEPARATE ARMS, AND THAT IS THIS RUNG'S POINT.**
// They used to be one `none`, which read as "nothing to report" and was reported as nothing — so a run
// that MINTED sixteen hundred first-time goldens and a run that COMPARED sixteen hundred committed ones
// printed the identical trailer. Minting is not verification; a golden this host was not allowed to
// write is not verification either; and only `matched` is. A reader has to be able to tell the three
// apart, so the type does.
export union GoldenDrift
	// This case pins no golden: its verdict was a failure or a target skip, or the target emits no
	// Target IR at all (wasm32 — see `emitsTargetIr`).
	notPinned
	// COMPARED against the committed golden, and equal. The only arm that is evidence of anything.
	matched
	// The committed golden differs from what the compiler just emitted. `detail` is the rendered
	// difference and the golden's path — carried, not printed here, because the PARENT prints it in
	// plan order (see `SpecWorkerPool`'s header on why a worker never reports).
	drifted(detail String)
	// There was no committed golden and this run WROTE one — a new case, or a regeneration under
	// `--update-required`. NO PRIOR RECORD EXISTED, so nothing was checked.
	minted
	// There was no committed golden and this run was NOT ALLOWED to write one, because the verdict
	// that would have validated it never happened here (`notRunHere`). See `recordFragmentAndCleanup`.
	unrecorded
end 'GoldenDrift'

// One test's verdict and its golden's, tagged with which spec it came from for the report.
export type SpecTestResult
	export let specName as String
	export let testName as String
	export let outcome as TestOutcome
	export let golden as GoldenDrift

	export static function create(specName String, testName String, outcome TestOutcome, golden GoldenDrift) returns SpecTestResult
		return Self{specName: specName, testName: testName, outcome: outcome, golden: golden}
	end 'create'
end 'SpecTestResult'

export typealias SpecTestResultArray = Array with SpecTestResult

// Captured result of one spawned process: exit code + both streams. The single
// spawn/capture choke point that BOTH the compile step and the run step go
// through, so there is exactly one place that talks to `Subprocess`.
//
// Exported because the scale-test runner spawns compiles too (one per rung) and must go through
// the SAME choke point. A second, private copy of "spawn and capture" is how the two runners would
// drift into disagreeing about what a captured stream is.
export type ProcessCapture
	export let exitCode as ExitCodeValue
	export let stdout as String
	export let stderr as String

	export static function create(exitCode ExitCodeValue, stdout String, stderr String) returns ProcessCapture
		return Self{exitCode: exitCode, stdout: stdout, stderr: stderr}
	end 'create'
end 'ProcessCapture'

// Takes an `Executable` rather than a `FilePath` so a caller can name a tool to be found on PATH
// (`orb`) as easily as one whose exact location it already computed. The distinction belongs to the
// caller — this is only the spawn.
// ⚠ EVERY SPAWN CARRIES A DEADLINE, because `Subprocess`'s default is to wait FOREVER
// (`timeoutMs = 0`) and a merge gate that hangs indefinitely is strictly worse than one that fails:
// it reports NOTHING, so there is nothing to act on.
//
// Not hypothetical. A cross run through `orb` was observed blocking for **18m35s AFTER the guest
// process had already exited and been reaped** (caught inside the VM as `<defunct>`); SIGTERM did
// not clear it, SIGKILL did, and the suite then finished normally. It showed ZERO output for those
// 19 minutes because the runner's stdout is block-buffered when redirected — so the failure mode is
// not merely slow, it is silent. Rate was ~1 in 700 harness-driven execs and 0 in 300 shell-driven
// ones, which puts the fault in the external runner rather than here; the CONSEQUENCE is still this
// harness's to bound, and bounding it is the only part we control.
//
// The value is deliberately ~1000x the headroom it needs rather than tuned to observed timings: all
// 780 tests COMPILE AND RUN in ~90 s serially (~0.12 s per test per phase), so no legitimate spec
// can approach it. It exists to convert an unbounded silent hang into a named failure
// (`SubprocessError.timeout` renders as "timed out after 120000ms"), NOT to police slow tests — a
// tight deadline would inject flakes into a suite that currently has none, which is the one way
// this guard could leave things worse than it found them.
//
// ⚠ EXPORTED BECAUSE THE POOL'S WEDGE WATCHDOG IS DERIVED FROM IT, and must be: this deadline and
// `WedgeWatchdogMs` are NESTED — this one bounds a single subprocess, that one bounds the dispatch
// loop waiting on the worker running it — so the outer must be strictly the longer or it fires first
// and reports this failure as a pool wedge. Do not re-spell either number as a literal; see
// `WedgeWatchdogMs`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE DEADLINE FOR ONE EXECUTION, WHICH IS NO LONGER THE SAME THING AS ONE SPAWN.** The
// batched WSL run performs a whole spec's executions inside a SINGLE `wsl` process (`runWslBatch`):
// each one is bounded by this value from inside the guest, by coreutils `timeout`, and the spawn
// carrying them all is bounded by their SUM (`batchDeadlineMs`). Two units, one number — the guest's
// `timeout` takes SECONDS and `Subprocess` takes MILLISECONDS — so one is DERIVED from the other
// rather than written down twice, which is also what keeps them from drifting apart.
let SpecSpawnTimeoutSeconds = 120
export let SpecSpawnTimeoutMs = SpecSpawnTimeoutSeconds * MillisPerSecond

// `Subprocess`'s own sentinel for "give the child MY cwd" — the EMPTY path, which is exactly what
// `Configuration.create` defaults `workingDirectory` to. Named here rather than spelled at each spawn
// because `workingDirectory` below is a REQUIRED argument and SEVEN of this harness's EIGHT spawns want
// this value (the compile, the git probe, the fixture sub-run, the two `--target` runner probes, the
// bootstrap trace build and the scale rung compile — every one but the RUN step's): seven hand-written
// `FilePath.from("")`s would be seven chances to write something that is not the sentinel, and a wrong
// one is silent — the child simply starts somewhere else.
//
// ⚠ It is a required argument rather than a defaulted one ON PURPOSE. The defect this parameter exists
// to fix (see `RunWorkingSubdirName`) was precisely a spawn that never said where it should run and got
// an answer nobody had chosen; a default would restore that, one call site at a time.
export function inheritedWorkingDir() returns FilePath
	return try FilePath.from("") otherwise panic("SpecTestRunner: the empty path is Subprocess's 'inherit cwd' sentinel and must parse")
end 'inheritedWorkingDir'

// ⚠ `timeoutMs` DEFAULTS, and that is deliberately the opposite decision from `workingDirectory`'s
// above. A working directory is a fact only the CALLER knows, and the defect that made it required was
// a spawn that never said where it should run; the deadline is the opposite — every spawn in this
// harness that performs ONE execution wants the same value, and there is exactly one caller that
// performs many at once and must therefore say so (`runWslBatch`). Defaulting puts the number in one
// place and makes the one site that differs the one site that states it.
export function runProcess(exe Executable, argv StringArray, workingDirectory FilePath, timeoutMs DurationMs = SpecSpawnTimeoutMs) returns ProcessCapture throws SubprocessError
	var config = Configuration.create(exe)
	config.arguments = argv
	config.timeoutMs = timeoutMs
	config.workingDirectory = workingDirectory

	let collected = try Subprocess.runConfiguration(config)
	return ProcessCapture.create(collected.exitCode(), stdout: collected.stdout, stderr: collected.stderr)
end 'runProcess'

// ============================================================================================
// THE CUMULATIVE COMPILE-CPU INDICATOR
// ============================================================================================
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR. The suite already compiles every selected test through a real `build` subprocess,
// and every one of those compiles already MEASURES its own CPU time — `PhaseProbe` brackets each
// phase unconditionally, and `--metrics` decides only whether the numbers are WRITTEN. Summing the
// `total` row across a whole run therefore costs one flag, one small file per test and one read, and
// buys a single number that answers "did compiling this corpus get dramatically dearer?".
//
// ⚠ IT IS AN INDICATOR, NOT A GATE. Nothing passes or fails on it, there is no budget, no golden and
// no verdict, and none may be added: `scale-test` carried exactly that apparatus and it was deleted,
// because a verdict over a noisy number teaches people to edit the instrument rather than explain the
// reading (see .claude/CLAUDE.md).
//
// ⚠ WHY CPU RATHER THAN THE SUITE'S WALL TIME, which `build-cost-log.md` already records. The suite's
// wall time is one number for a whole POOL, so it moves with the core count and with everything else
// on the box — a measured ~5% band before anything about the compiler has changed — and it says
// nothing at all about how much work was done, only about how long the slowest chain of it took. A sum
// of per-compile CPU cannot see preemption, cannot see another process, and does not move with WHICH
// worker happened to run which spec. Measured on this host, same binary, same 3285 tests, two
// consecutive default runs: 677,224,061,729 and 675,563,783,610 ticks — a spread of 0.25%, twenty
// times tighter than the wall-clock band.
//
// ⚠ IT IS NOT WORKER-COUNT INVARIANT, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED — the first draft of
// this comment claimed it was. `threadCpuTicks` is not an instruction count: on Windows it is TSC
// cycles accrued while the thread was ON A CORE (`QueryThreadCycleTime`), so it removes idle and
// preemption but still ABSORBS the slowdown contention causes — an SMT sibling, a shared L3, memory
// bandwidth. The same suite that reads ~676e9 at the default twelve workers reads 407,531,292,437 at
// `--workers=1`: identical work, 1.66x the ticks, purely from running twelve-up.
//
// ⇒ TWO NUMBERS ARE COMPARABLE ONLY AT THE SAME WORKER COUNT, which is why the reported line states
// the count it was measured at. Read a default run against a default run; a `--workers=1` figure
// belongs to a different scale entirely and is not an improvement.
//
// ⚠ THE UNIT IS PLATFORM-DEFINED (TSC ticks on Windows, nanoseconds on POSIX) and nothing converts
// between them; see `CpuTicks`. Two runs are comparable on ONE host, never across hosts.

// What ONE test's compile contributed. A union rather than a tick count where zero means "none",
// because "this compile reported nothing" and "this compile cost nothing" are different facts and a
// zero folds them together in the direction that reads as an improvement.
//
// `unreported` is the ORDINARY case for a `compilerError` test, and that is not a failure of any
// kind: `writeMetrics` is the last step of a SUCCESSFUL compile (see MetricsEmit), so a case that
// passes precisely BY failing to compile can never leave a metrics file. It also covers the rare test
// whose source could not be staged at all, which spawned no compiler. Both are one fact here — no
// measurement exists — and the tally counts them so the gap is visible rather than absorbed.
union CompileCpu
	reported(ticks CpuTicks)
	unreported
end 'CompileCpu'

// A count of TESTS. Its own alias rather than a bare int because the two fields it types are read
// against each other and against the suite's result count.
export typealias TestTally = int(0 to u32.max)

// The indicator's running total, summed per worker and again across workers.
//
// ⚠ THE TWO COUNTS ARE NOT DECORATION. A bare tick total is comparable only against a run of the SAME
// corpus at the SAME coverage — the suite grows (one spec port took it from 3272 tests to 3285), and
// how many of its tests are diagnostic cases decides how much of it the indicator can see at all. With
// the counts beside it a reader can tell a total that moved because the COMPILER did from one that
// moved because the CORPUS did; without them they are indistinguishable.
export type SuiteCpuTally
	export let ticks as CpuTicks
	export let reported as TestTally
	export let unreported as TestTally

	export static function create(ticks CpuTicks, reported TestTally, unreported TestTally) returns SuiteCpuTally
		return Self{ticks: ticks, reported: reported, unreported: unreported}
	end 'create'

	export static function zero() returns SuiteCpuTally
		return Self{ticks: 0, reported: 0, unreported: 0}
	end 'zero'

	export function adding(cpu CompileCpu) returns SuiteCpuTally
		return match cpu 'contribution'
			reported(compileTicks) gives Self{ticks: self.ticks + compileTicks, reported: self.reported + 1, unreported: self.unreported}
			unreported gives Self{ticks: self.ticks, reported: self.reported, unreported: self.unreported + 1}
		end 'contribution'
	end 'adding'

	export function plus(other SuiteCpuTally) returns SuiteCpuTally
		return Self{ticks: self.ticks + other.ticks, reported: self.reported + other.reported, unreported: self.unreported + other.unreported}
	end 'plus'

	// Every test contributes exactly one `CompileCpu`, so this IS the number of tests the tally
	// covers — which is what makes the parent's cross-check against its own result count meaningful.
	export function tests() returns TestTally
		return reported + unreported
	end 'tests'

	// The one line a run reports.
	//
	// ⚠ IT GOES TO STDERR, AND THAT IS A CONTRACT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. `--workers=1` and
	// `--workers=N` must print BYTE-IDENTICAL STDOUT (see SpecWorkerPool's header) — that identity is
	// the suite's sharpest test of async correctness — and a CPU reading differs between any two runs
	// of the same binary. On stdout it would break that gate every time it was run; on stderr it sits
	// beside the other diagnostics, which is what a cost figure is.
	//
	// ⚠ IT STATES THE WORKER COUNT, and that is the measured half of the line rather than decoration:
	// the tick total is 1.66x larger at twelve workers than at one for identical work (see this
	// section's header). Without the count, comparing two runs made at different pool sizes reads as a
	// 40% win that nothing did.
	//
	// Every number is spelled out, including a zero `unreported`, because a shape that dropped a term
	// when it happened to be zero would make two runs of the same suite print two different shapes.
	export function reportLine(workers WorkerCount) returns String
		return "compile CPU: {ticks} ticks from {reported} of {tests()} test compiles at {workers} worker(s) ({unreported} wrote no metrics — a compile that FAILS writes none, which every compilerError case and every case this target refused does by design)"
	end 'reportLine'
end 'SuiteCpuTally'

// What one spec's run produced: its verdicts, and what compiling them cost.
//
// The two travel together because they are counted together: the tally's `tests()` must equal the
// result count, and a shape that could return one without the other is a shape in which they can
// silently disagree.
export type SpecRun
	export let results as SpecTestResultArray
	export let cpu as SuiteCpuTally

	export static function create(results SpecTestResultArray, cpu SuiteCpuTally) returns SpecRun
		return Self{results: results, cpu: cpu}
	end 'create'
end 'SpecRun'

// One test's verdict, its golden's, and what its compile cost — the same pairing as `SpecRun`, one
// level down.
type TestRun
	export let outcome as TestOutcome
	export let cpu as CompileCpu
	export let golden as GoldenDrift

	static function create(outcome TestOutcome, cpu CompileCpu, golden GoldenDrift) returns TestRun
		return Self{outcome: outcome, cpu: cpu, golden: golden}
	end 'create'

	// A test that never reached a compiler: staging failed, or the output path could not be formed.
	// Named rather than spelled at each site so "no compile happened" and "the compile reported
	// nothing" cannot drift into two different tallies. Nothing compiled, so no golden was compared.
	static function notCompiled(reason String) returns TestRun
		return Self{outcome: TestOutcome.fail(reason), cpu: CompileCpu.unreported, golden: GoldenDrift.notPinned}
	end 'notCompiled'
end 'TestRun'

// Run the active tests of ONE spec — `<specDir>/<specName>.md` — through `compilerExe`,
// returning one result per test that `filter` selects, in DECLARATION order (a `draft`
// spec yields none). Throws only on a spec-file IO failure; a per-test compile or run
// failure is captured as a FAIL result, never thrown, so one bad test can't abort the run.
//
// One spec is the unit of work the pool dispatches (see SpecWorkerPool): a worker gets a
// spec NAME on stdin and calls exactly this. Nothing above this function walks a directory
// — the pool does that once, in the parent, because the listing ORDER is also the report
// order and only one process may own it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THREE PASSES OVER ONE SPEC, AND THE MIDDLE ONE IS WHY.** Every test is COMPILED (and, if
// its verdict never needed the program to run, decided) in the first; every test that DOES need its
// program run has that execution performed in the second, all of them together, by whatever this lane's
// runner does best (`executeRuns`); every verdict is finished, compared against its golden and cleaned
// up in the third. On the host lane the middle pass is still one spawn per test and the shape is a
// bookkeeping change only; on the x64-linux lane it is ONE `wsl` entry for the whole spec instead of
// one per test, which is where a measured ~37 s of the suite's wall time was going (see
// `runWslBatch`).
//
// ⚠ **THE THREE ARRAYS ARE ALIGNED WITH `tests`, INDEX FOR INDEX, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE BOOKKEEPING.**
// `prepared` and `observations` each hold exactly one entry per selected test in declaration order —
// including a `notRequired` observation for a test that never ran — so the third pass needs no counter,
// no sorting and no name lookup, and a length that disagrees is a panic rather than a silently
// misattributed result.
export function runOneSpec(compilerExe FilePath, specDir FilePath, specName String, filter String, updateRequired bool, effectiveTarget Target) returns SpecRun throws CompileError
	var results = SpecTestResultArray.create()
	var cpu = SuiteCpuTally.zero()

	let specFile = try parseSpecFile(specDir.join("{specName}{SpecFileExtension}"), effectiveTargetKey: targetToString(effectiveTarget))
	let tests = selectedTests(specFile, specName: specName, filter: filter, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
	if tests.count() == 0 'selectsNothing'
		// A `draft` spec, or one every test of which the filter rejected. The pool never dispatches
		// such a spec — its parent built the same list from the same function — so this is not the
		// path a pool run takes; returning here keeps the scratch directories below from being made
		// for a spec that would write nothing into them.
		return SpecRun.create(results, cpu: cpu)
	end 'selectsNothing'

	let tempDir = specTempDirFor(specDir, specName: specName)
	if not Directory.create(tempDir) 'noTempDir'
		throw CompileError.directoryNotFound(tempDir)
	end 'noTempDir'

	// The cwd each test binary will be spawned with — asked here rather than threaded from the parent
	// because a WORKER is a separate process and derives it itself, exactly as it derives `tempDir`.
	// NOT created here: it is shared by every spec, so the parent makes it once (`prepareSharedDirs`),
	// and N workers racing a mkdir on one directory is what that arrangement exists to prevent.
	let runWorkingDir = specRunWorkingDir()

	// BOTH follow the EFFECTIVE target. The exe suffix must, so the path reconstructed to run matches
	// what the build's `resolveOutputPath` wrote for the same `--target`.
	//
	// ⚠ THE FRAGMENT ROOT MUST TOO, AND IT USED TO BE HOST-KEYED. That was survivable only while the
	// one cross target wrote no golden at all: `--target=wasm32-wasi` skipped the fragment gate, so
	// nothing noticed the root it would have written into was the HOST's. arm64-linux writes goldens,
	// so a host-keyed root would send arm64-linux IR into `fragments/arm64-macos/` and CLOBBER the
	// host's committed tree — a cross run silently rewriting the goldens of a target it did not build
	// for. The pool's `prepareSharedDirs` keys the shared root off the same effective target.
	let exeExtension = executableExtension(effectiveTarget)
	let fragmentDir = fragmentsRootDir(specDir, target: effectiveTarget).join(specName)
	// mkdir -p, but ONLY for a target that has goldens. `recordFragmentAndCleanup` skips the fragment gate
	// entirely when the target emits no Target IR, so creating the tree under wasm32-wasi would
	// leave a directory per spec that nothing will ever write a `.test` into — empty scaffolding for
	// a gate that does not exist. A create failure is not a test gate either way: `checkTestFragment`
	// swallows the ensuing write error.
	if emitsTargetIr(effectiveTarget) 'hasGoldens'
		_ = Directory.create(fragmentDir)
	end 'hasGoldens'

	var prepared = PreparedTestArray.create()
	for test in tests 'eachCompile'
		prepared.push(prepareOneTest(compilerExe, test: test, specName: specName, tempDir: tempDir, exeExtension: exeExtension, fragmentDir: fragmentDir, updateRequired: updateRequired, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget))
	end 'eachCompile'

	let observations = executeRuns(prepared, tempDir: tempDir, runWorkingDir: runWorkingDir, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)

	for (iter, test) in tests.withIterator() 'eachVerdict'
		let step = try prepared.get(iter.index()) otherwise panic("runOneSpec: {prepared.count()} prepared tests for {tests.count()} selected — one is pushed per selected test, in one loop")
		let observation = try observations.get(iter.index()) otherwise panic("runOneSpec: {observations.count()} run observations for {prepared.count()} prepared tests — `executeRuns` answers one per prepared test, including the ones that needed no execution")
		let run = match step 'phase'
			finished(done) gives done
			awaiting(pending) gives finishPendingRun(pending, observation: observation, fragmentDir: fragmentDir, updateRequired: updateRequired, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
		end 'phase'

		results.push(SpecTestResult.create(specName, testName: test.name, outcome: run.outcome, golden: run.golden))
		cpu = cpu.adding(run.cpu)
	end 'eachVerdict'

	return SpecRun.create(results, cpu: cpu)
end 'runOneSpec'

// The tests of `specFile` that this run will actually execute, in DECLARATION order.
//
// THE ONE PLACE THE SELECTION RULES LIVE — the `draft` skip, the `--filter` match, and the two
// per-target gates — and it has to be, because BOTH HALVES OF THE POOL ASK THE SAME QUESTION IN DIFFERENT
// PROCESSES. The parent asks it to decide what the run will report and to weight the job queue
// (`buildPlans`); the worker asks it to decide what to run (`runOneSpec`). Written out twice, the two
// copies could drift — a rule added to one and not the other — and the drift would not read as a bug:
// the parent would sit waiting for records the worker was never going to send, or count tests nobody
// ran. `resultsFromLines` turns that into a panic, but ONE shared rule is what makes the panic
// unreachable rather than merely loud. The target gate is why this discipline matters here and not only
// in principle: it is what makes the same suite green on x64-windows AND arm64-macos, so it MUST be
// applied identically on both sides of the pool.
export function selectedTests(specFile SpecFile, specName String, filter String, effectiveTarget Target) returns SpecTestArray
	var selected = SpecTestArray.create()

	if specFile.status == DraftStatus 'draftSpec'
		return selected
	end 'draftSpec'

	// Resolved ONCE, not per test: the effective target is fixed for the whole run, and both halves of
	// the pool resolve it to the same value (the worker re-parses the parent's forwarded `--target`),
	// so this is the string every test's `targets` list is checked against.
	let effectiveTargetKey = targetToString(effectiveTarget)

	// ⭐ **SELECTION ASKS THE SPEC, NEVER THE SOURCE.** Whether the TARGET can compile what the case
	// WRITES is not asked here and structurally cannot be: that is a property of the case's SOURCE,
	// which only the compiler reads. It is answered where the compiler answers it — see
	// `targetRefusalIn` — and the answer arrives as a SKIPPED result rather than as an absent one, so
	// it is counted and reported instead of vanishing out of the plan. This used to carry a second gate
	// that guessed at it from the `<!-- Args: -->` marker, and the guess was measurably wrong (PLAN row
	// `X2`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO PER-TARGET QUESTIONS ASKED HERE ARE BOTH THE SPEC'S OWN, AND THE SECOND IS THE FIRST
	// ONE DERIVED.** `markerAllowsTarget` reads a restriction the author SPELLED; `targetCanCheckSectionPins`
	// reads one the author DECLARED by writing a ```RequiredData / ```RequiredRdata block, because a
	// section pin names bytes only a native container format has. Both are properties of the spec TEXT,
	// which is why both are answerable before a compiler is spawned and why neither is a guess.
	for test in specFile.tests 'eachTest'
		if selectedByFilter(specName, testName: test.name, filter: filter) and markerAllowsTarget(test, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey) and targetCanCheckSectionPins(test, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget) 'isSelected'
			selected.push(test)
		end 'isSelected'
	end 'eachTest'

	return selected
end 'selectedTests'

// The MARKER's own gate — what the SPEC AUTHOR SPELLED. A gated-out test is not run, not failed and
// not reported, exactly as a draft or filtered-out test is, which is why this lives beside
// `selectedByFilter` under the one door.
//
// UNIFORM rule for EVERY target (native and cross alike): a test runs on ALL
// targets unless it declares a `<!-- targets: … -->` RESTRICTION, in which case it runs ONLY on the
// listed targets. A marker is a restriction for a TECHNICAL reason — an ISA-specific
// register-allocation test (its register pool), a fault-handler-dependent test, a runtime chunk whose
// body only one backend emits — NEVER a per-target opt-in. So an unmarked test runs on x64-windows,
// arm64-macos AND wasm32-wasi; there is no marking to "enable" a target.
//
// ⛔ **A SECTION PIN IS NO LONGER ONE OF THOSE REASONS, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS SENTENCE CHANGED.**
// "A `RequiredData` test (the native `.data` section a wasm module has no equivalent of)" used to head
// that list, and the corpus obeyed it by hand: twenty-odd cases across four files each spelled
// `x64-windows, x64-linux, arm64-macos, arm64-linux`, which is the literal enumeration of
// `targetHasLinkedSections`. `enum-narrow-storage.md` did not, and its six cases panicked in
// `linkedSectionName` under `--target=wasm32-wasi`, killed the worker and aborted the whole lane. That
// restriction is DERIVED now — see `targetCanCheckSectionPins` — so writing it as a marker is writing
// the same fact twice, and the markers came out with the fix.
function markerAllowsTarget(test SpecTest, effectiveTargetKey String) returns bool
	if test.targets.isEmpty() 'unrestricted'
		return true
	end 'unrestricted'

	// An explicit walk rather than an `Array.contains`: the element comparison String equality needs
	// is exactly what a bare `contains` would have to be told, and a restriction list is one to four
	// entries.
	for listed in test.targets 'eachListed'
		if listed == effectiveTargetKey 'restrictionAllows'
			return true
		end 'restrictionAllows'
	end 'eachListed'

	return false
end 'markerAllowsTarget'

// ⭐⭐ **THE RESTRICTION A SECTION PIN CARRIES WITHOUT SPELLING IT — the same shape as
// `markerAllowsTarget` and gated-out the same silent way, but DERIVED from the case rather than read
// off a marker.** A ```RequiredData / ```RequiredRdata block is checked by reading named bytes back out
// of the LINKED IMAGE (`compareLinkedSection`), so a case carrying one asserts something only a
// container format with named sections can answer. Which formats those are is
// `LinkedSection.targetHasLinkedSections`, and it is asked here rather than restated by every author.
//
// ⚠ **THE WHOLE CASE IS EXCLUDED, NOT JUST ITS PIN, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** A case pins a section
// alongside its exit code because both halves are one indivisible claim about one build-and-run
// (`SpecExpectation.run`); running the half a target can answer and silently dropping the other would
// report PASS for an assertion nobody made — the exact shape `RequiredDataFence`'s header refuses. It
// is also precisely what the hand-written markers did, so the corpus's behaviour on every native
// target is unchanged.
//
// ⚠ **SILENT, LIKE A MARKER, AND UNLIKE A `TestOutcome.skipped`.** The counted, reported skip exists
// because the COMPILER's refusal can be wrong — an E3104 emitted in error would delete coverage while
// the suite read green — so it must never be believed quietly. This one cannot be wrong in that
// direction: it is decided from the spec's own text by a total match over `Os`, before anything is
// compiled, and a case it excludes had no verdict to lose.
function targetCanCheckSectionPins(test SpecTest, effectiveTarget Target) returns bool
	if targetHasLinkedSections(effectiveTarget) 'formatHasSections'
		return true
	end 'formatHasSections'

	return not test.pinsLinkedSection()
end 'targetCanCheckSectionPins'

// What the compiler's own stderr says about whether this target could express the case AT ALL.
union TargetRefusal
	// The compiler said something OTHER than "this target has no implementation for that": an ordinary
	// diagnostic, a panic, a crash, or nothing at all. Whatever the verdict was, it stands.
	notRefused
	// EVERY diagnostic the compiler printed is an E3104 — a construct the chosen target cannot provide.
	// `diagnostics` is that text verbatim, because it is the only thing that names the CONSTRUCT.
	refused(diagnostics String)
end 'TargetRefusal'

// ⭐⭐ **THE CROSS-TARGET SKIP — READ OFF THE COMPILER'S OWN VERDICT, NOT OFF A MARKER AND NOT OFF THE
// CASE'S SOURCE TEXT.**
//
// A case whose construct the chosen target cannot provide is SKIPPED: not run, not failed, counted and
// reported. The compiler already knows that fact exactly and states it precisely — `E3104
// targetUnsupportedConstruct`, at the call's own span, naming the runtime entry that has no lowering
// there (`SemanticCheck.requireTargetSupportsCallee`). That diagnostic IS the authority, so this asks
// it rather than keeping a second list beside it.
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE COMPILER AND NOT THE HARNESS.** The harness's previous answer guessed from the `<!--
// Args: … -->` marker a case carries — argv is the only host-only substrate the corpus reaches often —
// and the guess was MEASURED WRONG (PLAN row `X2`, filed by `X1`'s reviewer): `/specs` holds 52
// argv-reading code blocks against 51 markers. `command-line-args.args-length-no-extra` reads
// `CommandLine.args()` with NO marker at all, because the property it pins is precisely that argv[0]
// arrives ALONE — a marker would destroy the test — and `command-line-args.executable-path` is the same
// shape one substrate over (`Process.executablePath`, which is not argv and so never had one). ⇒ the
// marker is SUFFICIENT but NOT NECESSARY, and a rule that is only sufficient cannot be a gate. Marking
// those two cases by hand would have silenced the symptom by lying about the case and left the 53rd
// unmarked one to red again.
//
// ⭐ **IT UN-GATES ITSELF, AND IT HANDLES A PARTIAL LANDING FOR FREE.** The day a POSIX or WASI argv
// substrate lands, the compiler simply stops emitting E3104 on the lane that has it and these cases run
// there, with no edit here and none in any spec file. That is the case the old marker-derived
// expression (`calleeNeedsWin32Substrate(…) and not targetHasWin32Substrate(target)`) structurally
// could NOT express, because it was CALLEE-only: a landing on x64-linux but not wasm would have read
// false on both.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND THE HAZARD IT CREATES IS THE ONE THIS WHOLE MECHANISM EXISTS TO DELETE: a SILENT skip.** If
// the compiler ever emitted E3104 wrongly, cases would vanish and the suite would still read green —
// the same lie a marker on a case that never needed one would tell, arriving by a new road. That is why
// a skip is a RESULT (`TestOutcome.skipped`) rather than an absence, why it travels its own wire record,
// and why `reportTargetSkips` prints the count, the target and the constructs in the trailer. **A skip
// that is not counted must never be added here.**
//
// ⚠ **FOUR THINGS MAKE IT NARROW, AND EACH ANSWERS A DIFFERENT WAY IT COULD SWALLOW A REAL FAILURE:**
//   * only a verdict that did NOT hold is considered at all, so a case that pins E3104 as its own
//     expected diagnostic (`builtins-sleep.rejected-on-wasm`) matches, passes, and is never reached here;
//   * and a `compilerError` case is not converted even when its verdict DID fail, which is the half the
//     line above cannot cover and once did not: see `outcomeOrTargetSkip`, where a MEASURED silent skip
//     of exactly those cases is recorded. The coverage that pins the refusal survives the refusal
//     becoming a skip in BOTH directions only because of that second guard;
//   * EVERY NON-EMPTY LINE the compiler printed must BE an E3104, not merely contain one. One other
//     diagnostic beside it means the compile also had a real complaint, and a real complaint is a
//     failure; one line that is not a diagnostic at all — a panic, a runtime message, anything this
//     harness does not recognise — is likewise not something to interpret as "the target lacks a
//     feature". That is stricter than "no OTHER diagnostic", deliberately: the failure mode being
//     guarded against is a skip that swallows something real, so an unrecognised line must count
//     AGAINST the skip rather than be walked past;
//   * the exit code must be exactly the one a compile that REPORTED DIAGNOSTICS returns. A compiler
//     that LEAKED exits 101 having printed the very same lines, and folding that into a skip would
//     delete the leak gate.
function targetRefusalIn(compile ProcessCapture) returns TargetRefusal
	if compile.exitCode != DiagnosticsReportedExitCode 'notADiagnosticFailure'
		return TargetRefusal.notRefused
	end 'notADiagnosticFailure'

	// The rendered form of a diagnostic is `error <code>: …` (`Diagnostics.render`), and the code is
	// taken from the generated registry rather than spelled — a literal "E3104" here would be a fourth
	// copy of the number space, silently matching nothing after a renumber.
	let refusalPrefix = "{DiagnosticLinePrefix}{ErrorCode.targetUnsupportedConstruct.rawValue}{DiagnosticCodeSuffix}"
	var sawRefusal = false

	for line in compile.stderr.split(LineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		let trimmed = line.trim()
		if trimmed.isEmpty() 'blank'
			continue
		end 'blank'

		if not trimmed.startsWith(refusalPrefix) 'saidSomethingElseToo'
			return TargetRefusal.notRefused
		end 'saidSomethingElseToo'

		sawRefusal = true
	end 'eachLine'

	if not sawRefusal 'saidNothingAtAll'
		// Exit 1 with an empty stderr: a compile that failed without saying why is a defect to see, not
		// a target that lacks a feature.
		return TargetRefusal.notRefused
	end 'saidNothingAtAll'

	return TargetRefusal.refused(compile.stderr.trim())
end 'targetRefusalIn'

// The verdict, unless the target could not express the case — in which case the case did not run here
// and says so. Written as one door because the two halves must not be applied in either order by
// accident: `outcome` is consulted FIRST, so a case whose expectation IS the refusal keeps its pass.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A `compilerError` CASE IS NEVER CONVERTED, AND A PASSING ONE IS NOT THE REASON WHY.** Consulting
// `outcome` first is enough only while such a case PASSES; the moment its pinned text stops matching it
// becomes a FAILED verdict over a compile that exited 1 having printed nothing but E3104s — which is
// bit-for-bit the shape a skip is read off. So the very cases that pin the refusal —
// `builtins-sleep.rejected-on-wasm`, `stdlib-whitelist.target-refusal-blames-the-crossing-call`,
// `console-stdin`/`process-executable-path`/`builtins-clock`'s twins — would turn into SILENT SKIPS at
// the exact moment they broke, and a test that can only pass or vanish pins nothing.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED 2026-08-06 on this branch, twice and two ways: editing `builtins-sleep.rejected-on-wasm`'s
// pinned entry name to `__gt_SABOTAGE` reported `SKIP … / 1 passed, 0 failed`, **exit 0**; a purpose-built
// case pinning a wrong E3104 did the same on x64-linux. Both are FAIL after this guard.
//
// ⇒ **THE QUESTION IS WHO THE SUBJECT IS.** A `compilerError` case asserts WHAT THE COMPILER SAYS, so
// the diagnostics are the artifact under test and a disagreement is an assertion that did not hold. Only
// a case that expected to BUILD AND RUN can be "not runnable here", and that is the one this converts.
function outcomeOrTargetSkip(outcome TestOutcome, expectation SpecExpectation, compile ProcessCapture) returns TestOutcome
	if outcome.isPass() 'itRanHere'
		return outcome
	end 'itRanHere'

	let compilerIsTheSubject = match expectation 'e'
		run gives false
		compilerError gives true
	end 'e'

	if compilerIsTheSubject 'diagnosticsAreTheAssertion'
		return outcome
	end 'diagnosticsAreTheAssertion'

	return match targetRefusalIn(compile) 'refusal'
		notRefused gives outcome
		refused(diagnostics) gives TestOutcome.skipped(diagnostics)
	end 'refusal'
end 'outcomeOrTargetSkip'

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY RESULT IN EXACTLY ONE COLUMN.** The four verdicts a run can produce, counted in one walk.
//
// ⛔ **A TEST THAT FALLS OUT OF EVERY COLUMN HAS BEEN DROPPED FROM THE REPORT**, and a type is what makes
// that checkable: `total()` is computed from the four columns and `SpecVerdictSelfTest` asserts it equals
// the number of results, so an outcome arm nobody counted disagrees loudly instead of vanishing. That is
// the bootstrap's `TestSummary` rule (`SpecRunSelfTest.CheckEveryOutcomeIsCounted`) stated in shv2's terms;
// before this type the counting was three `var`s inside the printing loop, where nothing could ask it
// anything.
export type ResultTally
	export let passed as TestTally
	export let failed as TestTally
	export let skipped as TestTally
	export let notRunHere as TestTally

	export static function create(passed TestTally, failed TestTally, skipped TestTally, notRunHere TestTally) returns ResultTally
		return Self{passed: passed, failed: failed, skipped: skipped, notRunHere: notRunHere}
	end 'create'

	export function total() returns TestTally
		return self.passed + self.failed + self.skipped + self.notRunHere
	end 'total'

	// ⛔ **A RUN THAT PROVED NOTHING IS NOT GREEN**, whichever of the two not-run columns emptied it.
	// The filter selecting nothing is refused one step earlier (`runSpecSuite`); this is the same failure
	// arriving from the far side of the compile — every case refused by the target, or every case
	// unexecutable on this host. Both would otherwise print `0 passed, 0 failed` and exit 0.
	export function provedNothing() returns bool
		return self.passed == 0 and self.failed == 0
	end 'provedNothing'
end 'ResultTally'

// The one walk. EXPORTED because the reporter prints from it and the self-test asserts on it, and a
// second count written inside either is a count that can disagree with the one a reader sees.
export function tallyResults(results SpecTestResultArray) returns ResultTally
	var passed = 0 as TestTally
	var failed = 0 as TestTally
	var skipped = 0 as TestTally
	var notRunHere = 0 as TestTally

	for result in results 'each'
		match result.outcome 'tally'
			pass then passed = passed + 1
			fail then failed = failed + 1
			skipped then skipped = skipped + 1
			notRunHere then notRunHere = notRunHere + 1
		end 'tally'
	end 'each'

	return ResultTally.create(passed, failed: failed, skipped: skipped, notRunHere: notRunHere)
end 'tallyResults'

// ⭐ THE CENSUS OF EVERYTHING THIS RUN DID NOT RUN, and the reason it may never be silent is in
// `targetRefusalIn`'s header: a skip nobody counts is indistinguishable from a case that quietly
// vanished. It names the COUNT, the TARGET and the CONSTRUCTS — the last one straight out of the
// compiler's diagnostic, which is the only artifact that knows which runtime entry was missing.
//
// SILENT when nothing was skipped, which is the whole of the host lane and most of every other: the
// same convention `reportGoldenDrift` follows, where "no note" means zero.
//
// STDERR, for `reportGoldenDrift`'s reason: stdout is the report and stays byte-identical between
// worker counts. The per-test `SKIP` lines and the count are on stdout, in `Main.reportResults`.
export function reportTargetSkips(results SpecTestResultArray, effectiveTarget Target)
	var entries = StringArray.create()

	for result in results 'each'
		match result.outcome 'verdict'
			pass then continue
			fail then continue
			notRunHere then continue
			skipped(refusals) then entries.push(resultNoteEntry(result.specName, testName: result.testName, detail: indentedRefusals(refusals)))
		end 'verdict'
	end 'each'

	// The blank-at-zero guard and the entry loop are `reportResultNote`'s, shared with the golden-drift
	// trailer this one prints above — see `IndentedReport` for why the two are one shape.
	let headline = "note: {entries.count()} case(s) SKIPPED on {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} — NOT RUN and NOT PASSED. The compiler refused a construct this target has no implementation for ({ErrorCode.targetUnsupportedConstruct.rawValue}), which is a fact about the TARGET and not about the case:\n"
	reportResultNote(entries, headline: headline)
end 'reportTargetSkips'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS HOST COULD NOT EXECUTE, AND THE ONE REASON ALL OF IT SHARES.**
//
// The reason is DERIVED, once, from the same table the run was routed by (`externalRunnerFor`) rather
// than carried per case — it is a property of the (target, host) pair and is identical for every entry,
// so a per-case copy would be one sentence shipped over a pipe several thousand times.
//
// ⚠ **IT NAMES THE COUNT AND NOT THE CASES**, which is the opposite choice from the skip census above,
// and deliberately: a skip is a per-CASE fact (which construct did the compiler refuse?) that only the
// entry can carry, while this is a per-RUN fact whose case list is simply "every case that needed to
// run" — several thousand lines saying the same thing. What a reader needs is the number, the reason,
// and the knowledge that these cases were NOT verified here.
//
// SILENT at zero, which is every lane this host can execute — the convention the other two trailers set.
//
// It takes the TALLY the summary already printed rather than re-walking the results: one count, printed
// twice, is one count.
export function reportUnrunHere(tally ResultTally, effectiveTarget Target)
	if tally.notRunHere == 0 'everythingRan'
		return
	end 'everythingRan'

	printError("note: {tally.notRunHere} case(s) COMPILED but NOT RUN on {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} — {unrunnableReasonFor(effectiveTarget)}. Their compiles and their committed goldens were checked in full; only the executions are missing, so they are NOT PASSED.\n")
end 'reportUnrunHere'

// The one sentence saying why this host cannot execute this target, read off the one runner table.
//
// A PANIC on every arm that names a runner: the caller has just counted at least one case the run
// declined to execute, and only `unspawnable` produces those (`runStrategyFor`). Reaching another arm
// means the counting and the routing disagree about the same target.
function unrunnableReasonFor(effectiveTarget Target) returns String
	return match externalRunnerFor(effectiveTarget) 'runner'
		unspawnable(reason) gives reason
		wasmtime panic("unrunnableReasonFor: {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} runs under wasmtime, so no case of it can be unrun — `runStrategyFor` and this census disagree")
		orbstack panic("unrunnableReasonFor: {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} runs under OrbStack, so no case of it can be unrun — `runStrategyFor` and this census disagree")
		wsl panic("unrunnableReasonFor: {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} runs under WSL, so no case of it can be unrun — `runStrategyFor` and this census disagree")
	end 'runner'
end 'unrunnableReasonFor'

// One compiler diagnostic block, indented under the test it belongs to. EVERY line of it, because one
// E3104 per offending call site is normal and the second one usually names a different construct than
// the first (`command-line-args.argv-exec-parity` names both `__cl_count` and `__proc_exe_path`).
//
// The list itself is rendered by `indentedList` rather than by a loop written here — that function
// exists because four files had each written this loop and nothing would have made them agree.
function indentedRefusals(text String) returns String
	var lines = StringArray.create()
	for line in text.split(LineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		let trimmed = line.trim()
		if trimmed.isEmpty() 'blank'
			continue
		end 'blank'

		lines.push("{trimmed}\n")
	end 'eachLine'

	return indentedList(lines, indent: RefusalIndent)
end 'indentedRefusals'

// ⭐ Does a binary built for `target` need an EXTERNAL RUNNER, rather than being spawned directly?
//
// The fact this asks about is "is this the host?", and it asks it of the host itself — NOT of a list
// of target names. A process can spawn exactly one thing: a binary for the machine it is running on.
// Anything else is somebody else's instruction set or somebody else's loader, and needs a program
// that knows how to run it (wasmtime, an OrbStack Linux VM). Written as a name list instead, the
// same target would be "native" on the host that can run it and on the three that cannot — which is
// how `x64-windows` came to be classified native on an arm64-macOS host, where spawning it can only
// fail.
//
// ⚠ THIS IS NOT `not emitsTargetIr`, AND CONFLATING THE TWO IS THE BUG THIS RUNG FIXES. arm64-linux
// answers TRUE here and TRUE there: it cannot be spawned on macOS, and it is fully pinned by a
// golden. See `buildRunCommand` for which runner each answer routes to.
function needsExternalRunner(target Target) returns bool
	let host = detectHostTarget()
	return target.cpu != host.cpu or target.os != host.os
end 'needsExternalRunner'

// ⭐ Does `target` emit a Target-IR module — i.e. is there a golden to pin?
//
// Keyed on the CPU, because that IS the fact: a REGISTER backend lowers Std IR into the Target
// dialect, allocates registers over it and encodes it, and `--emit-ir` writes that dialect out as
// the `.ir` sidecar the `.test` fragments gate. wasm32 has no register file at all (`RegBits
// .registerFileFor` panics for it) — its backend consumes the Std module directly, so there is no
// Target dialect for it to emit and nothing for a golden to hold.
//
// That is what makes wasm's `false` UNFORGEABLE by either predicate drifting: it does not come from
// a name, an OS, or a list that someone must remember to update, but from the same property that
// decides whether the register allocator runs. Every register backend — x64 and arm64, on every
// OS — is TRUE, including the ones that need an external runner.
//
// Exported for the same reason `fragmentsRootDir` is: BOTH HALVES OF THE POOL ASK IT, in different
// processes. The parent gates `prepareSharedDirs` on it; the worker gates the `--emit-ir` flag and
// the fragment write. A second copy in either is a copy that can disagree.
export function emitsTargetIr(target Target) returns bool
	return match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 gives true
		arm64 gives true
		wasm32 gives false
	end 'cpu'
end 'emitsTargetIr'

// This spec's private scratch directory: `<specDir>/.spec-tmp/<specName>/`.
//
// PER-SPEC, and that is a CORRECTNESS requirement, not tidiness. Test names are unique WITHIN
// a spec but not ACROSS specs, so a single shared scratch directory has two workers writing,
// compiling and deleting the same `<testName>.maxon` at the same time — a race that shows up
// as an unreproducible compile failure in whichever worker lost. One spec is only ever in
// flight on one worker, so keying the directory by spec name makes the collision impossible by
// construction, and needs no worker id on the wire to do it.
export function specTempDirFor(specDir FilePath, specName String) returns FilePath
	return specTempRootDir(specDir).join(specName)
end 'specTempDirFor'

// The root the per-spec scratch directories hang off. The pool creates it once, in the parent,
// so N workers don't race `Directory.create`'s mkdir -p on the shared parent.
//
// Built through a FilePath component (not the String overload): `TempSubdirName` is a fixed
// valid relative path, and a FilePath argument picks `join(FilePath)` unambiguously (a bare
// String constant is ambiguous between join's String/FilePath overloads).
export function specTempRootDir(specDir FilePath) returns FilePath
	let tempSub = try FilePath.from(TempSubdirName) otherwise panic("SpecTestRunner: TempSubdirName '{TempSubdirName}' must be a valid relative path")
	return specDir.join(tempSub)
end 'specTempRootDir'

// The cwd every test binary is spawned with — `<checkoutRoot>/temp/`. See `RunWorkingSubdirName` for
// why the corpus, and not this runner, decides where that is.
//
// ⚠ **THE CHECKOUT ROOT IS THE PROCESS CWD, AND THAT IS THIS HARNESS'S EXISTING CONTRACT RATHER THAN A
// NEW ASSUMPTION** — the same fact `HarnessSelfTest.FixturesRelativeDir`, `LadderIndex` and
// `Main.DefaultSpecDir` are all already built on ("Run the suite from the repo root", which one of them
// says in a panic).
//
// ⛔ It is deliberately NOT `specDir.parent()`, which is the obvious reading and is WRONG — MEASURED,
// by `HarnessSelfTest` failing the moment it was tried. A harness FIXTURE SUB-RUN is this same harness
// relaunched with `specDir` pointing at `maxon-shv2/Testing/harness-fixtures/<case>`, so the parent of
// its spec directory is the fixture CORPUS, not the checkout — and the sub-run promptly created a
// `temp/` inside it, which the next sweep read as a fixture case holding zero specs. A run's spec
// directory says which specs to read; it does not say which tree it is standing in.
//
// ⚠ Exported for `specTempRootDir`'s reason, which is the same reason: BOTH HALVES OF THE POOL ASK IT,
// in different processes. The parent creates it once (`prepareSharedDirs`), each worker spawns into it.
// A second copy in either is a copy that can disagree — and this one would disagree SILENTLY, as a
// wrong answer from a test rather than as a missing directory.
export function specRunWorkingDir() returns FilePath
	let runSub = try FilePath.from(RunWorkingSubdirName) otherwise panic("SpecTestRunner: RunWorkingSubdirName '{RunWorkingSubdirName}' must be a valid relative path")
	return Directory.currentPath().join(runSub)
end 'specRunWorkingDir'

// Does `--filter=<pattern>` select this test? The pattern is matched as a
// SUBSTRING of the `<spec>/<test>` label the runner reports — the same string a
// reader sees in a PASS/FAIL line — so one rule covers every way you would want to
// narrow a run: a bare spec name takes that whole spec, a bare test name takes that
// test wherever it lives, and `spec/test` takes exactly one. An empty pattern
// (no `--filter` given) selects everything.
//
// Private: `selectedTests` above is the only door in, so neither half of the pool can apply this
// rule without also applying the `draft` rule that sits beside it.
function selectedByFilter(specName String, testName String, filter String) returns bool
	if filter.isEmpty() 'wholeSuite'
		return true
	end 'wholeSuite'

	return "{specName}/{testName}".contains(filter)
end 'selectedByFilter'

// The committed-fragment root for `target`: `<specDir>/fragments/<target>`
// (e.g. `specs-shv2/fragments/x64-windows`). Each spec's `.test` files hang off a
// per-spec subdirectory of this root.
//
// `target` is the run's EFFECTIVE target, never the host — the goldens belong to the code that was
// GENERATED, and which machine generated it says nothing about that.
//
// Exported for the same reason as `specTempRootDir`: the pool creates this shared parent once,
// in the parent process, rather than letting N workers race to make it.
export function fragmentsRootDir(specDir FilePath, target Target) returns FilePath
	let rel = "{FragmentsSubdir}/{targetToString(target)}"
	let relPath = try FilePath.from(rel) otherwise panic("SpecTestRunner: fragments path '{rel}' must be a valid relative path")
	return specDir.join(relPath)
end 'fragmentsRootDir'

// The `*.md` spec files directly under `specDir`, in directory-listing order —
// deterministic per host filesystem (String is not Comparable, so there is no
// portable key to sort on; NTFS returns names in a stable order).
//
// THIS ORDER IS THE REPORT ORDER. The pool reassembles every worker's out-of-order answers
// back into it, which is how `--workers=1` and `--workers=N` print the same bytes.
export function specFilePaths(specDir FilePath) returns FilePathArray throws CompileError
	let entries = try Directory.list(specDir) otherwise throw CompileError.directoryNotFound(specDir)
	var specs = FilePathArray.create()
	for entry in entries 'eachEntry'
		if entry.fileExtension() == SpecFileExtension 'isSpec'
			specs.push(entry)
		end 'isSpec'
	end 'eachEntry'
	return specs
end 'specFilePaths'

// One path the compiler may PRINT in a diagnostic, and the offset that maps that file's line
// numbers onto the merged fragment's (see SpecSourceFile.mergedLineOffset).
//
// ⭐ **`replacementToken` CARRIES THE FILE'S OWN DIRECTORY, AND WITHOUT IT A MULTI-FILE TEST CANNOT
// SAY WHICH FILE COMPLAINED.** Every staged file used to normalize to the bare `<fragment>` token, so
// `dir_a/helper.maxon:3:1` and `dir_b/main.maxon:3:1` produced the SAME text — a spec pinning the
// diagnostic against one file passed just as well when the other file raised it. The corpus records
// the directory (`error E3088: dir_b/<fragment>:11:9`, `module-keyword`), which is the reference
// runner's shape too: its rewrite matches the temp path, misses, and falls through to a bare-FILENAME
// match that leaves the `dir_b/` in place (`TestRunner.RewriteMultiFileErrorPaths`). A file at the
// test directory's top level carries an empty prefix, which is every multi-file expectation that
// existed before this — so nothing already committed moves.
type FragmentPathMapping
	export let compiledPath as String
	export let lineOffset as LineIndex
	// The whole token a match is replaced by — `<dir>/<fragment>` — built ONCE at staging rather than
	// re-joined at each of `rewriteDiagnosticLine`'s five replacement sites, which would be five places
	// free to disagree about whether the directory rides.
	export let replacementToken as String

	static function create(compiledPath String, lineOffset LineIndex, sourceDirPrefix String) returns FragmentPathMapping
		return Self{compiledPath: compiledPath, lineOffset: lineOffset, replacementToken: "{sourceDirPrefix}{FragmentPathToken}"}
	end 'create'
end 'FragmentPathMapping'

// The `// --- file:` name's directory part, separator included (`dir_b/main.maxon` ⇒ `dir_b/`), or
// empty for a file at the test directory's top level.
//
// Reads the SPEC's spelling rather than the staged path's, so the answer is the corpus's own
// forward-slash form on every host — the staged path is platform-separated and a diagnostic's
// expectation is not a claim about the host that ran it. `isSafeRelativeName` normalizes the two
// separators the same way and for the same reason.
function sourceDirPrefixOf(fileName String) returns String
	let segments = fileName.replace(WindowsPathSeparator, with: PathSeparator).split(PathSeparator)
	if segments.count() < 2 'topLevel'
		return ""
	end 'topLevel'

	var prefix = ""
	for i in 0 upto segments.count() - 1 'eachDirectory'
		let segment = try segments.get(i) otherwise panic("sourceDirPrefixOf: segments.get({i}) failed on a {segments.count()}-part split")
		prefix = "{prefix}{segment}{PathSeparator}"
	end 'eachDirectory'
	return prefix
end 'sourceDirPrefixOf'

typealias FragmentPathMappingArray = Array with FragmentPathMapping

// A test's source, STAGED on disk and ready to compile.
//
// The two shapes differ in exactly one interesting way, and it is the reason this is a type rather
// than a path:
//   • SINGLE-file — one `.maxon` file. The compiled file IS the fragment, so its line numbers are
//     already the golden's.
//   • MULTI-file  — a DIRECTORY of files (`// --- file:`), compiled as a project. The compiler
//     names each file and each file's OWN line numbers, and both must be translated back onto the
//     merged fragment before anything is compared or committed.
type StagedTest
	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `build` IS POINTED AT — a LIST, because the ORDER is part of what a multi-file case
	// SAYS (A3m).** One entry for a single-file test: the fragment. For a multi-file test, one entry
	// per `// --- file:` section in DECLARATION order, followed by the test's source directory.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TRAILING DIRECTORY IS WHAT ROOTS THE PROJECT, AND IT IS NOT REDUNDANT.**
	// `Compiler.commonAncestorOf` derives `Project.rootPath` from the paths it is handed, and
	// `Queries.namespaceAnchorFor` measures every file's NAMESPACE from that root (N1). A case whose
	// every `// --- file:` sits under one subdirectory — `app/main.maxon`, `app/helper.maxon` — has
	// `<testDir>/app` as its files' common ancestor, so naming only the files would silently re-anchor
	// both into the root module and change what the case MEANS. Naming the directory last pins the
	// root at `<testDir>` for every shape, and costs nothing: staging wrote exactly the declared
	// files, and `Compiler.loadSourceFiles` registers a path it has already seen zero more times.
	export let buildTargets as FilePathArray
	// ⭐ WHICH BINARY COMPILES IT — the compiler under test, or, for a `// --- stdlib-overlay:` case,
	// this test's private COPY of it (see `stageStdlibOverlay`). It rides on the staging rather than
	// being threaded from the caller because the overlay is what chooses it, and staging is where the
	// overlay happens: a caller that passed its own would silently compile against the checkout's
	// `stdlib/` and the case would report on a program that was never staged.
	export let compilerExe as FilePath
	// Every path the compiler may name, with the offset that maps it onto the fragment.
	export let paths as FragmentPathMappingArray
	// ⭐ THE LIBRARY ROOT THAT COMPILE WILL LOAD, as a STRING because that is the form a diagnostic
	// carries it in. Asked of `compilerExe` and not of this process: a `// --- stdlib-overlay:` case is
	// compiled by its own copy beside its own `stdlib/`, and the checkout's is not the one it names.
	// See `rewriteStdlibPaths` for what it is for.
	export let stdlibRoot as String
	// What was written, so it can be removed afterwards.
	export let writtenFiles as FilePathArray

	static function create(buildTargets FilePathArray, compilerExe FilePath, stdlibRoot String, paths FragmentPathMappingArray, writtenFiles FilePathArray) returns StagedTest
		return Self{buildTargets: buildTargets, compilerExe: compilerExe, paths: paths, stdlibRoot: stdlibRoot, writtenFiles: writtenFiles}
	end 'create'
end 'StagedTest'

// Staging can fail before a compiler is ever spawned — a write error, or a `// --- file:` marker
// that names a path escaping the scratch directory. A union, so a failure carries its reason and
// there is no half-staged state to inspect.
union StagingResult
	staged(test StagedTest)
	failed(reason String)
end 'StagingResult'

// ⭐ EVERYTHING ONE COMPILED TEST LEFT BEHIND — what the compile said, what it cost, and the artifacts
// it wrote — carried from the compile pass to the verdict pass.
//
// ⚠ **`irText` IS THE SIDECAR'S BYTES, NOT ITS PATH, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN
// A CONVENIENCE.** `<name>.ir` and `<name>.metrics` are `changeExtension`'d from the output path, which
// cuts at the LAST DOT — so on any target whose `executableExtension` is empty (linux, macos) two tests
// whose names differ only after their last dot land on ONE sidecar path:
// `async-linearity.error.double-await` and `…double-await-in-loop` are both `async-linearity.error.ir`,
// and 1,701 of the suite's 4,677 test names contain a dot (RE-MEASURED 2026-08-06; the 1,271 written
// here at this rung was an older count and understated it). That was survivable while a test's life —
// compile, read, compare, delete — happened before the next test compiled. It is not survivable now
// that the golden comparison waits for a batched run, so the bytes are taken at the ONE moment they
// unambiguously belong to this test: immediately after the compile that wrote them. The path collision
// is then harmless by construction rather than by execution order.
//
// ⚠ **THAT COVERS THE READS. IT DOES NOT COVER THE EXE, WHOSE LIFETIME THIS SAME CHANGE EXTENDED, AND
// THE REMAINING INVARIANT IS A PROPERTY OF THE CORPUS RATHER THAN OF THIS FILE.** Every binary a spec
// builds now survives from its own compile until the run pass, so a LATER test in the same spec whose
// sidecar path happens to BE an earlier test's exe path overwrites a binary that has not run yet — a
// test named `foo.ir` beside any test named `foo.<anything>`, or a test named `foo.test` beside a test
// named `foo`, on any target whose `executableExtension` is empty. It cannot become a silent pass: the
// clobbered file is IR text or Maxon source, `exec` refuses it, and the case fails on the exit code and
// on the guest shell's own message in its stderr. MEASURED over all 4,677 test names in `specs-shv2/`,
// 2026-08-06: ZERO such pairs, in any spec, for `.ir`, `.metrics`, `.test`, `.src` or `.stdlib`. Named
// here because the census is the whole guarantee and it dates from outside the compiler.
type CompiledTest
	export let test as SpecTest
	export let staged as StagedTest
	export let compile as ProcessCapture
	export let cpu as CompileCpu
	export let irText as String
	export let exePath as FilePath
	export let irPath as FilePath
	export let metricsPath as FilePath

	static function create(test SpecTest, staged StagedTest, compile ProcessCapture, cpu CompileCpu, irText String, exePath FilePath, irPath FilePath, metricsPath FilePath) returns CompiledTest
		return Self{test: test, staged: staged, compile: compile, cpu: cpu, irText: irText, exePath: exePath, irPath: irPath, metricsPath: metricsPath}
	end 'create'
end 'CompiledTest'

// A compiled test whose verdict cannot exist until its program has been RUN, plus the three halves of
// the expectation that run will be judged against.
//
// The pins are carried HERE, unpacked from `SpecExpectation` at the one place that already knew the
// case was a run expectation, so nothing downstream has to re-match an expectation it has already
// established — and so there is no `compilerError` arm to write a "cannot happen" for.
type PendingRun
	export let compiled as CompiledTest
	export let expected as SpecExitCode
	export let stderr as SpecStderr
	export let stdout as SpecStdout

	static function create(compiled CompiledTest, expected SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout) returns PendingRun
		return Self{compiled: compiled, expected: expected, stderr: stderr, stdout: stdout}
	end 'create'
end 'PendingRun'

// Where one test stood when the compile pass was done with it.
//
// A test is `finished` when its verdict never needed the program to run — a `maxoncstderr` case, a
// compile that failed, a `RequiredData` pin that disagreed with the linked image, a construct this
// target refused — and those are decided, compared against their golden and cleaned up in the compile
// pass exactly as they always were. Only `awaiting` survives into the run pass, which is why a spec of
// nothing but diagnostic cases costs no `wsl` entry at all.
union PreparedTest
	finished(run TestRun)
	awaiting(pending PendingRun)
end 'PreparedTest'

typealias PreparedTestArray = Array with PreparedTest

// Compile one test via a subprocess build (with `--emit-ir` so the Target-IR sidecar is produced) and
// take its verdict as far as it can go without running the program.
function prepareOneTest(compilerExe FilePath, test SpecTest, specName String, tempDir FilePath, exeExtension String, fragmentDir FilePath, updateRequired bool, effectiveTarget Target) returns PreparedTest
	let exeBase = tempDir.join(test.name)
	// The driver appends the platform extension to the `-o` value
	// (resolveOutputPath), so the produced exe is `<exeBase><ext>` — reconstruct
	// that exact path to spawn it for the exit-code check.
	let exePath = try FilePath.from("{exeBase.toString()}{exeExtension}") otherwise 'badExePath'
		return PreparedTest.finished(TestRun.notCompiled("could not form output path for {test.name}"))
	end 'badExePath'
	// `--emit-ir` writes `<output>.ir` (Compiler.writeTargetIr uses the same
	// changeExtension), so the sidecar sits beside the exe under `.ir`.
	let irPath = exePath.changeExtension(IrFileExtension)
	// `--metrics` writes where it is TOLD, so unlike the sidecar this path is chosen here rather than
	// derived by the compiler — sited beside the exe so one `cleanup` removes everything one test made.
	//
	// ⚠ THIS PATH IS NOT UNIQUE, and neither is the `.ir` beside it — see `CompiledTest.irText` for the
	// collision and for the rule that makes it harmless: every artifact is READ in the pass that
	// compiled it, before any later compile in this spec can reuse the name. This one is also DELETED
	// before the compile that writes it (below), so a leftover can never be read as a later compile's
	// cost either.
	let metricsPath = exePath.changeExtension(MetricsFileExtension)

	match stageTest(tempDir, test: test, compilerExe: compilerExe) 'staging'
		failed(reason) then return PreparedTest.finished(TestRun.notCompiled(reason))
		staged(s) then return compileStagedTest(test, specName: specName, staged: s, exeBase: exeBase, exePath: exePath, irPath: irPath, metricsPath: metricsPath, fragmentDir: fragmentDir, updateRequired: updateRequired, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
	end 'staging'
end 'prepareOneTest'

// Compile a test whose source is already on disk and decide as much of its verdict as the compiler's
// answer alone can decide. Split out of `prepareOneTest` only because staging can FAIL, and a union's
// arms are the honest way to say so — the two functions are one story read top to bottom.
//
// ⚠ It takes NO `compilerExe`: staging decides which binary compiles this test (`StagedTest.compilerExe`),
// because a `// --- stdlib-overlay:` case is compiled by its own copy. A parameter here as well would be a
// second answer to that question, and the wrong one would silently compile against the checkout's stdlib.
function compileStagedTest(test SpecTest, specName String, staged StagedTest, exeBase FilePath, exePath FilePath, irPath FilePath, metricsPath FilePath, fragmentDir FilePath, updateRequired bool, effectiveTarget Target) returns PreparedTest
	let pinnable = emitsTargetIr(effectiveTarget)

	var compileArgv = StringArray.create()
	compileArgv.push(BuildSubcommand)
	// In order: a multi-file case's whole point is that the compiler registers its files in the order
	// the case declared them (see `StagedTest.buildTargets`).
	for buildTarget in staged.buildTargets 'eachSource'
		compileArgv.push(buildTarget.toString())
	end 'eachSource'
	compileArgv.push(OutputFlag)
	compileArgv.push(exeBase.toString())
	// The cumulative-CPU indicator's only input. It costs the compile one small file write, and
	// nothing else: `PhaseProbe` is unconditional, so the numbers exist either way (see MetricsEmit).
	compileArgv.push("{MetricsFlagPrefix}{metricsPath.toString()}")
	// ALWAYS explicit, even when the effective target IS the host. The runner states what it is
	// testing rather than trusting the build's default to agree with it — and the two ARE the same
	// value on the host path (`Main.resolveTargetFlag` defaults to `detectHostTarget()`), so this
	// changes no byte of a host build, only who said so.
	compileArgv.push("{TargetFlagPrefix}{targetToString(effectiveTarget)}")
	if pinnable 'emitGolden'
		compileArgv.push(EmitIrFlag)
		// A ```RequiredRuntime block only means anything where there IS a golden, so it rides inside
		// the same gate: under wasm32 there is no sidecar to name functions in.
		if test.runtimeNames.count() > 0 'namesRuntime'
			compileArgv.push("{EmitIrRuntimeFlagPrefix}{joinRuntimeNames(test.runtimeNames)}")
		end 'namesRuntime'
	end 'emitGolden'

	// An `<!-- AsyncTrace -->` case is built with the trace on. Per-CASE, exactly like the golden flags above,
	// so no other case pays for it and an untraced program stays byte-for-byte what it was.
	if test.asyncTrace 'asyncTraceFlag'
		compileArgv.push(AsyncTraceFlag)
	end 'asyncTraceFlag'

	// ⭐ DELETED BEFORE THE COMPILE, NOT ONLY AFTER IT. A run killed part-way through leaves this
	// test's metrics file on disk, and the next run's compile writes one only if it SUCCEEDS — so a
	// failing compile would be credited with the ticks of whichever earlier run last passed here.
	// The instrument would then report a number about a compile that did not happen, which is this
	// project's own recurring stale-artifact trap (see `Compiler.discardPreviousOutput`, which
	// applies the same rule to the exe and the IR sidecar for the same reason).
	try File.delete(metricsPath) otherwise ignore

	let compile = try runProcess(Executable.path(staged.compilerExe), argv: compileArgv, workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'compileSpawnFailed'
		cleanup(staged, exePath: exePath, irPath: irPath, metricsPath: metricsPath)
		return PreparedTest.finished(TestRun.notCompiled("compiler spawn failed: {e.displayReason()}"))
	end 'compileSpawnFailed'

	// BOTH artifacts are read HERE, in the pass that compiled them — the metrics because `cleanup`
	// removes it, the IR because the NEXT test's compile may write to the very same path (see
	// `CompiledTest.irText`). Neither can be deferred to the verdict pass.
	let compiled = CompiledTest.create(test, staged: staged, compile: compile, cpu: readCompileCpu(metricsPath), irText: try File.readText(irPath) otherwise MissingIrPlaceholder, exePath: exePath, irPath: irPath, metricsPath: metricsPath)

	return match preRunVerdict(test, specName: specName, compile: compile, staged: staged, exePath: exePath, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget) 'verdict'
		// ⭐ THE ONE PLACE A CROSS-TARGET SKIP IS DECIDED, and it is here because here is where the
		// compiler's verdict first exists: `compile` has just come back, and nothing downstream re-reads
		// its stderr. It wraps the verdict rather than preceding it, because a case whose EXPECTATION is
		// the refusal must keep its pass — and it is handed that EXPECTATION as well, because a
		// `compilerError` case must keep its FAIL too. See `outcomeOrTargetSkip`.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE `awaiting` ARM NEEDS NO SUCH WRAP, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.**
		// `runPreconditions` yields it only for a compile that exited 0, and `targetRefusalIn` answers
		// `notRefused` for anything but the exit code a compile that REPORTED DIAGNOSTICS returns. A
		// refusal therefore cannot be reachable from a test that got as far as being run.
		settled(outcome) gives PreparedTest.finished(recordFragmentAndCleanup(compiled, outcome: outcomeOrTargetSkip(outcome, expectation: test.expectation, compile: compile), fragmentDir: fragmentDir, updateRequired: updateRequired, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget))
		needsExecution(expected, stderr, stdout) gives PreparedTest.awaiting(PendingRun.create(compiled, expected: expected, stderr: stderr, stdout: stdout))
	end 'verdict'
end 'compileStagedTest'

// The last step of EVERY test, whichever pass its verdict became final in: record the fragment (or
// don't), remove what the test wrote, and hand back the run.
//
// ONE function because the two callers differ only in WHEN they arrive — a `maxoncstderr` case reaches
// it in the compile pass, an exit-code case after its program has run — and a second copy is how the
// golden rule and the cleanup rule would come to differ between two kinds of test.
//
// The fragment records the emitted Target IR for REFERENCE (see `FragmentsSubdir`), and the step is
// skipped in three cases:
//   (0) the case was SKIPPED — no binary was linked and no IR was emitted, so there is no artifact
//       to compare and a "golden missing" note would blame the case for the target's gap. It rides
//       the `pinsEmittedCode` test below, which is exactly the condition;
//   (1) BEHAVIOUR already FAILED — a failing test's IR is noise, and a note about its golden on top
//       would bury the real failure;
//   (2) the target emits NO Target IR (wasm32 alone — see `emitsTargetIr`), so there is nothing
//       to record; the RUN plus the worker-count byte-identity gate are the whole check for it.
// ⚠ The second condition is `emitsTargetIr`, NOT "is this the host". A cross target that goes
// through a register backend — arm64-linux — is pinned exactly like a host build; it was the old
// single `cross` boolean here that would have silently dropped its golden.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE GATE IS `pinsEmittedCode`, NOT `isPass`, AND THAT DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE VALUE OF A LANE
// THIS HOST CANNOT EXECUTE.** A `notRunHere` case compiled cleanly and emitted a full module; comparing
// it against the committed golden is the only verification available for it here, and gating on `isPass`
// threw exactly that away. What `isPass` still decides is the OTHER half — whether a case with no
// committed golden may MINT one — because that question really is "was this verdict established here":
//
// ⚖ **A GOLDEN IS ONLY WORTH THE VERDICT THAT VALIDATED IT** (the bootstrap's mint rule, user ruling
// 2026-08-02, read in shv2's terms). A `notRunHere` case never ran, so a first golden minted from it
// would record output nobody checked — and would then be the reference a LATER, genuine run on a real
// host is compared against, silently blessing whatever it got wrong. That is not hypothetical here:
// MEASURED on this tree, 810 committed `fragments/arm64-macos/*.test` carry `movImm x1, 4294967295`,
// the WINDOWS `ExitCode` bound, on a target whose bound is 255 — minted from a Windows host, before
// `bc21be3e1` fixed the stdlib host-leak, by a run that could not execute one of them. So the compare
// happens and the MINT does not; the refusal is counted and named as `GoldenDrift.unrecorded`, never
// silent. A `compilerError` case on such a target PASSES here and mints normally, which is the same
// rule rather than an exception to it: its verdict really was established on this machine.
function recordFragmentAndCleanup(compiled CompiledTest, outcome TestOutcome, fragmentDir FilePath, updateRequired bool, effectiveTarget Target) returns TestRun
	if not outcome.pinsEmittedCode() or not emitsTargetIr(effectiveTarget) 'noFragment'
		cleanup(compiled.staged, exePath: compiled.exePath, irPath: compiled.irPath, metricsPath: compiled.metricsPath)
		return TestRun.create(outcome, cpu: compiled.cpu, golden: GoldenDrift.notPinned)
	end 'noFragment'

	let fragment = checkTestFragment(fragmentDir, test: compiled.test, compile: compiled.compile, staged: compiled.staged, irText: compiled.irText, updateRequired: updateRequired, mayMint: outcome.isPass())

	cleanup(compiled.staged, exePath: compiled.exePath, irPath: compiled.irPath, metricsPath: compiled.metricsPath)

	// ⚖ ONLY ONE ARM IS A FAILURE. `compared`, `drifted`, `minted` and `unrecorded` all leave the
	// behaviour verdict exactly as the assertions left it — the goldens are reference, reported and
	// never judged. `notWritten` is the odd one out and stays a genuine failure: it is not a verdict
	// about codegen at all but the harness being unable to write a file it was told to write, which is
	// the same class as `stageTest`'s own "could not write fragment" and is failed for the same reason.
	return match fragment 'reference'
		compared gives TestRun.create(outcome, cpu: compiled.cpu, golden: GoldenDrift.matched)
		drifted(detail) gives TestRun.create(outcome, cpu: compiled.cpu, golden: GoldenDrift.drifted(detail))
		minted gives TestRun.create(outcome, cpu: compiled.cpu, golden: GoldenDrift.minted)
		unrecorded gives TestRun.create(outcome, cpu: compiled.cpu, golden: GoldenDrift.unrecorded)
		notWritten(reason) gives TestRun.create(TestOutcome.fail(reason), cpu: compiled.cpu, golden: GoldenDrift.notPinned)
	end 'reference'
end 'recordFragmentAndCleanup'

// The verdict of a test whose program HAS now run.
//
// ⚠ It does not consult `outcomeOrTargetSkip`: a test only reaches here through a compile that exited
// 0, and a target refusal is read off a compile that exited 1 having printed nothing but E3104s. See
// `compileStagedTest`'s `settled` arm, which is the only door onto that conversion.
function finishPendingRun(pending PendingRun, observation RunObservation, fragmentDir FilePath, updateRequired bool, effectiveTarget Target) returns TestRun
	// `asyncTrace` is read off the case itself rather than threaded through `PendingRun` — `CompiledTest`
	// already carries the whole `SpecTest`, so a fifth field would be the same fact stored twice.
	let outcome = checkObservedRun(pending.expected, stderr: pending.stderr, stdout: pending.stdout, observation: observation, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget, asyncTrace: pending.compiled.test.asyncTrace)
	return recordFragmentAndCleanup(pending.compiled, outcome: outcome, fragmentDir: fragmentDir, updateRequired: updateRequired, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
end 'finishPendingRun'

// What this test's compile spent, read back out of the `--metrics` file it was told to write.
//
// ABSENT IS A REAL ANSWER, NOT AN ERROR. `writeMetrics` is the last step of a compile that SUCCEEDED,
// so a `compilerError` case — which passes precisely by failing to compile — can never leave one. The
// ticks it burned are genuinely unmeasurable from here, and the tally counts the test as `unreported`
// so the indicator's coverage is visible instead of being quietly rolled into a total.
//
// MALFORMED IS NOT A REAL ANSWER. A file that exists but whose rows this cannot read means the writer
// and the reader disagree about a format both halves of ONE tree are built from — the same class of
// break the worker-pool protocol panics on, and for the same reason: skipping it silently would buy
// an indicator that under-reports for a cause nobody can see.
function readCompileCpu(path FilePath) returns CompileCpu
	let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise 'noMetrics'
		return CompileCpu.unreported
	end 'noMetrics'

	// `MetricsRowSep`, not this file's own `LineSeparator`: the spec-file text and the metrics TSV are
	// two different formats that happen to agree about newlines today, and only one of them is
	// MetricsEmit's to change.
	for line in text.split(MetricsRowSep) 'eachRow'
		let trimmed = line.trim()
		if trimmed.isEmpty() or trimmed.startsWith(MetricsCommentPrefix) 'notARow'
			continue
		end 'notARow'

		let fields = trimmed.split(MetricsColumnSep)
		if fields.count() != MetricsFieldCount 'malformed'
			panic("readCompileCpu: {path.toString()} has a row with {fields.count()} tab-separated fields, not {MetricsFieldCount} — either this harness and MetricsEmit disagree about the metrics format, or the file is TRUNCATED: `writeMetrics` deliberately swallows a write failure (a full disk must not fail a build), so a partial file is reachable and arrives here looking like a format drift: '{trimmed}'")
		end 'malformed'

		let kind = try fields.get(MetricsKindField) otherwise panic("readCompileCpu: kind field OOB after the count check")
		if kind != MetricsKindTotal 'notTheTotal'
			continue
		end 'notTheTotal'

		let raw = try fields.get(MetricsCpuTicksField) otherwise panic("readCompileCpu: cputicks field OOB after the count check")
		// `parseCount` is the suite's ONE decimal parser (see ScaleTestRunner); its error union is
		// named for the harness that first needed it, and a number this compiler itself wrote cannot
		// fail it.
		let ticks = try parseCount(raw) otherwise (e) 'unreadableTicks'
			panic("readCompileCpu: {path.toString()} has an unreadable cputicks column '{raw}': {scaleErrorText(e)}")
		end 'unreadableTicks'

		return CompileCpu.reported(ticks)
	end 'eachRow'

	panic("readCompileCpu: {path.toString()} carries no `{MetricsKindTotal}` row — MetricsEmit writes one first on every metrics build, so either the format drifted or the file is TRUNCATED (`writeMetrics` swallows a write failure by design, so a zero-length or partial file is reachable). An ABSENT file is not this: it is `CompileCpu.unreported`, counted and reported, never a panic.")
end 'readCompileCpu'

// The `--emit-ir-runtime=` value for a case's ```RequiredRuntime names. A `StringBuilder` rather than
// repeated `String` concatenation for the reason every accumulator in this file gives: a `String`
// owns exactly the bytes it holds and its `append` recopies all of them.
function joinRuntimeNames(names RuntimeFunctionNameArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for (iter, name) in names.withIterator() 'each'
		if iter.index() > 0 'separator'
			out.append(RuntimeNameSeparator)
		end 'separator'
		out.append(name)
	end 'each'
	return out.build()
end 'joinRuntimeNames'

// Write `test`'s source to disk and describe how the compiler's diagnostics map back onto the
// merged fragment. THE one place a test's source reaches the filesystem.
function stageTest(tempDir FilePath, test SpecTest, compilerExe FilePath) returns StagingResult
	if test.isMultiFile() 'multiFile'
		return stageSourceFiles(tempDir, test: test, compilerExe: compilerExe)
	end 'multiFile'

	// A `// --- stdlib-overlay:` section cannot reach this branch: `SpecParser.splitSourceSections`
	// refuses a case that declares one without a `// --- file:` section, because an overlay says what
	// the compile's STDLIB is and never what it compiles.
	let fragmentPath = tempDir.join("{test.name}{MaxonFragmentExtension}")
	if not writeFragment(fragmentPath, test: test) 'writeFailed'
		return StagingResult.failed("could not write fragment {fragmentPath.toString()}")
	end 'writeFailed'

	var paths = FragmentPathMappingArray.create()
	// The compiled file IS the fragment (`fragmentSource` wrote the golden's own bytes), so its
	// line numbers need no translation.
	paths.push(FragmentPathMapping.create(fragmentPath.toString(), lineOffset: NoLineOffset, sourceDirPrefix: ""))

	var written = FilePathArray.create()
	written.push(fragmentPath)

	var buildTargets = FilePathArray.create()
	buildTargets.push(fragmentPath)

	let stdlibRoot = try stdlibRootOf(compilerExe) otherwise return StagingResult.failed(missingStdlibReason(compilerExe))
	return StagingResult.staged(StagedTest.create(buildTargets, compilerExe: compilerExe, stdlibRoot: stdlibRoot, paths: paths, writtenFiles: written))
end 'stageTest'

// A MULTI-file test: one directory, one file per `// --- file:` section, compiled as a project — and
// the build NAMES those files, in the order the case declared them, ahead of the directory itself.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE DECLARED ORDER IS THE COMPILED ORDER (A3m), AND NOTHING NEW SAYS SO.** The `// --- file:`
// sections were already ordered and already staged in that order; the only thing missing was a way to
// TELL the compiler, which `build <file>... <dir>` now is. Five `-either-order` spec pairs exist to pin
// a program's answer in BOTH sibling-file orders and could pin neither: `build` took one path, the
// loader walked raw `Directory.list`, and the order was therefore a property of the staging directory's
// on-disk state rather than of the case. Both halves of every such pair got whichever order the host
// served — two tickets in one lottery. See `StagedTest.buildTargets` for why the directory still rides
// along at the end.
//
// The directory is RECONSTRUCTED, never inherited. A `.maxon` left behind by an earlier version of
// this test would join the build silently and change what the test MEANS — and "the instrument died
// on its own leftovers" is a failure this project has already had twice (OPEN.md #6). Whatever this
// run did not write, this run removes.
function stageSourceFiles(tempDir FilePath, test SpecTest, compilerExe FilePath) returns StagingResult
	// `.src`-suffixed so it never equals `exeBase` (`<tempDir>/<name>`) — see MultiFileSourceDirSuffix.
	let testDir = tempDir.join("{test.name}{MultiFileSourceDirSuffix}")
	if not Directory.create(testDir) 'noTestDir'
		return StagingResult.failed("could not create source directory {testDir.toString()}")
	end 'noTestDir'
	purgeFilesIn(testDir)

	var paths = FragmentPathMappingArray.create()
	var written = FilePathArray.create()
	// Separate from `written` on purpose: an overlay appends its own files to that list below, and a
	// case's private stdlib is not one of the units the program is built FROM.
	var buildTargets = FilePathArray.create()

	for file in test.sourceFiles 'eachFile'
		if not isSafeRelativeName(file.name) 'unsafeName'
			return StagingResult.failed("'{FileMarker}' names '{file.name}': a '.' or '..' segment escapes the test's scratch directory")
		end 'unsafeName'

		let path = testDir.join(file.name)
		let parent = try path.parent() otherwise 'noParent'
			return StagingResult.failed("could not derive a directory for '{file.name}'")
		end 'noParent'
		if not Directory.create(parent) 'noSubdir'
			return StagingResult.failed("could not create directory {parent.toString()}")
		end 'noSubdir'

		try File.writeText(path, content: file.source) otherwise 'writeFailed'
			return StagingResult.failed("could not write {path.toString()}")
		end 'writeFailed'

		paths.push(FragmentPathMapping.create(path.toString(), lineOffset: file.mergedLineOffset, sourceDirPrefix: sourceDirPrefixOf(file.name)))
		written.push(path)
		buildTargets.push(path)
	end 'eachFile'

	var compiler = compilerExe
	if test.stdlibOverlays.count() > 0 'hasStdlibOverlay'
		compiler = try stageStdlibOverlay(tempDir, test: test, compilerExe: compilerExe, paths: paths, written: written) otherwise (e) 'overlayFailed'
			return StagingResult.failed(overlayErrorText(e))
		end 'overlayFailed'
	end 'hasStdlibOverlay'

	// LAST, so every file above is registered before the walk reaches it and the walk finds nothing
	// new — the directory is here to fix the project ROOT, not to contribute a unit.
	buildTargets.push(testDir)

	// Asked of `compiler` rather than of `compilerExe`, so an overlay case's root is the PRIVATE
	// checkout its copy will load and not this one's.
	let stdlibRoot = try stdlibRootOf(compiler) otherwise return StagingResult.failed(missingStdlibReason(compiler))
	return StagingResult.staged(StagedTest.create(buildTargets, compilerExe: compiler, stdlibRoot: stdlibRoot, paths: paths, writtenFiles: written))
end 'stageSourceFiles'

// The `stdlib/` a compile driven by `compilerExe` will load, in the spelling a diagnostic from inside it
// will carry.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE COMPILER'S OWN WALK, CALLED ON THE COMPILER'S OWN DIRECTORY** (`stdlibDirAbove`, which
// `StdlibSource.locateStdlibDir` reaches from `Process.executablePath()`). A second rule here would be a
// second answer to "where is the library", and the way that failure presents is the nastiest kind: the
// rewrite silently does not fire and a case reports the host's absolute path as though normalization had
// never been asked for.
function stdlibRootOf(compilerExe FilePath) returns String throws CompileError
	let exeDir = try compilerExe.resolve(Directory.currentPath()).parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	let root = try stdlibDirAbove(exeDir)
	return root.toString()
end 'stdlibRootOf'

// Why staging stopped when the library could not be located. Its own function because both staging
// shapes report it and the sentence has to say the same thing in both — and because it is worth being
// explicit that this is a STAGING failure rather than a compile one: the compile would have failed too
// (`CompileError.stdlibNotFound`), but it would have failed AFTER the runner had already promised it
// could rewrite what the compiler printed.
function missingStdlibReason(compilerExe FilePath) returns String
	return "no {StdlibDirName}/ above the compiler under test {compilerExe.toString()}, so a diagnostic raised inside the library could not be rewritten to a portable path"
end 'missingStdlibReason'

// Why a `// --- stdlib-overlay:` case could not be staged. A one-arm union implementing `Error` — the
// shape `ScaleError` already uses next door — so the staging steps below read as a `try` chain and every
// failure carries its own sentence rather than a boolean the caller has to explain.
union OverlayError implements Error
	refused(reason String)
end 'OverlayError'

// The sentence a refusal carries, for the one caller that turns it back into a `StagingResult`.
// `scaleErrorText`'s shape, for the same reason: a union arm is how the reason travels, and unwrapping
// it belongs at the boundary rather than at every `throw`.
function overlayErrorText(error OverlayError) returns String
	return match error 'e'
		refused(reason) gives reason
	end 'e'
end 'overlayErrorText'

// ⭐⭐ **A CASE'S PRIVATE STDLIB — the fixture that makes `<stdlibDir>/Builtins.maxon`'s OWN rules
// testable, and the reason it has to exist rather than being a spec someone forgot to write.**
//
// Three of the compiler's refusals can only be reached by a DECLARATION carrying the reserved `__`
// prefix, and exactly one file in a compile may write one — `<stdlibDir>/Builtins.maxon`, matched by
// IDENTITY (`Queries.declaresCompilerInternalNames`). A spec fragment is a throwaway file in a scratch
// directory, so it can never BE that file, and the corpus's own negative controls are what prove it
// cannot fake it by taking the basename. Those findings were therefore recorded as SHELL TRANSCRIPTS in
// `specs-shv2/reserved-double-underscore.md` — three checks that could not fail.
//
// ⭐ **THE LEVER IS `StdlibSource.locateStdlibDir`: A COMPILER FINDS ITS `stdlib/` BY WALKING UP FROM
// ITS OWN EXECUTABLE.** So a COPY of the compiler, placed in a directory that has its own `stdlib/`,
// loads that one — no flag, no environment variable, and no change to the compiler at all. The copied
// tree is this test's own, so the checkout's `stdlib/` is never written to and nothing outside this
// directory can observe the overlay.
//
// ⚠ **ISOLATION, in a suite that runs 12 workers over ONE tree.** The overlay root is
// `<tempDir>/<test.name>.stdlib`, and every term of that is already private: `tempDir` is per SPEC
// (`specTempDirFor`), one worker owns a spec for its whole duration, and a spec's tests run strictly
// sequentially inside it. Two cases cannot share the path even in one spec, because the test's own name
// is in it. It is PURGED before it is written for `stageSourceFiles`' reason — a file a killed run left
// behind would otherwise join a later compile — and every byte it writes is recorded in `written`, so
// `cleanup` removes it.
//
// ⚠ **THE SOURCE OF THE COPY IS THE COMPILER UNDER TEST'S OWN `stdlib/`, NOT THIS PROCESS'S.** They are
// the same directory in every in-tree run and are not required to be: `--compiler=<path>` names some
// other binary, and copying THIS harness's stdlib into a tree that other binary then loads would build
// the case against a library its compiler was never written for.
function stageStdlibOverlay(tempDir FilePath, test SpecTest, compilerExe FilePath, paths FragmentPathMappingArray, written FilePathArray) returns FilePath throws OverlayError
	let exeUnderTest = compilerExe.resolve(Directory.currentPath())
	let exeDir = try exeUnderTest.parent() otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not derive a directory for the compiler under test {exeUnderTest.toString()}")
	let sourceStdlib = try stdlibDirAbove(exeDir) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("no {StdlibDirName}/ above the compiler under test {exeUnderTest.toString()}, so there is nothing for '{StdlibOverlayMarker}' to overlay")

	let overlayRoot = tempDir.join("{test.name}{StdlibOverlayDirSuffix}")
	if not Directory.create(overlayRoot) 'noRoot'
		throw OverlayError.refused("could not create stdlib-overlay directory {overlayRoot.toString()}")
	end 'noRoot'
	purgeFilesIn(overlayRoot)

	// Interpolated so the argument is a committed `String` — see `specTempRootDir` for why a bare
	// module-`let` constant is ambiguous between `join`'s two overloads.
	let stagedStdlib = overlayRoot.join("{StdlibDirName}")
	try copyStdlibSources(sourceStdlib, into: stagedStdlib, written: written)
	try applyOverlaySections(test, stagedStdlib: stagedStdlib, sourceStdlib: sourceStdlib, paths: paths)

	// The copy is spawned, so on a POSIX host it needs the execute bit the original has —
	// `FilePermission.normal` would produce a file the runner then fails to start, which reads as a
	// compiler crash rather than as a staging bug.
	let stagedExe = overlayRoot.join(exeUnderTest.filename())
	let image = try File.readBinary(exeUnderTest) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not read the compiler under test {exeUnderTest.toString()} to copy it beside the overlaid {StdlibDirName}/")
	try File.writeBinary(stagedExe, content: image, mode: FilePermission.executable) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not write {stagedExe.toString()}")
	written.push(stagedExe)

	return stagedExe
end 'stageStdlibOverlay'

// Copy every file the compiler would LOAD from `from` into `into`, preserving the tree.
//
// ⚠ **THE SET OF FILES IS ASKED OF `Compiler.collectMaxonSources`, WHICH IS THE COMPILER'S OWN ANSWER
// TO "WHAT IS A SOURCE UNDER A DIRECTORY?"** A copy built from a second rule here — every `.maxon`,
// say — would drift the day that walk grows a clause (`.maxonignore` is already named as its next
// one), and the drift would show up as a case compiling against a library subtly unlike the one the
// same compiler loads everywhere else.
function copyStdlibSources(from FilePath, into FilePath, written FilePathArray) throws OverlayError
	var sources = MaxonSourcePathArray.create()
	_ = try collectMaxonSources(from, into: sources) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not walk {from.toString()} to copy it for a '{StdlibOverlayMarker}' case")

	for source in sources 'eachSource'
		let relative = try source.relativeTo(from) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("{source.toString()} is not under {from.toString()}, though that walk is what found it")

		let destination = into.join(relative)
		let parent = try destination.parent() otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not derive a directory for {destination.toString()}")
		if not Directory.create(parent) 'noSubdir'
			throw OverlayError.refused("could not create directory {parent.toString()}")
		end 'noSubdir'

		let content = try File.readText(source) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not read {source.toString()}")
		try File.writeText(destination, content: content) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not write {destination.toString()}")
		written.push(destination)
	end 'eachSource'
end 'copyStdlibSources'

// Write each `// --- stdlib-overlay:` section into the copy of the file it names, and record where the
// compiler's diagnostics about it map back to.
//
// ⭐ **THE SECTION GOES AT THE TOP OF THE FILE, AND THAT IS A POSITION CONTRACT.** A diagnostic in an
// overlay has to name a line a reader can find in the spec, and the only line number that does not move
// when the real `Builtins.maxon` gains or loses a line above is one counted from the file's START. At
// the top, the section's own line 1 is the staged file's line 1 — exactly the relation a `// --- file:`
// section's staged file already has — so ONE `mergedLineOffset` rule covers both and the expectation is
// written `<fragment>:<line in the merged block>` like every other. Appending would have made every such
// expectation a hostage to the length of a file the case does not control.
//
// It is legitimate for shv2 because `queryProgramSignatures` sweeps every file's declarations before any
// file is parsed, so where in a module a declaration sits decides nothing.
function applyOverlaySections(test SpecTest, stagedStdlib FilePath, sourceStdlib FilePath, paths FragmentPathMappingArray) throws OverlayError
	var overlaid = OverlaidNameSet.create()

	for overlay in test.stdlibOverlays 'eachOverlay'
		if not isSafeRelativeName(overlay.name) 'unsafeName'
			throw OverlayError.refused("'{StdlibOverlayMarker}' names '{overlay.name}': a '.' or '..' segment escapes the staged {StdlibDirName}/")
		end 'unsafeName'

		// Compared in the corpus's own forward-slash spelling for `sourceDirPrefixOf`'s reason: which
		// separator a spec wrote is not a claim about the host that runs it.
		let key = overlay.name.replace(WindowsPathSeparator, with: PathSeparator)
		if overlaid.contains(key) 'namedTwice'
			throw OverlayError.refused("two '{StdlibOverlayMarker}' sections both name '{overlay.name}'. The second would be written above the first, so neither section's line numbers would be the ones its expectation was written against — merge them into one section")
		end 'namedTwice'
		overlaid.insert(key)

		let relative = try FilePath.from(key) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("'{StdlibOverlayMarker}' names '{overlay.name}', which is not a valid relative path")

		let destination = stagedStdlib.join(relative)
		if not File.exists(destination) 'namesNoModule'
			throw OverlayError.refused("'{StdlibOverlayMarker}' names '{overlay.name}', which is not a file the compiler loads from {sourceStdlib.toString()} — an overlay over a file nothing reads would change nothing and the case would pass having tested nothing")
		end 'namesNoModule'

		let original = try File.readText(destination) otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not read the staged {destination.toString()}")
		try File.writeText(destination, content: "{overlay.source}\n{original}") otherwise throw OverlayError.refused("could not write the overlaid {destination.toString()}")

		paths.push(FragmentPathMapping.create(destination.toString(), lineOffset: overlay.mergedLineOffset, sourceDirPrefix: sourceDirPrefixOf(overlay.name)))
	end 'eachOverlay'
end 'applyOverlaySections'

// The overlay names already written, so a second section naming one file is REFUSED rather than
// silently stacked on top of the first. A Set because membership is the only question.
typealias OverlaidNameSet = Set with String

// Remove every FILE under `dir`, depth-first. The stdlib has no directory-remove, so empty
// subdirectories survive — which is harmless: nothing here reads a directory's existence, only its
// files. Three callers, one rule — a multi-file test's staged sources, a `stdlib-overlay` case's
// private checkout, and the batch's spent capture files — because "whatever this run did not write,
// this run removes" is one sentence, not three.
function purgeFilesIn(dir FilePath)
	let entries = try Directory.list(dir) otherwise return
	for entry in entries 'each'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'isSubdir'
			purgeFilesIn(entry)
		end 'isSubdir' else 'isFile'
			try File.delete(entry) otherwise ignore
		end 'isFile'
	end 'each'
end 'purgeFilesIn'

// Would writing this `// --- file:` name stay inside the test's scratch directory? Enforced at the
// WRITE rather than at the parse, because the write is where the escape would actually happen.
//
// It mirrors the C# reference's rejection of `.` and `..` segments (`SpecParser.cs:284`) and then goes
// FURTHER, because that check has a hole: an ABSOLUTE name has no `..` in it at all, and joining one
// onto a directory DISCARDS the directory (`Path.Combine(tmp, "/etc/x")` is `/etc/x`; the same is true
// of `FilePath.join`). A spec is a file anyone can send you, and the whole point of the check is that
// the name in it cannot decide where the bytes land — so a rooted name, and a Windows drive letter,
// are refused too.
function isSafeRelativeName(name String) returns bool
	if name.isEmpty() 'noName'
		return false
	end 'noName'

	let normalized = name.replace(WindowsPathSeparator, with: PathSeparator)
	if normalized.startsWith(PathSeparator) or normalized.contains(DriveLetterSeparator) 'rooted'
		return false
	end 'rooted'

	for segment in normalized.split(PathSeparator) 'eachSegment'
		if segment == DotSegment or segment == ParentSegment 'escapes'
			return false
		end 'escapes'
	end 'eachSegment'

	return true
end 'isSafeRelativeName'

// What the fragment step concluded. Only ONE of these is a failure — see `recordFragmentAndCleanup`,
// which is the single place that decides what each means for the test.
//
// ⚠ **"COMPARED" AND "WROTE" ARE SEPARATE ANSWERS**, and folding them into one `recorded` is what let a
// run that verified nothing read exactly like a run that verified everything.
union FragmentCheck
	// Compared against a committed golden, and equal.
	compared
	// The committed golden and the fresh output differ. REFERENCE DRIFT: reported, never failed.
	drifted(detail String)
	// There was no committed golden (or `--update-required` asked for a fresh one) and this run wrote
	// it. NOTHING WAS COMPARED.
	minted
	// There was no committed golden and this verdict may not write one — see `recordFragmentAndCleanup`
	// for the rule. Nothing was compared and nothing was written.
	unrecorded
	// The file could not be written. An IO failure of the harness, not a verdict about codegen.
	notWritten(reason String)
end 'FragmentCheck'

// The committed `.test` fragment for one test — its source plus either the generated Target IR
// (exitCode tests) or the normalized compiler error (compilerError tests) — compared against what
// the compiler just produced.
//
// ⚖ IT IS A REFERENCE, NOT A GATE (see `FragmentsSubdir` for the ruling and for what a gate cost us).
// Running the test proves the allocation is CORRECT (a value in the wrong register computes the wrong
// answer and the exit-code assertion fails). It cannot show whether the allocation got WORSE: an extra
// spill, a lost coalesce, a wider live range all still compute the right answer, and nothing but the
// recorded IR can see them. So the comparison stays and every difference is REPORTED — it just does
// not decide anything, because a difference is a change to read, not a claim that something is broken.
//
// It also reaches where the run cannot. A test executes ONE path; a function's other blocks are never
// entered, so a bad allocation in them is invisible to the exit code. The fragment records the WHOLE
// function, every block, taken or not.
//
// `--update-required` regenerates instead of comparing. That is still the door a change enters
// through, and it is deliberately a door you have to open: the diff is the review.
//
// ⭐ **A GOLDEN IS INDEPENDENT OF WHICH OTHER TESTS RAN, AND `scripts/remote-mac.sh` RELIES ON IT.**
// The content compared (or written) here comes from ONE compile of ONE test: `runOneSpec` walks its
// selected tests one at a time and `stageTest` puts a single source file on disk for each, which a
// single `build` subprocess compiles alone. Nothing is shared between two tests — no module, no
// literal pool — so `--filter` cannot move a byte of any fragment it does not exclude, and a
// FILTERED `--update-required` regenerates exactly what an unfiltered one would. That is what lets
// the remote-golden script carry an arm64 run's `specs-shv2/fragments` home from a filtered run; the
// C# suite, which batches a spec's tests into one module, is excluded there for exactly the reason
// this runner does not need to be. ⚠ If shv2 ever compiles several tests together, that script's
// exclusion list is the OTHER end of this invariant and must change with it.
//
// `mayMint` is the caller's verdict-derived permission to WRITE a first golden — see
// `recordFragmentAndCleanup`, which is the one place it is decided. It gates both write doors, because
// `--update-required` writes unvalidated content exactly as a first mint does.
function checkTestFragment(fragmentDir FilePath, test SpecTest, compile ProcessCapture, staged StagedTest, irText String, updateRequired bool, mayMint bool) returns FragmentCheck
	// The fragment records the CODE, so a run expectation contributes only its exit code and
	// its IR — a pinned ```stderr is an assertion about the RUN, checked there, and adding
	// it here would move every golden for nothing.
	let content = match test.expectation 'e'
		run(expected, _, _, _) gives buildExitCodeFragment(test, expected: expected, irText: irText)
		compilerError gives buildErrorFragment(test, compile: compile, staged: staged)
	end 'e'

	let outPath = fragmentDir.join("{test.name}{FragmentExtension}")

	// No committed fragment yet — a NEW test, or a lane that has never seen this case. Record it if
	// this verdict is allowed to; there is nothing to compare against either way.
	let committed = try File.readText(outPath) otherwise 'noGolden'
		return writeFragmentFile(outPath, content: content, what: "new", mayMint: mayMint)
	end 'noGolden'

	if updateRequired 'regenerate'
		return writeFragmentFile(outPath, content: content, what: "updated", mayMint: mayMint)
	end 'regenerate'

	if committed == content 'unchanged'
		return FragmentCheck.compared
	end 'unchanged'

	return FragmentCheck.drifted("{firstDifference(committed, actual: content)}\n    {outPath.toString()}")
end 'checkTestFragment'

// Write one golden, turning the IO failure into the arm that says so. Both write sites go through it:
// they differ only in the word the message uses, and spelling the failure twice is how the two could
// come to disagree about whether it is a failure at all.
//
// ⭐ **THE MINT PERMISSION IS CHECKED HERE FOR THAT SAME REASON** — it is a rule about WRITING, and
// this is the one place a golden is written. A first mint and an `--update-required` regeneration are
// both a fresh reference produced from this run's compile, so both are refused by exactly the verdict
// that could not validate one (see `recordFragmentAndCleanup`).
function writeFragmentFile(outPath FilePath, content String, what String, mayMint bool) returns FragmentCheck
	if not mayMint 'unvalidatedHere'
		return FragmentCheck.unrecorded
	end 'unvalidatedHere'

	try File.writeText(outPath, content: content) otherwise 'writeFailed'
		return FragmentCheck.notWritten("could not write {what} golden fragment {outPath.toString()}")
	end 'writeFailed'
	return FragmentCheck.minted
end 'writeFragmentFile'

// The first line at which the committed golden and the freshly generated fragment diverge, rendered
// for the drift report. A whole-fragment dump would bury the one line that matters under a hundred
// that did not move.
function firstDifference(committed String, actual String) returns String
	let oldLines = committed.split("\n")
	let newLines = actual.split("\n")
	let shared = oldLines.count() if oldLines.count() < newLines.count() else newLines.count()

	for i in 0 upto shared 'eachLine'
		let o = try oldLines.get(i) otherwise panic("firstDifference: oldLines.get OOB at {i}")
		let n = try newLines.get(i) otherwise panic("firstDifference: newLines.get OOB at {i}")
		if o != n 'diverged'
			return "    line {i + 1}:\n      golden: {o}\n      actual: {n}"
		end 'diverged'
	end 'eachLine'

	return "    golden has {oldLines.count()} lines, actual has {newLines.count()} — one is a prefix of the other"
end 'firstDifference'

// The EXACT BYTES of the fragment the compiler is handed: a ONE-LINE `// Test: <name>` header,
// the test's ```maxon block verbatim, and a final newline (the lexer needs one so it doesn't hit
// EOF mid-statement; `readFence` joins content lines WITHOUT a trailing newline, and a fence whose
// last line is blank already ends in one).
//
// THE HEADER LINE IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is exactly one line for a reason: the C# reference
// runner writes the same header (`TestRunner.cs:1719`), so a diagnostic's `line` here EQUALS the
// reference's for the same source. That is what makes the /specs corpus's ```maxoncstderr blocks
// — every one of them authored against the reference — portable BYTE-IDENTICALLY. Drop the header
// and all 442 of them are off by one, for no reason a reader of either spec could see.
//
// THE TRAILING NEWLINE IS PART OF THAT CONTRACT, not a detail of the writer. `writeFragment` writes
// this string and NOTHING ELSE, and the golden's source section is this string and NOTHING ELSE —
// so the file the compiler compiled and the file the golden shows are the same bytes BY
// CONSTRUCTION. Settle the newline anywhere but here and the two drift apart the first time a spec
// ends a fence with a blank line: the golden grows a line the compiler never saw, and a golden that
// describes a file that was never compiled is a golden nobody can trust to explain a `line:col`.
function fragmentSource(test SpecTest) returns String
	let text = "{FragmentTestPrefix}{test.name}\n{test.source}"
	return text if text.endsWith("\n") else "{text}\n"
end 'fragmentSource'

// The shared fragment head both expectation kinds carry: the compiled fragment's bytes verbatim,
// then the section separator that closes the source block.
function fragmentHeader(test SpecTest) returns String
	return "{fragmentSource(test)}{FragmentSectionSep}\n"
end 'fragmentHeader'

// An exitCode test's fragment: the shared head, the expected exit code, and the generated Target IR.
//
// ⚠ The IR arrives as BYTES rather than as a path to read now, because "now" is too late: the sidecar
// was read in the pass that compiled it, and by here another test may already own that filename. See
// `CompiledTest.irText`.
function buildExitCodeFragment(test SpecTest, expected SpecExitCode, irText String) returns String
	return "{fragmentHeader(test)}{ExitCodeLabel}{fragmentExitCodeText(expected)}\n{FragmentSectionSep}\n{irText}"
end 'buildExitCodeFragment'

// What the fragment's `ExitCode:` line says. A PINNED code renders as the number, byte-identically to
// before `SpecExitCode` existed — every committed golden is a pinned case, so none of them moves. A case
// that pins only a stream renders `unpinned`, which is the honest word: writing `0` there would put a
// claim into the golden that the spec never made, and a reviewer diffing the fragment would read it as an
// assertion.
function fragmentExitCodeText(expected SpecExitCode) returns String
	return match expected 'pin'
		unpinned gives UnpinnedExitCodeLabel
		pinned(value) gives "{value}"
	end 'pin'
end 'fragmentExitCodeText'

// A compilerError test's fragment: the shared head plus the compiler's normalized
// stderr (the ACTUAL diagnostic the compiler produced — what the fragment exists
// to track — with every compiled file's path and line rewritten onto the fragment).
function buildErrorFragment(test SpecTest, compile ProcessCapture, staged StagedTest) returns String
	let normalizedError = normalizeCompilerOutput(compile.stderr, paths: staged.paths, stdlibRoot: staged.stdlibRoot).trim()
	return "{fragmentHeader(test)}{CompilerErrorLabel}\n{normalizedError}\n"
end 'buildErrorFragment'

// How far a test's verdict can be taken on the compiler's answer alone.
//
// ⭐ **THE SPLIT IS WHERE THE PROGRAM WOULD HAVE TO RUN, AND NOWHERE ELSE.** A `maxoncstderr` case
// asserts what the COMPILER said, so it is decided here in full and never joins a batch. A run
// expectation is decided here only when it cannot get as far as running — the compile failed, or a
// section the case pinned disagrees with the linked image — and otherwise says so, carrying the three
// pins its execution will be judged against.
union PreRunVerdict
	settled(outcome TestOutcome)
	needsExecution(expected SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout)
end 'PreRunVerdict'

function preRunVerdict(test SpecTest, specName String, compile ProcessCapture, staged StagedTest, exePath FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns PreRunVerdict
	return match test.expectation 'e'
		run(expected, stderr, stdout, sectionPins) gives runPreconditions(expected, stderr: stderr, stdout: stdout, sectionPins: sectionPins, compile: compile, exePath: exePath, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
		compilerError(text) gives PreRunVerdict.settled(checkCompilerError(text, specName: specName, testName: test.name, compile: compile, staged: staged))
	end 'e'
end 'preRunVerdict'

// A ```maxoncstderr test: the compile MUST fail, and its stderr must equal the expected text once
// BOTH have had the fragment's path replaced by the stable `<fragment>` token — the actual by its
// scratch path, the expected by the path the C# reference would have used for this same test.
//
// Normalizing BOTH sides is what lets one spec corpus feed two compilers. A /specs expectation
// names `specs/fragments/<spec>/<test>.test` because that is where the reference compiled it; an
// shv2-native one names `<fragment>` because that is what this runner has always printed. Neither
// is a claim about the language, so neither should decide whether a test passes. Everything that
// IS a claim — the code, the message, the line, the column — survives normalization untouched and
// is compared exactly.
function checkCompilerError(expectedText String, specName String, testName String, compile ProcessCapture, staged StagedTest) returns TestOutcome
	if compile.exitCode == 0 'unexpectedSuccess'
		return TestOutcome.fail("expected a compile error but compilation succeeded")
	end 'unexpectedSuccess'

	let actual = normalizeCompilerOutput(compile.stderr, paths: staged.paths, stdlibRoot: staged.stdlibRoot).trim()
	// The EXPECTED side needs no renumbering: an expectation — ported or authored — already names
	// the MERGED fragment's lines, because that is the file a reader has in front of them. Only its
	// PATH is rewritten, which is one mapping with no offset. It goes through the SAME library rewrite
	// for the reason this whole routine is one function: what "normalized" means may not differ by side,
	// and an expectation naming a host's absolute checkout is a mistake worth normalizing INTO agreement
	// rather than one worth passing through so it can fail somewhere else.
	let expected = normalizeCompilerOutput(expectedText, paths: referencePathMapping(specName, testName: testName), stdlibRoot: staged.stdlibRoot).trim()
	if actual == expected 'matched'
		return TestOutcome.pass
	end 'matched'

	return TestOutcome.fail("compiler-error mismatch\n    expected: {expected}\n    actual:   {actual}")
end 'checkCompilerError'

// The executable + argv pair one RUN goes through, whichever target produced the binary. The host's
// own binary runs with no argv; a wasm module goes behind wasmtime and its flags; an ELF goes behind
// `orb run`. One type so the single `runProcess` call in `spawnOneRun` is target-agnostic.
//
// `exe` is an `Executable`, not a `FilePath`, because the three runners are found in genuinely
// different ways and the stdlib already names that difference: a built binary and the VENDORED
// wasmtime are `.path` (an exact location this runner computed), while `orb` is a SYSTEM install
// located by PATH, which is `.name` — and the PATH search that backs it lives inside the spawn
// (`Subprocess.resolveByName`), not in `Executable.resolve()`.
type RunCommand
	export let exe as Executable
	export let argv as StringArray

	export static function create(exe Executable, argv StringArray) returns RunCommand
		return Self{exe: exe, argv: argv}
	end 'create'
end 'RunCommand'

// ⭐ THE RUNNER REGISTRY — the program that runs a binary this host cannot spawn itself.
//
// The HOST's own binary needs none of this: it is spawned directly (see `buildRunCommand`).
// Anything else needs a program that knows how to run it, and WHICH program is a property of the
// target's OS:
//   * `wasi`  — a `.wasm` COMPONENT, run under the vendored wasmtime.
//   * `linux` — a static ELF, run under WSL (from a Windows host) or an OrbStack Linux VM (from a
//               macOS host). WHICH one is a property of the HOST, not the target — see `externalRunnerFor`.
//
// ⚠ THIS UNION IS WHY THE TABLE IS WRITTEN ONCE. Two callers ask the OS the same question in
// different words — `buildRunCommand` turns the answer into a command, `requireRunnerAvailable`
// turns the SAME answer into a preflight probe — and they had each carried their own `match` over
// `Os`, with the two unreachable arms' panic text duplicated verbatim between them. Nothing made the
// copies agree: a runner added to one and not the other passes preflight and then panics per test,
// or probes for a runner no test will use. Routed through this union, adding an arm here makes BOTH
// matches fail to compile until they are taught about it — a drift that was silent becomes a build
// error.
union ExternalRunner
	wasmtime
	orbstack
	wsl
	// ⭐⭐ **THERE IS NO PROGRAM ON THIS MACHINE THAT CAN RUN THAT BINARY — AND THAT IS AN ANSWER, NOT AN
	// ABORT.** Every arm below used to be a `panic`, which answered the question this table is asked
	// ("which program spawns a Mach-O here?" — none) correctly, and was then used to answer a DIFFERENT
	// one one level up: "is any of this suite checkable here?". It is not the same question and the
	// answer is not the same either. A `--target=arm64-macos` run on Windows can COMPILE every case,
	// decide every `compilerError` case in full, and compare every emitted module against its committed
	// golden; only the executions are missing. Killing the run at the preflight threw all of that away —
	// and it threw it away for ~28 rungs, on the one lane that could have caught a wrong answer.
	//
	// `reason` is the sentence those panics carried, unchanged, now delivered per case (as
	// `TestOutcome.notRunHere`) and once in the trailer instead of once as a stack trace.
	unspawnable(reason String)
end 'ExternalRunner'

// Which external runner executes a binary built for `target`, or the statement that nothing here can.
// The one table, per the note above.
//
// EXHAUSTIVE over `Os`, with no bare `default`: a host that cannot run its own target's binaries is
// a contradiction, so the `windows`/`macos` arms are reachable only by cross-compiling to an OS this
// harness has no runner for. The `linux` arm is the one place `target.os` alone is NOT the answer: a
// Linux ELF runs under WSL from Windows and OrbStack from macOS, so it defers to the host
// (`linuxRunnerForHost`), which also needs the target to reject a CPU the host runner cannot execute.
function externalRunnerFor(target Target) returns ExternalRunner
	return match target.os 'runner'
		wasi gives ExternalRunner.wasmtime
		linux gives linuxRunnerForHost(target)
		windows gives ExternalRunner.unspawnable(noRunnerReason(target, container: "PE", onlyHostOs: "Windows"))
		macos gives ExternalRunner.unspawnable(noRunnerReason(target, container: "Mach-O", onlyHostOs: "macOS"))
	end 'runner'
end 'externalRunnerFor'

// The sentence a target with no runner here is reported by — one wording for every such arm, because
// the three of them differ only in WHICH container format and WHICH host could execute it, and three
// hand-written copies are three chances for the arm a reader hits to be the one phrased differently.
//
// It names the target and the host with `targetToString`, which is also the spelling `--target` takes,
// so the remedy in the sentence is typeable.
function noRunnerReason(target Target, container String, onlyHostOs String) returns String
	return "no runner for a {targetToString(target)} binary on a {targetToString(detectHostTarget())} host — a {container} can only be spawned on {onlyHostOs}, so only such a host can execute these cases"
end 'noRunnerReason'

// The runner for a Linux ELF, chosen by the HOST rather than the target: a Linux target is not one
// thing to run but two ways to run it — WSL on a Windows host, an OrbStack VM on a macOS host. A Linux
// host runs its own-CPU Linux binaries natively (`needsExternalRunner` is false there), and a
// cross-CPU Linux target from a Linux host has no runner in this harness. `wasi` is never a host OS
// (`detectHostTarget` returns only windows/macos/linux), so that arm is unreachable and says so.
function linuxRunnerForHost(target Target) returns ExternalRunner
	let host = detectHostTarget()
	return match host.os 'host'
		windows gives windowsLinuxRunner(target, host: host)
		macos gives ExternalRunner.orbstack
		linux gives ExternalRunner.unspawnable("a Linux ELF for a foreign CPU cannot be run from a Linux host by this harness (host {targetToString(host)}) — only a host whose CPU matches {targetToString(target)} can execute these cases")
		wasi panic("SpecTestRunner: 'wasi' is not a host OS — detectHostTarget never returns it, so this arm is unreachable")
	end 'host'
end 'linuxRunnerForHost'

// WSL runs only the HOST CPU's Linux binaries — there is no in-box binfmt/qemu cross-execution — so a
// foreign-CPU Linux target (e.g. `arm64-linux` on an x86-64 Windows host) has NO runner here. Saying so
// as a classification, rather than letting the WSL probe pass and then every test fail with the
// kernel's opaque "exec format error". The matching-CPU case (`x64-linux` on x64-windows) is the one
// that returns a runner. macOS's OrbStack is not gated this way — it runs arm64-linux natively and
// x64-linux under Rosetta — so only the WSL path carries this check.
function windowsLinuxRunner(target Target, host Target) returns ExternalRunner
	if target.cpu != host.cpu 'foreignCpu'
		return ExternalRunner.unspawnable("no runner for a {targetToString(target)} binary on a {targetToString(host)} host — WSL runs only the host CPU's Linux binaries (no in-box cross-CPU emulation), so only a matching host can execute these cases")
	end 'foreignCpu'
	return ExternalRunner.wsl
end 'windowsLinuxRunner'

// How to execute the binary the build just produced, with the arguments the case asked for.
//
// ⭐ THE PROGRAM'S OWN ARGUMENTS GO LAST, AND THAT IS ONE RULE FOR EVERY RUNNER. Each builder below
// ends its argv with THE THING TO BE EXECUTED — the module path for wasmtime, the resolved exe for
// `orb run` and for `wsl -e` — and each runner forwards whatever follows that to the program it
// launched (wasmtime's `-S`-ordering note is the same rule stated for its own flags). So appending
// once, here, is not a convenience over three copies of a push loop: it is the single place the rule
// is written, and a fourth runner inherits it by construction.
//
// The HOST's own binary needs no runner in front of it, so there is nothing to append after and its
// argv simply IS the program's arguments; everything else goes behind the runner `externalRunnerFor`
// names. Restored from v1 `SpecTestRunner.maxon:623` (`buildRunCommand`), which had only the first
// two arms.
//
// ⚠ The spawned process sees these starting at argv[1] — argv[0] is the program's own path, which
// `Subprocess` supplies from the executable and which no caller may occupy. That is what a real
// process sees, and what every `/specs` case reading `args.get(1)` was written against.
function buildRunCommand(modulePath FilePath, effectiveTarget Target, programArgs ProgramArgArray) returns RunCommand
	if not needsExternalRunner(effectiveTarget) 'hostSpawn'
		return RunCommand.create(Executable.path(modulePath), argv: programArgs)
	end 'hostSpawn'

	let launch = match externalRunnerFor(effectiveTarget) 'runner'
		wasmtime gives buildWasmRunCommand(modulePath)
		orbstack gives buildLinuxVmRunCommand(modulePath)
		// ⛔ NOT A GAP — the WSL lane does not execute one program per command, so there is no per-test
		// command to build for it. It runs a whole spec's programs from one generated script inside a
		// single entry (`runWslBatch`), and `runStrategyFor` is the ONE table that decides which lane
		// does which. This arm is what makes the two disagreeing a panic instead of a silent second
		// answer: reaching it means a lane was routed here that `runStrategyFor` had claimed to batch.
		wsl panic("SpecTestRunner: the {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} lane runs a whole spec in one {WslExecutableName} entry (see runWslBatch), so it has no per-test run command — `runStrategyFor` and `buildRunCommand` disagree about this target")
		// ⛔ NOT A GAP EITHER, and the same disagreement said the other way round: nothing on this host
		// can spawn such a binary, so `runStrategyFor` routes the whole lane to `unrunnableHere` and no
		// execution is ever attempted. Reaching here means a run WAS attempted for a target the table
		// had just said had no runner.
		unspawnable panic("SpecTestRunner: nothing on this host can execute a {targetToString(effectiveTarget)} binary, so there is no run command to build — `runStrategyFor` and `buildRunCommand` disagree about this target")
	end 'runner'

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.append(launch.argv)
	argv.append(programArgs)

	return RunCommand.create(launch.exe, argv: argv)
end 'buildRunCommand'

// `wasmtime run -S cli-exit-with-code=y <module.wasm>` — the exact command the wasm component
// skeleton was proven under (see the contract golden). No guest argv, no `--dir`/socket preopens:
// the scalar slice does no I/O. Ported from v1 `SpecTestRunner.maxon:650` (`buildWasmRunCommand`),
// trimmed to the one `-S` setting the scalar surface needs.
function buildWasmRunCommand(modulePath FilePath) returns RunCommand
	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(WasmtimeRunSubcommand)
	argv.push(WasmtimeSettingFlag)
	argv.push(WasmtimeExitWithCodeSetting)
	// The module path MUST come after every `-S`: anything after it is forwarded to the guest as argv.
	argv.push(modulePath.toString())

	return RunCommand.create(Executable.path(findWasmtimePath()), argv: argv)
end 'buildWasmRunCommand'

// Locate the vendored wasmtime through the SAME upward-walk + host-preferred-binary locator the
// component-wrapping step uses (`findFileUpward` + `hostWasmToolCandidates`, both exported from
// WasmComponent for exactly this reason). One search rule, not two: the spec runner and the compiler
// must agree on which `vendor/wasmtime/` binary is "the" wasmtime, and a divergent copy is how two
// callers end up disagreeing (v1 grew a second one — SpecTestRunner.maxon:588). PANICS when it is
// nowhere on the way to the filesystem root: the wasm run path cannot proceed without it, and there
// is no valid FilePath to return in its place (a "" sentinel is what this project forbids — which is
// why the shared locator's empty-FilePath not-found result is turned into a panic right here).
function findWasmtimePath() returns FilePath
	let found = findFileUpward(hostWasmToolCandidates(WasmtimeDirName))
	if found.isEmpty() 'notFound'
		panic("SpecTestRunner: wasmtime not found under {VendorDirName}/{WasmtimeDirName}/ (searched up from the cwd to the filesystem root) — the wasm run path needs the vendored binary")
	end 'notFound'
	return found
end 'findWasmtimePath'

// `orb run -m maxon-linux <exe>` — run a static aarch64 ELF inside the OrbStack Linux machine.
//
// ⚠ THE PATH IS MADE ABSOLUTE BEFORE IT CROSSES, AND THAT IS NOT DEFENSIVENESS. The VM mirrors the
// Mac's filesystem at IDENTICAL REAL paths, which is what lets it run a binary the build wrote
// moments ago with no copying — but "identical" is about the path the KERNEL sees, and the two sides
// do not agree on every spelling of one. macOS's `/tmp` is a symlink to `/private/tmp`; the VM's
// `/tmp` is its own separate, empty directory. Hand `orb` a relative path and it resolves against
// whatever cwd the VM inherited; hand it `/tmp/x` and it looks in a directory that is not the one
// the file is in. (Measured: `/private/tmp/probe` ran and exited 7, `/tmp/probe` failed 127.)
//
// Resolving against the cwd fixes the relative half outright. It cannot rewrite a symlinked prefix —
// the stdlib has no realpath — but it does not need to: `Directory.currentPath()` is `getcwd`, which
// is already the PHYSICAL path, and the runner's binaries live under the spec directory, which is
// itself derived from that cwd (`Main.resolveSpecDir`). The one way back into the trap is an
// explicit `spec-test /tmp/...` argument on a host whose `/tmp` is a symlink.
//
// ⚠ WHY `orb` IS NAMED AND NOT LOCATED. `findWasmtimePath` walks up to a VENDORED binary because
// there IS an in-repo copy and it is the one that must win. `orb` is a SYSTEM install with no
// canonical location, so the locator for it is PATH — and the PATH search that honours
// `Executable.name` lives inside the SPAWN (`Subprocess.resolveByName` → `subprocessResolveOnPath`),
// not in `Executable.resolve()`, which despite its doc comment only wraps the bare name. Naming it
// is therefore the way to get the lookup; pre-resolving it here would not.
//
// A missing `orb` is caught ONCE, up front, by `requireRunnerAvailable` — not here, per test.
function buildLinuxVmRunCommand(exePath FilePath) returns RunCommand
	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(OrbRunSubcommand)
	argv.push(OrbMachineFlag)
	argv.push(OrbMachineName)
	argv.push(exePath.resolve(Directory.currentPath()).toString())

	return RunCommand.create(Executable.name(OrbExecutableName), argv: argv)
end 'buildLinuxVmRunCommand'

// ============================================================================================
// THE RUN PASS — every execution one spec needs, performed the way this lane performs them best
// ============================================================================================

// What one execution produced, or why there is none. ONE per PREPARED test, including the tests that
// never needed an execution, so the verdict pass can index observations by the same position it indexes
// everything else by (see `runOneSpec`).
//
// `failed` is the run-step twin of what a spawn failure used to return inline: a reason, for that test
// and no other. It is what a batch must produce for every test it could not account for, because the
// pool's accounting is one record per test and an absence would be a missing record rather than a
// failure anyone could read.
export union RunObservation
	notRequired
	completed(capture ProcessCapture)
	failed(reason String)
	// ⭐ THE EXECUTION WAS REQUIRED AND THIS MACHINE CANNOT PERFORM IT — see `ExternalRunner.unspawnable`.
	// Distinct from `failed`, which says a launch was ATTEMPTED and did not work: that is a defect in
	// something (the binary, the runner, the OS) and must stay a FAIL. This one is a machine limit known
	// before anything was attempted, and it carries no reason for `TestOutcome.notRunHere`'s reason.
	notRunnableHere
end 'RunObservation'

typealias RunObservationArray = Array with RunObservation

// ⭐ HOW THIS LANE PERFORMS A SPEC'S EXECUTIONS. The ONE table, over the same `ExternalRunner` union the
// commands and the preflight are built from, so "which runner" and "one entry or many" cannot come to
// disagree — adding a runner makes this match fail to compile until it decides.
//
// ⚠ **ORBSTACK IS DELIBERATELY NOT BATCHED, AND THIS IS THE STATEMENT OF THAT.** The gain measured here
// is WSL's VM-ENTRY latency (137 ms per entry, on a Windows host); `orb`'s per-entry cost has never been
// measured, and it cannot be from this host. More decisive: the two lanes give a test binary DIFFERENT
// working directories today — WSL's `--cd` puts it in the spec's scratch directory while `orb run`
// leaves it in the cwd this harness spawns with (`temp/`, the `RunWorkingSubdirName` contract) — so a
// shared script would have to reproduce each lane's cwd exactly, and only one of the two can be
// verified from here. Batching a lane whose results cannot be diffed is how a speed change becomes a
// silent behaviour change.
export union RunStrategy
	individually
	wslBatch
	// ⭐ PERFORM NONE OF THEM. Not an absence of a strategy but a strategy: every test that needed its
	// program run is told so, in its own record, exactly as the two executing lanes tell theirs.
	noneHere
end 'RunStrategy'

// EXPORTED because the verdict self-test asks it of every supported target — that guard's whole subject
// is which lane this host routes each target to, and a second copy of the routing rule inside a test is
// a test that can agree with itself while disagreeing with the runner.
export function runStrategyFor(effectiveTarget Target) returns RunStrategy
	if not needsExternalRunner(effectiveTarget) 'hostSpawn'
		return RunStrategy.individually
	end 'hostSpawn'

	return match externalRunnerFor(effectiveTarget) 'runner'
		wasmtime gives RunStrategy.individually
		orbstack gives RunStrategy.individually
		wsl gives RunStrategy.wslBatch
		unspawnable gives RunStrategy.noneHere
	end 'runner'
end 'runStrategyFor'

function executeRuns(prepared PreparedTestArray, tempDir FilePath, runWorkingDir FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns RunObservationArray
	return match runStrategyFor(effectiveTarget) 'strategy'
		individually gives spawnEachRun(prepared, runWorkingDir: runWorkingDir, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
		wslBatch gives runWslBatch(prepared, tempDir: tempDir, runWorkingDir: runWorkingDir)
		noneHere gives unperformedRuns(prepared)
	end 'strategy'
end 'executeRuns'

// One observation per prepared test, saying of each that its execution did not happen here. The SAME
// walk the two executing lanes make — `finished` still answers `notRequired`, because a test whose
// verdict was settled at its compile is not waiting on anything and must not be reported as unrun.
function unperformedRuns(prepared PreparedTestArray) returns RunObservationArray
	var observations = RunObservationArray.create()
	for step in prepared 'eachStep'
		observations.push(match step 'phase'
			finished gives RunObservation.notRequired
			awaiting gives RunObservation.notRunnableHere
		end 'phase')
	end 'eachStep'
	return observations
end 'unperformedRuns'

// One spawn per test — the host's own binaries, and the two external runners that are not batched.
function spawnEachRun(prepared PreparedTestArray, runWorkingDir FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns RunObservationArray
	var observations = RunObservationArray.create()
	for step in prepared 'eachStep'
		observations.push(match step 'phase'
			finished gives RunObservation.notRequired
			awaiting(pending) gives spawnOneRun(pending, runWorkingDir: runWorkingDir, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
		end 'phase')
	end 'eachStep'
	return observations
end 'spawnEachRun'

function spawnOneRun(pending PendingRun, runWorkingDir FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns RunObservation
	// The native path spawns the exe directly; a CROSS target's `.wasm` component cannot be spawned by
	// the host, so it goes behind the vendored wasmtime instead — what is checked afterwards is
	// IDENTICAL either way.
	let runCommand = buildRunCommand(pending.compiled.exePath, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget, programArgs: pending.compiled.test.programArgs)
	// ⭐ A TEST BINARY'S WORKING DIRECTORY IS PART OF WHAT ITS SPEC ASSERTS — `directory.md` reaches
	// `../bin` through it — so it is stated rather than inherited from whoever launched the suite. See
	// `RunWorkingSubdirName`.
	let spawned = try runProcess(runCommand.exe, argv: runCommand.argv, workingDirectory: runWorkingDir) otherwise (e) 'runSpawnFailed'
		return RunObservation.failed("program spawn failed: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'runSpawnFailed'

	return RunObservation.completed(spawned)
end 'spawnOneRun'

// Whether the one `wsl` entry happened at all. A launch that never got off the ground is not one test's
// failure — every test the batch was carrying has to be told, in its own record, which is what keeps
// the pool's one-record-per-test accounting true.
union BatchLaunch
	completed(capture ProcessCapture)
	failed(reason String)
end 'BatchLaunch'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE `wsl` ENTRY FOR THE WHOLE SPEC.** See the batch constants block for the measurement that
// motivates it and for why the capture is files rather than a stream.
//
// The entry is `wsl --cd <spec scratch dir> -e /bin/sh .batch/run.sh`, which is the per-test command's
// own shape with the script where the binary used to be: `--cd` takes the directory as a RAW Windows
// path and WSL translates it, so nothing here maps a Windows path to a guest one and nothing here
// assumes an automount root. Every binary that spec built is IN that directory, so each execution
// inherits exactly the working directory the per-test `--cd` gave it.
function runWslBatch(prepared PreparedTestArray, tempDir FilePath, runWorkingDir FilePath) returns RunObservationArray
	let batchSubdir = batchSubdirPath()
	let batchDir = tempDir.join(batchSubdir)
	let launch = launchBatch(prepared, tempDir: tempDir, batchDir: batchDir, runWorkingDir: runWorkingDir)

	var observations = RunObservationArray.create()
	for step in prepared 'eachStep'
		observations.push(match step 'phase'
			finished gives RunObservation.notRequired
			awaiting(run) gives batchObservationFor(batchKeyFor(run.compiled), batchDir: batchDir, launch: launch)
		end 'phase')
	end 'eachStep'

	// The captures have been read into the observations, so what is on disk is spent. Removed for
	// `cleanup`'s reason and with the same best-effort rule: nothing downstream depends on it, but a
	// suite that leaves three files per test behind is a suite whose scratch tree grows without bound.
	purgeFilesIn(batchDir)

	return observations
end 'runWslBatch'

// How many of this spec's tests still need their program run — the batch's size, which is what its
// deadline is derived from and what decides whether there is anything to enter the guest FOR.
//
// ⚠ COUNTED off `prepared` rather than collected into an array of its own, and that is not merely
// taste: an `Array with PendingRun` obliges the bootstrap to synthesize `PendingRun.clone`, which
// obliges `CompiledTest.clone` — which it does not generate for a struct that is nobody's array element
// (`E9001 Function 'CompiledTest.clone' not found in module`). Reading the one array that already
// exists copies nothing and needs nothing synthesized.
function pendingRunCount(prepared PreparedTestArray) returns TestTally
	var pending = 0 as TestTally
	for step in prepared 'eachStep'
		match step 'phase'
			finished then continue
			awaiting then pending = pending + 1
		end 'phase'
	end 'eachStep'
	return pending
end 'pendingRunCount'

// Write the script and run it. Everything that can go wrong BEFORE the guest starts is one reason,
// carried to every test the batch was to have run.
function launchBatch(prepared PreparedTestArray, tempDir FilePath, batchDir FilePath, runWorkingDir FilePath) returns BatchLaunch
	let executions = pendingRunCount(prepared)
	if executions == 0 'nothingToRun'
		// A spec of nothing but `maxoncstderr` cases runs no program at all, and must not pay a 137 ms
		// entry to prove it. Every observation this launch is read for is `notRequired` anyway.
		return BatchLaunch.completed(emptyBatchCapture())
	end 'nothingToRun'

	if not Directory.create(batchDir) 'noBatchDir'
		return BatchLaunch.failed("could not create the batch capture directory {batchDir.toString()}")
	end 'noBatchDir'

	// ⭐ DELETED BEFORE, NOT ONLY AFTER — the same rule the metrics file is deleted under, for the same
	// reason: a run killed part-way through leaves a `.code` behind, and a test whose execution never
	// happened would then be read as having produced whatever the last run left there.
	for step in prepared 'eachStep'
		match step 'phase'
			finished then continue
			awaiting(run) then discardBatchCaptures(batchDir, key: batchKeyFor(run.compiled))
		end 'phase'
	end 'eachStep'

	let scriptPath = batchDir.join("{BatchScriptName}")
	try File.writeText(scriptPath, content: batchScript(prepared)) otherwise 'noScript'
		return BatchLaunch.failed("could not write the batch script {scriptPath.toString()}")
	end 'noScript'

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(WslCwdFlag)
	argv.push(tempDir.resolve(Directory.currentPath()).toString())
	argv.push(WslExecFlag)
	argv.push(GuestShell)
	argv.push("{BatchSubdirName}{PathSeparator}{BatchScriptName}")

	let batch = try runProcess(Executable.name(WslExecutableName), argv: argv, workingDirectory: runWorkingDir, timeoutMs: batchDeadlineMs(executions)) otherwise (e) 'batchSpawnFailed'
		return BatchLaunch.failed("the batched {WslExecutableName} run failed: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'batchSpawnFailed'

	// The script's own exit status is the last execution's and means nothing — every verdict is in the
	// files. It is carried, not judged, so that a test whose record is MISSING can quote it.
	return BatchLaunch.completed(batch)
end 'launchBatch'

// ⚠ **THE BATCH IS ALLOWED EXACTLY THE SUM OF WHAT ITS EXECUTIONS ARE ALLOWED**, plus one execution's
// worth for the shell around them. It is the same structural ceiling a spec had when each test was its
// own spawn — (tests) x `SpecSpawnTimeoutMs` — stated once instead of accumulated by accident.
//
// ⛔ **A TIGHTER NUMBER WOULD BE WORSE THAN NO NUMBER.** Nothing outside the guest can tell "a test is
// inside its own deadline" from "`wsl` has stopped responding", so a deadline short enough to catch the
// second kills a batch that is merely doing what the first one legitimately does — and every test after
// the one that was slow would then fail for a reason that is not about it. The inner `timeout` is what
// actually bounds a wedged PROGRAM, per test, at exactly the value one spawn used to be bounded by; this
// bounds only a `wsl` that never returns at all, which the pool's own wedge watchdog would report
// anyway.
function batchDeadlineMs(executions TestTally) returns DurationMs
	return SpecSpawnTimeoutMs * (executions + 1)
end 'batchDeadlineMs'

// What a batch that never had to run reports: no process, no output, no failure. Named because
// "nothing ran" is a real answer here and every field of it has to be the empty one.
function emptyBatchCapture() returns ProcessCapture
	return ProcessCapture.create(0, stdout: "", stderr: "")
end 'emptyBatchCapture'

// The capture subdirectory as a path component. A FilePath (not the String overload) for
// `specTempRootDir`'s reason: a bare String constant is ambiguous between `join`'s two overloads.
function batchSubdirPath() returns FilePath
	return try FilePath.from(BatchSubdirName) otherwise panic("SpecTestRunner: BatchSubdirName '{BatchSubdirName}' must be a valid relative path")
end 'batchSubdirPath'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE NAME THE GUEST IS GIVEN, DERIVED FROM THE PATH THE COMPILE ACTUALLY PRODUCED.** The
// script names the binary `./<this>`, and the pre-run discard, the script's redirections and the
// read-back all key their three capture files off the same word.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS `exePath`'s FILENAME AND NOT `test.name`, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** `prepareOneTest` is the
// one place that knows what the build wrote — `<name>` plus `executableExtension(effectiveTarget)` —
// and a script that spelled `./<test.name>` would be a SECOND answer to that question, agreeing with
// the first only for as long as this lane's extension stays EMPTY. Nothing would make them agree: the
// day a batched lane has a suffix, the script names a file that does not exist and the whole spec
// reports 127 with the guest shell's `not found` in each test's stderr. Asked of the path, there is
// only ever one answer.
function batchKeyFor(compiled CompiledTest) returns String
	return compiled.exePath.filename()
end 'batchKeyFor'

// One capture file, on the HOST side. The stem-plus-extension formula is written HERE and nowhere else
// because its other end is a shell script (`guestCapturePath`) that this process cannot type-check
// against: a reader that composed the name even slightly differently from the writer would not fail to
// build, it would report every test in the spec as having produced no record.
function batchCapturePath(batchDir FilePath, key String, extension String) returns FilePath
	return batchDir.join("{key}{extension}")
end 'batchCapturePath'

// One test's three capture files, removed. Best-effort: an absent file is the normal case both at the
// pre-run discard and at the post-run purge.
function discardBatchCaptures(batchDir FilePath, key String)
	try File.delete(batchCapturePath(batchDir, key: key, extension: BatchStdoutExtension)) otherwise ignore
	try File.delete(batchCapturePath(batchDir, key: key, extension: BatchStderrExtension)) otherwise ignore
	try File.delete(batchCapturePath(batchDir, key: key, extension: BatchStatusExtension)) otherwise ignore
end 'discardBatchCaptures'

// One test's observation, read back out of the files its execution wrote.
function batchObservationFor(key String, batchDir FilePath, launch BatchLaunch) returns RunObservation
	return match launch 'l'
		failed(reason) gives RunObservation.failed(reason)
		completed(capture) gives batchCaptureFor(key, batchDir: batchDir, batch: capture)
	end 'l'
end 'batchObservationFor'

function batchCaptureFor(key String, batchDir FilePath, batch ProcessCapture) returns RunObservation
	// THE STATUS FIRST, because it is the file that says the execution finished at all — the other two
	// exist from the moment the redirection is set up, so neither can answer that question.
	let status = try File.readText(batchCapturePath(batchDir, key: key, extension: BatchStatusExtension)) otherwise 'noStatus'
		return RunObservation.failed("the batched {WslExecutableName} run recorded no exit status for this test; the batch itself exited {batch.exitCode}{batchStderrNote(batch)}")
	end 'noStatus'

	let recorded = status.trim()
	if recorded == BatchTimedOutToken 'timedOut'
		return RunObservation.failed("the program did not finish within {SpecSpawnTimeoutSeconds}s and was killed inside the batch")
	end 'timedOut'

	// `parseCount` is the suite's ONE decimal parser (see ScaleTestRunner). Anything it refuses is a
	// disagreement between this reader and the script above it, which is a harness break rather than a
	// verdict about the program — so it FAILS this one test with the bytes it could not read.
	let exitCode = try parseCount(recorded) otherwise (e) 'unreadableStatus'
		return RunObservation.failed("the batched {WslExecutableName} run wrote an unreadable exit status '{recorded}': {scaleErrorText(e)}")
	end 'unreadableStatus'

	let stdout = try File.readText(batchCapturePath(batchDir, key: key, extension: BatchStdoutExtension)) otherwise 'noStdout'
		return RunObservation.failed("the batched {WslExecutableName} run recorded exit status {exitCode} but no stdout capture for this test")
	end 'noStdout'

	let stderr = try File.readText(batchCapturePath(batchDir, key: key, extension: BatchStderrExtension)) otherwise 'noStderr'
		return RunObservation.failed("the batched {WslExecutableName} run recorded exit status {exitCode} but no stderr capture for this test")
	end 'noStderr'

	return RunObservation.completed(ProcessCapture.create(exitCode as ExitCodeValue, stdout: stdout, stderr: stderr))
end 'batchCaptureFor'

// The batch process's own stderr, quoted under a missing record — the only place it is worth reading,
// because that is when something went wrong OUTSIDE any one test. Silent when there is nothing to
// quote, so the ordinary message does not end in a dangling colon.
function batchStderrNote(batch ProcessCapture) returns String
	let text = batch.stderr.trim()
	return "" if text.isEmpty() else " and said: {text}"
end 'batchStderrNote'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SCRIPT.** One shell function, then one call per test — the call carrying the test's name
// (which is its binary's name and the stem of its three capture files) and the case's own `Args:`.
//
// ⭐ **THE STATUS IS WRITTEN BY THE EXECUTION'S OWN SHELL, INSIDE THE PROCESS GROUP `timeout` SIGNALS.**
// That is the whole reason there is an inner shell at all: it is the only way to tell `timeout`'s exit
// 124 from a PROGRAM's exit 124 (see `BatchTimedOutToken`). If the program ran to completion its status
// is in the file, whatever it was; if it was killed, nothing wrote the file and the outer shell says so.
//
// ⭐ **THE PROGRAM IS `exec`ed INSIDE A SUBSHELL, AND BOTH HALVES OF THAT ARE LOAD-BEARING.** The
// subshell is what the redirections belong to, so the program's own stdout and stderr are the only
// things in the capture files; `exec` is what makes the subshell BECOME the program, so a program that
// dies by a signal is reported by the shell OUTSIDE those redirections — where the per-test path had
// nothing to report it at all. MEASURED, both ways: a SIGSEGV under `wsl -e ./crash` gives exit 139 and
// an empty stderr, and so does this (the shell's `Segmentation fault (core dumped)` goes to the batch's
// own stderr). Without `exec`, that sentence lands in the test's `.err` and a case pinning silence
// FAILS.
//
// ⭐ **THE FUNCTION BODY IS A SUBSHELL `( … )` RATHER THAN A BRACE BLOCK.** POSIX allows either, and
// `{`/`}` would have to be escaped in every Maxon string literal that spells this script.
function batchScript(prepared PreparedTestArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()

	out.append("{BatchRunFunctionName}() (\n")
	out.append("\t{BatchKeyShellName}=$1\n")
	out.append("\tshift\n")
	out.append("\tc=\"{guestCapturePath(BatchStatusExtension)}\"\n")
	out.append("\t{GuestTimeoutCommand} -k {BatchKillGraceSeconds}{GuestTimeoutSecondsSuffix} {SpecSpawnTimeoutSeconds}{GuestTimeoutSecondsSuffix} {GuestShell} {GuestShellCommandFlag} 'exe=$1; out=$2; err=$3; code=$4; shift 4; ( exec \"$exe\" \"$@\" > \"$out\" 2> \"$err\" ); printf %s $? > \"$code\"' _ \"{WslRelativePrefix}{batchKeyShellVar()}\" \"{guestCapturePath(BatchStdoutExtension)}\" \"{guestCapturePath(BatchStderrExtension)}\" \"$c\" \"$@\"\n")
	out.append("\t[ -f \"$c\" ] || printf %s {BatchTimedOutToken} > \"$c\"\n")
	out.append(")\n")

	for step in prepared 'eachStep'
		match step 'phase'
			finished then continue
			awaiting(run) then out.append(batchCallLine(run.compiled))
		end 'phase'
	end 'eachStep'

	return out.build()
end 'batchScript'

// One capture file, on the GUEST side: the same stem-plus-extension formula `batchCapturePath` composes
// for the reader, written relative to the `--cd` directory with the shell's own variable where the host
// puts the key.
//
// ⚠ `PathSeparator`, never a literal `/`: the guest is POSIX whatever the host is, and the argv that
// names the script five lines away already spells the separator from this same constant. Two spellings
// of one separator is how the writer and the reader come to disagree about a path neither compiler
// checks.
function guestCapturePath(extension String) returns String
	return "{BatchSubdirName}{PathSeparator}{batchKeyShellVar()}{extension}"
end 'guestCapturePath'

// One test's line: the function, the binary's own filename, then the case's own `Args:`.
//
// ⭐ THE PROGRAM'S OWN ARGUMENTS GO LAST, exactly as they do behind every other runner
// (`buildRunCommand`): the shell function forwards whatever follows the key straight to the binary, so
// `Args:` reaches the same argv[1..] a direct spawn would have given it.
function batchCallLine(compiled CompiledTest) returns String
	var line = StringBuilder.create()
	line.append(BatchRunFunctionName)
	line.append(" ")
	line.append(shQuoted(batchKeyFor(compiled)))

	for arg in compiled.test.programArgs 'eachArg'
		line.append(" ")
		line.append(shQuoted(arg))
	end 'eachArg'

	line.append("\n")
	return line.build()
end 'batchCallLine'

// One value, quoted so the shell reads it as exactly the bytes it was given. See the `ShQuote` block:
// single quotes make everything literal, so the only case is the quote itself.
function shQuoted(value String) returns String
	return "{ShQuote}{value.replace(ShQuote, with: "{ShQuote}{ShBackslash}{ShQuote}{ShQuote}")}{ShQuote}"
end 'shQuoted'

// ⭐ IS THE RUNNER THIS RUN NEEDS ACTUALLY INSTALLED? Asked ONCE, by the parent, before a single
// worker is spawned.
//
// A missing runner is not a property of any one test — it is a property of the RUN, and answering it
// per test answers it 780 times and reports it as 780 unrelated failures (or, in the pool, as a dead
// worker whose real message is buried in another process's stderr). Asked here, it costs one probe
// and it names the fix while the reader is still looking at the command they typed.
//
// The wasm arm's probe is the LOCATOR itself — finding the vendored binary is exactly the question,
// and `findWasmtimePath` already panics with its own fix when it cannot. The linux arm has to
// actually ASK, because it is checking two things PATH cannot answer on its own: that OrbStack is
// installed, and that the machine the run names exists and can execute something.
//
// ⛔ **A TARGET WITH NO RUNNER AT ALL IS NOT A REFUSAL HERE, AND THAT IS THIS RUNG.** This function
// used to carry a second job — classifying the target and PANICKING when nothing on this machine could
// spawn it — and that refusal killed a run in which every compile, every `compilerError` verdict and
// every golden comparison was perfectly checkable. `unspawnable` is now an ANSWER the run carries
// through to a per-case `TestOutcome.notRunHere` (see `runStrategyFor`), so there is nothing to probe
// and nothing to refuse: a runner that does not exist cannot be missing from PATH.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE WORKERS NO LONGER ASK.** They used to, with a `probeInstalled: false` parameter whose
// entire purpose was to reach this refusal from inside a worker — because a worker is a second door
// onto the golden writer and a `maxoncstderr` case reaches it without launching anything. That door is
// now guarded where the writing happens instead of by refusing the run: `recordFragmentAndCleanup` mints
// only for a verdict that was established in full on this host (`TestOutcome.pinsEmittedCode` and the
// `mayMint` argument beside it). The guard moved to the thing it guards, and the parameter went with it.
export function requireRunnerAvailable(effectiveTarget Target)
	if not needsExternalRunner(effectiveTarget) 'hostSpawn'
		return
	end 'hostSpawn'

	match externalRunnerFor(effectiveTarget) 'runner'
		wasmtime then _ = findWasmtimePath()
		orbstack then requireOrbMachineReachable(effectiveTarget)
		wsl then requireWslAvailable(effectiveTarget)
		unspawnable then break 'runner'
	end 'runner'
end 'requireRunnerAvailable'

// Probe the OrbStack machine by running the smallest possible command in it.
//
// It answers THREE questions in one spawn, and each has a different fix: is `orb` installed, does the
// machine this runner names exist, and can it execute a binary. A probe rather than a file-existence
// check because two of the three are not questions the filesystem can answer — and because a stopped
// machine is STARTED by this call, so the first real test does not pay that latency inside its own
// timing.
function requireOrbMachineReachable(effectiveTarget Target)
	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(OrbRunSubcommand)
	argv.push(OrbMachineFlag)
	argv.push(OrbMachineName)
	argv.push(OrbProbeCommand)

	// DERIVED from the target the caller is actually running, never spelled as a literal — the same
	// discipline the deleted `nativeTargetKeys` was built on. A message that tells the reader to drop
	// `--target=<x>` has to name the flag `parseBuildTarget` accepts, and `targetToString` is the one
	// producer of that spelling; a literal here would misname a cross-CPU orb target (x64-linux under
	// Rosetta) and could not be reached by a renaming.
	let orbTargetKey = targetToString(effectiveTarget)

	let probe = try runProcess(Executable.name(OrbExecutableName), argv: argv, workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'notInstalled'
		panic("SpecTestRunner: could not run '{OrbExecutableName}' ({e.displayReason()}) — running a {orbTargetKey} binary from a non-Linux host needs OrbStack (https://orbstack.dev); install it, or drop {TargetFlagPrefix}{orbTargetKey} and run the suite for this host")
	end 'notInstalled'

	if probe.exitCode != 0 'machineUnreachable'
		panic("SpecTestRunner: '{OrbExecutableName} {OrbRunSubcommand} {OrbMachineFlag} {OrbMachineName} {OrbProbeCommand}' failed (exit {probe.exitCode}): {probe.stderr.trim()} — the {orbTargetKey} run path needs an OrbStack Linux machine named '{OrbMachineName}' (`orb create ubuntu {OrbMachineName}`)")
	end 'machineUnreachable'
end 'requireOrbMachineReachable'

// Probe WSL by running the smallest command that proves the LANE, not merely the distro — see
// `wslProbeScript` for why that is `/bin/sh` and `timeout` rather than `/bin/true`. Like the orb probe
// it answers every question it can in ONE spawn, and each has a different fix: is `wsl` installed, is a
// distro reachable, can it execute `/bin/sh`, is coreutils there. The target key is DERIVED from a
// `Target`, never spelled literally — the same discipline the orb probe follows.
function requireWslAvailable(effectiveTarget Target)
	let wslTargetKey = targetToString(effectiveTarget)

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(WslExecFlag)
	argv.push(GuestShell)
	argv.push(GuestShellCommandFlag)
	argv.push(wslProbeScript())

	let probe = try runProcess(Executable.name(WslExecutableName), argv: argv, workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'notInstalled'
		panic("SpecTestRunner: could not run '{WslExecutableName}' ({e.displayReason()}) — running a {wslTargetKey} binary from a Windows host needs WSL2 (`wsl --install`); install it, or drop {TargetFlagPrefix}{wslTargetKey} and run the suite for this host")
	end 'notInstalled'

	if probe.exitCode != 0 'distroUnusable'
		panic("SpecTestRunner: '{WslExecutableName} {WslExecFlag} {GuestShell} {GuestShellCommandFlag} {wslProbeScript()}' failed (exit {probe.exitCode}): {probe.stderr.trim()} — the {wslTargetKey} run path needs a working WSL2 distribution (`wsl --install`) whose {GuestShell} and coreutils `{GuestTimeoutCommand}` are present, because a spec's binaries are run from a generated shell script that bounds each one with it")
	end 'distroUnusable'
end 'requireWslAvailable'

// A RUN test (whatever result blocks the case carried — an ```exitcode, a ```stdout, a ```stderr, a
// ```RequiredData, a ```RequiredRdata, in any combination): the compile MUST succeed and every section
// the case pinned must hold BEFORE the program is worth running at all.
function runPreconditions(expected SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout, sectionPins SpecSectionPinArray, compile ProcessCapture, exePath FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns PreRunVerdict
	if compile.exitCode != 0 'compileFailed'
		return PreRunVerdict.settled(TestOutcome.fail("compilation failed (exit {compile.exitCode}): {compile.stderr.trim()}"))
	end 'compileFailed'

	// The section gates read the BINARY, so they run before the program does — they are properties of
	// what was linked, not of what executing it happened to touch. A layout failure reported here
	// rather than after the run also names the actual defect: a program whose globals are laid out
	// wrong usually still exits with some number, and "exit code mismatch" would send the reader
	// looking in the wrong place entirely. They are NATIVE-binary properties; a wasm test never pins
	// one (the scalar slice has no data sections), so an empty pin list short-circuits this to a pass
	// under wasm.
	//
	// The EFFECTIVE target selects which executable format to parse — the file on disk is an ELF
	// under `--target=arm64-linux` however macOS-shaped the host is.
	// ⚠ `isPass()` rather than a `match` with a `fail` arm, here and at the three propagation points
	// below it. The four sites all ask ONE question — "did this half of the expectation hold, and if not
	// hand its verdict straight up" — and spelling it as a two-arm match made each of them a place a
	// third outcome had to be remembered. `isPass()` answers over the whole union in one place, so a
	// verdict this function never mints (`skipped`, which only `outcomeOrTargetSkip` can produce and only
	// from a FAILED compile) needs no arm at any of them.
	let sectionOutcome = checkRequiredSections(sectionPins, exePath: exePath, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
	if not sectionOutcome.isPass() 'sectionDisagreed'
		return PreRunVerdict.settled(sectionOutcome)
	end 'sectionDisagreed'

	return PreRunVerdict.needsExecution(expected, stderr: stderr, stdout: stdout)
end 'runPreconditions'

// The verdict a RUN expectation takes from the execution it asked for — whichever way this lane
// performed it (`executeRuns`).
//
// `notRequired` is unreachable and says so: the executors mint it for exactly the tests that FINISHED
// in the compile pass, and nothing but a `PendingRun` reaches here.
export function checkObservedRun(expected SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout, observation RunObservation, effectiveTarget Target, asyncTrace bool) returns TestOutcome
	return match observation 'observed'
		notRequired panic("checkObservedRun: a pending run was handed the 'no execution needed' observation — `executeRuns` answers one observation per PREPARED test and mints that arm only for a test that finished at its compile")
		failed(reason) gives TestOutcome.fail(reason)
		notRunnableHere gives TestOutcome.notRunHere
		completed(capture) gives runAssertions(expected, stderr: stderr, stdout: stdout, spawned: capture, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget, asyncTrace: asyncTrace)
	end 'observed'
end 'checkObservedRun'

// EVERY half the spec pinned, checked against that ONE execution.
function runAssertions(expected SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout, spawned ProcessCapture, effectiveTarget Target, asyncTrace bool) returns TestOutcome
	// What the PROGRAM said and how it ended, which under an external runner is not always what the
	// PROCESS said and how the process ended — see `observedRun`. Every check below reads this one
	// observation, so the three halves of a case's expectation cannot be compared against two
	// different accounts of the same execution.
	let run = observedRun(spawned, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)

	// An UNPINNED exit code is "this spec says nothing about it" — not "expect 0". A `/specs` case that
	// pins only stdout (the `init-from-literal` shape) is asserting what the program PRINTS, and holding it
	// to an exit code it never wrote would be this harness inventing an expectation. That is the one place
	// `SpecExitCode.unpinned` differs from `SpecStdout.unpinned`, which DOES assert silence — and the
	// difference is real: a program that prints nothing is a claim a spec can make by omission, while a
	// program that exits 0 is not.
	let exitOutcome = checkRunExitCode(expected, actual: run.exitCode)
	if not exitOutcome.isPass() 'exitDisagreed'
		return exitOutcome
	end 'exitDisagreed'

	// Both streams are checked against the ONE execution. stdout first (it is what a `print` test
	// exists to pin); on a pass, stderr is checked and its outcome returned.
	let stdoutOutcome = checkRunStdout(stdout, actual: run.stdout)
	if not stdoutOutcome.isPass() 'stdoutDisagreed'
		return stdoutOutcome
	end 'stdoutDisagreed'

	return checkRunStderr(stderr, actual: run.stderr, asyncTrace: asyncTrace)
end 'runAssertions'

// ⭐ **THE SECTION GATES — a linked binary's own bytes, for every section the case pinned.**
//
// ⚠ A ```RequiredData block was SILENTLY IGNORED before P1.0d.5b (see `SpecParser.RequiredDataFence`):
// six `specs/static-variables.md` cases carried one, the parser dropped it, and each would have
// passed on its exit code alone while claiming to check its layout. A gate that cannot see its own
// property must not report PASS.
//
// **IT READS THE IMAGE, NOT THE COMPILER'S OPINION OF IT**, and that is the whole value. The IR
// fragment already pins what the compiler THINKS it laid out (`printDataSection`), and pinning that
// twice would prove only that the compiler agrees with itself. Reading the section back out of the
// executable catches the layer between: a section header with a wrong RVA, a raw pointer off by an
// alignment, an image the writer padded instead of wrote. This is a faithful port of
// `TestRunner.cs`'s `CheckRequiredData` → `CheckRequiredSection`, including the PREFIX rule below.
//
// Every pin is checked, in the order the case wrote them, and the FIRST failure is reported: a case
// pinning both sections is making two claims about one link, and reporting the second while the
// first is wrong would name a consequence rather than a cause.
function checkRequiredSections(sectionPins SpecSectionPinArray, exePath FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns TestOutcome
	for pin in sectionPins 'eachPin'
		let outcome = compareLinkedSection(pin, exePath: exePath, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
		if not outcome.isPass() 'pinDisagreed'
			return outcome
		end 'pinDisagreed'
	end 'eachPin'

	return TestOutcome.pass
end 'checkRequiredSections'

function compareLinkedSection(pin SpecSectionPin, exePath FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns TestOutcome
	// The section's name in THIS target's format — `.rdata` in a PE, `.rodata` in an ELF — so every
	// message below names the section that was actually read rather than one spelling of it.
	let sectionName = linkedSectionName(pin.section, target: effectiveTarget)

	let expected = try parseTypedSectionValues(pin.text) otherwise (e) 'badBlock'
		return TestOutcome.fail("the {sectionName} pin is malformed: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'badBlock'

	let actual = try readLinkedSection(exePath, target: effectiveTarget, section: pin.section) otherwise (e) 'noSection'
		return TestOutcome.fail("could not read the {sectionName} section of {exePath.toString()}: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'noSection'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO PIN FORMS ARE COMPARED DIFFERENTLY, AND `SectionPinExpectation` CARRIES THE REASON**:
	// scalar/array lines describe fields of ONE payload (contiguous by construction, so their position is
	// part of the claim), while each `utf8` line names one separately-registered interned blob (whose
	// neighbours are an artifact of registration order, so its position is not).
	return match expected 'form'
		contiguousRun(bytes) gives compareSectionPrefix(bytes, actual: actual, sectionName: sectionName)
		locatedPayloads(payloads) gives locateSectionPayloads(payloads, actual: actual, sectionName: sectionName)
	end 'form'
end 'compareLinkedSection'

// ⚠ **A PREFIX FROM BYTE 0, NOT AN EQUALITY**, and the reference draws the line in the same place and
// says why: "the data section may contain additional runtime globals (e.g. green thread scheduler
// state) after the user data". shv2's `.data` holds only user globals TODAY, so a prefix check
// and an equality check agree on every current case — but the runtime that grows into this
// section (P1.5's scheduler) would break equality on every one of these six specs at once,
// for a change that says nothing about the property they pin.
//
// ⚠ **A PREFIX PINS THE FIRST PAYLOAD REGISTERED, WHICH IS A REAL CONSTRAINT ON THE PROGRAMS THAT MAY USE
// THIS FORM.** `.rdata` is a concatenation in REGISTRATION order (`GlobalDataTable.appendRdataPayload`), so
// a second program literal written ABOVE the pinned one displaces it and the message points at the pinned
// blob rather than at the literal that moved it. Two things hold it in place today, and only the first is
// this file's: `LowerMaxonToStd.registerProgramLiteralBlobs` registers every blob the SOURCE names ahead of
// everything the COMPILER composes (panic messages, String records, layout descriptors, witness tables,
// float islands), which is v1's own guarantee; and each pinning case is written with one program literal.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THIS BLOCK USED TO PRESCRIBE A LABEL-ANCHORED READ. THAT ROUTE WAS MEASURED AND IT IS NOT THE
// ANSWER — do not spend a day on it.** Two findings killed it:
//   • **it cannot express what the failing case needed.** `codegen-internals`'
//     `managed-string-reassignment-handles-old-value` pins TWO blobs that shv2 places at 0 and 80 with a
//     48-byte String record and 3 bytes of padding between them. An anchor names one offset; the pin needed
//     two payloads found independently, which is a different mechanism.
//   • **segregating `.rdata` into a blob region and a record region — the other candidate — BREAKS
//     `rdata-section`.** Measured: that spec's own image is its `__layout_Sizer_bool` descriptor (72 bytes)
//     followed by a range-check panic BLOB (80). Blob-region-first puts the panic text at offset 0 and the
//     descriptor at 80, so its committed pin would have had to change its expected bytes.
// ⇒ **What was adopted instead** (user ruling) is the per-form split above: `utf8` lines are LOCATED
// independently and in order; scalar and array lines keep this prefix rule exactly. `specs-shv2/
// rdata-section.md` states the rule and carries the case that defends it.
function compareSectionPrefix(expected ByteArray, actual ByteArray, sectionName String) returns TestOutcome
	if actual.count() < expected.count() 'tooShort'
		return TestOutcome.fail("{sectionName} is shorter than the pin claims\n    expected ({expected.count()} bytes): {formatHexBytes(expected)}\n    actual   ({actual.count()} bytes): {formatHexBytes(actual)}")
	end 'tooShort'

	for i in 0 upto expected.count() 'eachByte'
		let e = try expected.get(i) otherwise panic("compareSectionPrefix: expected.get OOB at {i} despite the upto bound")
		let a = try actual.get(i) otherwise panic("compareSectionPrefix: actual.get OOB at {i} — the length was checked above")
		if e != a 'differs'
			return TestOutcome.fail("{sectionName} mismatch at byte {i}: expected 0x{hexByte(e)}, got 0x{hexByte(a)}\n    expected ({expected.count()} bytes): {formatHexBytes(expected)}\n    actual   ({actual.count()} bytes): {formatHexBytes(actual)}")
		end 'differs'
	end 'eachByte'

	return TestOutcome.pass
end 'compareSectionPrefix'

// ⭐⭐ **EACH `utf8` PAYLOAD FOUND ON ITS OWN, IN ORDER — the form that pins CONTENT and SEQUENCE without
// pinning adjacency.** A blob's neighbours are decided by what else registered around it, which is a fact
// about the rest of the program rather than about the string being pinned; its BYTES and its ORDER relative
// to the block's other blobs are the claims a spec can honestly make, and they are exactly what this checks.
//
// The search for payload `n` starts at the END of payload `n-1`'s match, so the pin's order is a real
// constraint: two blobs written in one order do not pass if the image places them in the other. It is
// deliberately not an "anywhere" containment — that would let a re-ordered image pass unnoticed.
function locateSectionPayloads(payloads ByteArrayArray, actual ByteArray, sectionName String) returns TestOutcome
	var searchFrom = 0 as ByteOffset
	for (iter, payload) in payloads.withIterator() 'eachPayload'
		match findByteRun(actual, needle: payload, from: searchFrom) 'located'
			found(offset) then searchFrom = offset + payload.count()
			absent then return TestOutcome.fail("{sectionName} does not hold payload {iter.index() + 1} of {payloads.count()} at or after byte {searchFrom}\n    expected ({payload.count()} bytes): {formatHexBytes(payload)}\n    actual   ({actual.count()} bytes): {formatHexBytes(actual)}")
		end 'located'
	end 'eachPayload'

	return TestOutcome.pass
end 'locateSectionPayloads'

// The stderr half of a run expectation.
//
// The `unpinned` arm is NOT "ignore stderr" — it asserts the program printed NOTHING, which
// is what a spec that declines to mention stderr is saying about a program that should have
// no diagnostic to print. The C# reference draws the line in exactly this place
// (`TestRunner.cs:920` — "Unexpected stderr output" when `Stderr == null` and the stream is
// non-blank), and the alternative is worse than lax: a panic regression in any of the specs
// that pin only an exit code would print its diagnostic into a stream nothing reads, and the
// suite would stay green while the compiler started faulting.
function checkRunStderr(stderr SpecStderr, actual String, asyncTrace bool) returns TestOutcome
	let cleaned = normalizeAsyncTraceStderr(stripFaultRipSuffix(actual), asyncTrace: asyncTrace).trim()
	return match stderr 'pin'
		unpinned gives checkStreamSilent(cleaned, streamName: StderrStreamName)
		pinned(text) gives checkStreamMatches(normalizeAsyncTraceStderr(text, asyncTrace: asyncTrace).trim(), actual: cleaned, streamName: StderrStreamName)
	end 'pin'
end 'checkRunStderr'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ASYNC-TRACE NORMALIZER — APPLIED TO BOTH SIDES, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT.** A green-thread trace
// carries two kinds of line: the DETERMINISTIC ones a single-threaded scheduler always produces in the same
// order (`spawn #`, `io_yield #`, `io_resume #`, `await #`), and the WORKER-LIFECYCLE ones
// (`worker_start`/`worker_park`/`worker_wake`/`worker_exit`) whose count and position depend on how many OS
// threads the scheduler happened to run and when they woke. Comparing the second kind byte-for-byte would pin
// a timing accident. So both the expected text and the actual output are reduced to their deterministic subset
// before comparison — the EXPECTED side too, because canonical `.md` files carry the worker lines (the
// reference emits them) and a spec is not rewritten to match whichever compiler reads it.
//
// The ` [M=N]` suffix names the OS thread a line came from and is dropped for the same reason.
//
// ⚠ shv2 emits neither today: it is single-M, so it has no worker lifecycle to report and never tags a line
// with an M. The normalizer still has to run, because the SPEC text it is compared against has both.
function normalizeAsyncTraceStderr(text String, asyncTrace bool) returns String
	if not asyncTrace 'notATraceCase'
		return text
	end 'notATraceCase'

	var out = LineJoiner.create()
	for line in text.split(LineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		let stripped = stripWorkerMarkerFromLine(line)
		if isAsyncWorkerLine(stripped) 'timingDependent'
			continue
		end 'timingDependent'
		out.push(stripped)
	end 'eachLine'
	return out.build()
end 'normalizeAsyncTraceStderr'

// One line, cut at the ` [M=` worker tag. Unchanged when it carries none.
function stripWorkerMarkerFromLine(line String) returns String
	let cut = try line.findFirst(AsyncTraceWorkerMarker) otherwise return line
	return line.slice(line.startIndex(), endIndex: cut)
end 'stripWorkerMarkerFromLine'

// Is this one of the worker-lifecycle lines? Tested on the line's FIRST TOKEN rather than by `startsWith`, so
// a line whose payload merely contains one of these words is never mistaken for one.
function isAsyncWorkerLine(line String) returns bool
	let token = firstToken(line)
	return token == WorkerStartTag or token == WorkerParkTag or token == WorkerWakeTag or token == WorkerExitTag
end 'isAsyncWorkerLine'

// The first whitespace-delimited token of a line — the whole line when it has no space.
function firstToken(line String) returns String
	let space = try line.findFirst(TokenSeparator) otherwise return line
	return line.slice(line.startIndex(), endIndex: space)
end 'firstToken'

// The stdout half of a run expectation (P1.2 wave A — `print`) — `checkRunStderr`'s exact twin, minus
// the fault-rip stripping (that tail is a stderr panic artifact; stdout carries none), so it shares
// the same `checkStreamSilent`/`checkStreamMatches` rather than a second copy of them.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH SIDES ARE `.trim()`ED, which is what makes the ```stdout newline handling correct.** A
// program that `print`s `hello` writes `hello` with NO trailing newline; the ```stdout block's content
// is the single line `hello` (a fence strips its own trailing newline). Trimming both compares `hello`
// to `hello`. A program that `print`s `1\n` writes `1` then a newline; the block content is the line
// `1`; trimming both compares `1` to `1`. So a `print("x")` and a `print("x\n")` case both pass their
// block, and the newline the source chose is not something the golden has to spell — the SAME rule
// `checkRunStderr` already applies to a panic message.
//
// The `unpinned` arm is NOT "ignore stdout": it asserts the program printed NOTHING, which is what a
// non-`print` program does. Every pre-P1.2 program printed nothing to stdout, so this holds the whole
// suite to it — an accidental stray write becomes a FAILURE rather than an invisible one.
// The exit-code half of a run expectation. `unpinned` passes without looking — see `runAssertions`'s note on
// why that is not the same rule the stdout half follows.
function checkRunExitCode(expected SpecExitCode, actual ExitCodeValue) returns TestOutcome
	return match expected 'pin'
		unpinned gives TestOutcome.pass
		pinned(value) gives exitCodeOutcome(value, actual: actual)
	end 'pin'
end 'checkRunExitCode'

// The verdict for a case that DID pin an exit code — the arm body, split out because a `match` arm takes
// an expression, which is the same shape `checkRunStdout` uses for its two stream checkers.
function exitCodeOutcome(expected ExitCodeValue, actual ExitCodeValue) returns TestOutcome
	if actual != expected 'exitMismatch'
		return TestOutcome.fail("exit code mismatch: expected {expected}, got {actual}")
	end 'exitMismatch'
	return TestOutcome.pass
end 'exitCodeOutcome'

function checkRunStdout(stdout SpecStdout, actual String) returns TestOutcome
	let cleaned = actual.trim()
	return match stdout 'pin'
		unpinned gives checkStreamSilent(cleaned, streamName: StdoutStreamName)
		pinned(text) gives checkStreamMatches(text.trim(), actual: cleaned, streamName: StdoutStreamName)
	end 'pin'
end 'checkRunStdout'

// The two output streams a run expectation can pin — named so the shared stream checkers below phrase
// their diagnostic for the right one without either checker knowing which stream it serves.
let StderrStreamName = "stderr"
let StdoutStreamName = "stdout"

// A program whose spec pins no `streamName` output must print none. Shared by stderr and stdout: the
// rule ("declining to mention a stream asserts it is empty") is identical, and only the stream's NAME
// in the message differs.
function checkStreamSilent(actual String, streamName String) returns TestOutcome
	if actual.isEmpty() 'silent'
		return TestOutcome.pass
	end 'silent'
	return TestOutcome.fail("unexpected {streamName} output:\n{actual}")
end 'checkStreamSilent'

// A program whose spec pins `streamName` output must print exactly it. Shared for the same reason as
// `checkStreamSilent`.
function checkStreamMatches(expected String, actual String, streamName String) returns TestOutcome
	if actual == expected 'matched'
		return TestOutcome.pass
	end 'matched'
	return TestOutcome.fail("{streamName} mismatch\n    expected: {expected}\n    actual:   {actual}")
end 'checkStreamMatches'

// Drop the ` at rip=0xHEX diag_base=0xHEX` tail a CPU-fault panic line carries.
//
// The tail is the whole point of the tag and the whole reason it cannot be compared: it names
// the faulting instruction and the image's load address, which is what lets an operator
// recover the ASLR slide and resolve the fault against `llvm-objdump -d` — and which is
// therefore different on every run of every build. Everything a spec DOES claim sits to the
// left of it. Mirrors the C# reference's `StripFaultRipSuffix` (`TestRunner.cs:1427`), which
// is why the corpus's ```stderr blocks are portable byte-for-byte.
//
// A no-op on a line without the marker, so it costs nothing on the 279 tests that never fault.
function stripFaultRipSuffix(text String) returns String
	if not text.contains(FaultRipSuffixMarker) 'noFault'
		return text
	end 'noFault'

	var out = LineJoiner.create()
	for line in text.split(LineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		out.push(stripFaultRipFromLine(line))
	end 'eachLine'
	return out.build()
end 'stripFaultRipSuffix'

// One line, cut at the fault tag. Unchanged when it carries none — only the panic LINE does,
// so every other line of a stack trace passes through whole.
function stripFaultRipFromLine(line String) returns String
	let cut = try line.findFirst(FaultRipSuffixMarker) otherwise return line
	return line.slice(line.startIndex(), endIndex: cut)
end 'stripFaultRipFromLine'

// What the program said and how it ended — which on every target but one is simply what the process
// said and how the process ended.
//
// wasm32-wasi is the exception because the program does not get to end: a Maxon panic there is a wasm
// TRAP (see `StdToWasm.appendPanicRuntime` for why it must be), and a trap is the engine refusing to
// continue, so wasmtime — not the guest — chooses both the final status and the text that describes
// the failure. This is the one place that knows it is looking at a wasmtime process rather than at a
// Maxon program, so it is the place that maps the one back onto the other.
//
// EXHAUSTIVE over `Os` with no bare `default`: a target that grows a host wrapper of its own must
// decide here whether that wrapper edits the program's report, rather than inheriting an answer.
function observedRun(run ProcessCapture, effectiveTarget Target) returns ProcessCapture
	return match effectiveTarget.os 'os'
		wasi gives wasmPanicReport(run)
		windows to macos gives run
	end 'os'
end 'observedRun'

// ⭐⭐ **THE wasm PANIC REPORT: PUT BACK WHAT THE ENGINE TOOK AWAY, AND NOTHING ELSE.**
//
// ⚠ **PART OF A PINNED `stderr` ON THIS TARGET IS THEREFORE PRODUCED HERE AND NOT BY THE PROGRAM, AND
// THAT DESERVES THE SCRUTINY IT LOOKS LIKE IT NEEDS.** What is real, and what this only re-spells:
//
//   * **The MESSAGE is the program's, byte for byte.** `mrt_panic` wrote it to stderr before trapping;
//     it is copied out of the stream verbatim and never edited.
//   * **The FRAMES are wasmtime's, and they are the true call stack.** The engine unwound it and named
//     each function from the module's own "name" section. This reformats `0: 0x329 - maxon!fail` into
//     `  in fail` — the SAME words, in `BacktraceFormat`'s layout, using `BacktraceFormat`'s own
//     constants — because the corpus pins one trace format and the host spells it differently.
//   * **The EXIT CODE is `PanicExitCode` because the program never got to spend it.** Every backend's
//     panic path ends the process with that number; on this one the trap pre-empts the exit, and 134
//     is wasmtime's status for "the guest trapped", not an answer the program gave.
//
// ⭐ **EXACTLY ONE FRAME IS DROPPED — FRAME 0 — AND ONLY WHEN IT IS `mrt_panic`.** That is not an
// elision LIST and deliberately not one: it is the same rule the native runtimes implement in the
// runtime itself. x64's walk seeds from `mrt_panic`'s OWN frame and starts printing at `[rbp+8]`, so
// *"`mrt_panic` never appears in the trace"* (`X64Runtime.maxon:960-968`); arm64's does the same. The
// panic runtime is not part of the program's call stack in any target's report, so removing it here
// removes a frame **no other target shows either** — which is the whole test a normalization has to
// pass. Nothing else is touched: `main` and `mrt_start` are printed exactly as wasmtime reported them,
// and if a frame ever appeared that the native lanes do not show, it would appear in the output and
// FAIL, which is what it should do.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS FRAME 0 THAT DECIDES WHETHER THIS IS A PANIC AT ALL.** A trap whose innermost frame is
// not `mrt_panic` is not a Maxon panic — a `memory.grow` OOM, a `call_indirect` type mismatch, an
// `unreachable` this backend emits for a rejected op — and it passes through UNCHANGED, keeping
// wasmtime's own text and wasmtime's own 134. So this cannot launder an unrelated trap into a clean
// `exitcode 1`, which a message-shaped or "did it write anything to stderr" test would have let it do.
function wasmPanicReport(run ProcessCapture) returns ProcessCapture
	let markerIdx = try run.stderr.findLast(WasmtimeTrapReportMarker) otherwise return run
	let frames = wasmtimeBacktraceFrames(run.stderr.slice(markerIdx, endIndex: run.stderr.endIndex()))

	let innermost = try frames.get(WasmPanicRuntimeFrame) otherwise return run
	if innermost != String.from(PanicName) 'notAMaxonPanic'
		return run
	end 'notAMaxonPanic'

	// ⚠ **THE MARKER'S LEADING NEWLINE IS THE GUEST'S, NOT wasmtime's** — it is the
	// `PanicMessageLineTerminator` the panic line ends with (wasmtime's report starts flush at
	// `Error:`, measured on a guest that trapped having printed nothing). So the program's region ends
	// AFTER it; cutting at the marker instead swallows the message's own newline and runs
	// `Stack trace:` onto the end of the message.
	let programEnd = try run.stderr.indexAfter(markerIdx) otherwise panic("wasmPanicReport: no index after the trap marker at the very end of the stream, though the marker's own text follows it")

	// Reassembled from `BacktraceFormat`'s own constants, in the order `mrt_panic` writes them on a
	// target where it can: the message, the header, then one prefixed and terminated line per frame.
	var report = StringBuilder.create()
	report.append(run.stderr.slice(run.stderr.startIndex(), endIndex: programEnd))
	report.append(String.from(BacktraceHeader))

	// Frames PRINTED are capped exactly as the native walkers cap theirs (`MaxBacktraceFrames`) — one
	// diagnostic format means one length rule too. The cap counts printed frames, so it is offset by
	// the one runtime frame dropped ahead of them.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT NEVER FIRES TODAY, AND WHAT ACTUALLY TRUNCATES A DEEP wasm TRACE IS THE HOST — SILENTLY.**
	// MEASURED on wasmtime 44.0.0 with a 41-deep panic: it reports exactly **20** frames, emits no
	// "frames omitted" note, and offers no CLI flag or environment variable to raise it (`--help`,
	// `WASMTIME_BACKTRACE_DETAILS`, `RUST_BACKTRACE=full` — all 20). 20 is below `MaxBacktraceFrames`
	// (32), so this cap is unreachable while that holds. ⇒ **A panic more than ~19 program frames deep
	// prints a SHORTER trace on wasm than on the native lanes.** That is a real cross-target divergence,
	// it is the ENGINE's and not this harness's, and no case in the corpus is nearly deep enough to meet
	// it. The cap stays because the LENGTH RULE is ours to state and the host's limit is not a promise:
	// if a later wasmtime reports more, this keeps the two lanes agreeing without anyone noticing it had
	// to be re-decided.
	var lastFrame = frames.count()
	if lastFrame > MaxBacktraceFrames + WasmFirstProgramFrame 'capped'
		lastFrame = MaxBacktraceFrames + WasmFirstProgramFrame
	end 'capped'

	for i in WasmFirstProgramFrame upto lastFrame 'eachFrame'
		let frame = try frames.get(i) otherwise panic("wasmPanicReport: frames.get OOB at {i}, inside a loop bounded by the array's own count")
		report.append(String.from(FrameLinePrefix))
		report.append(frame)
		report.append(String.from(FrameLineTerminator))
	end 'eachFrame'

	return ProcessCapture.create(PanicExitCode as ExitCodeValue, stdout: run.stdout, stderr: report.build())
end 'wasmPanicReport'

// The innermost frame of a Maxon panic's trap — `mrt_panic` itself — and therefore the first frame
// that belongs to the PROGRAM. Named because both numbers are the same fact read two ways, and the
// cap arithmetic above depends on their relationship rather than on either value.
let WasmPanicRuntimeFrame = 0
let WasmFirstProgramFrame = 1

// The function names in wasmtime's backtrace region, innermost first. A line that is not a frame line
// contributes nothing — the region also carries wasmtime's `Caused by:` scaffolding and its closing
// `wasm trap:` line, neither of which names a function.
function wasmtimeBacktraceFrames(backtraceRegion String) returns StringArray
	var frames = StringArray.create()

	for line in backtraceRegion.split(LineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		let name = try wasmtimeFrameName(line) otherwise 'notAFrameLine'
			continue
		end 'notAFrameLine'
		frames.push(name)
	end 'eachLine'

	return frames
end 'wasmtimeBacktraceFrames'

// The function name a single frame line carries: the text after the `module!function` separator,
// which is itself required to sit after the address separator (see the constants for why both).
// Throws for any line that is not a frame line, which is what lets the caller skip it.
function wasmtimeFrameName(line String) returns String throws StringError
	let addrIdx = try line.findFirst(WasmtimeFrameAddrSeparator)
	let afterAddr = line.slice(addrIdx, endIndex: line.endIndex())
	let bangIdx = try afterAddr.findFirst(WasmtimeFrameModuleSeparator)
	return afterAddr.slice(try afterAddr.indexAfter(bangIdx), endIndex: afterAddr.endIndex()).trim()
end 'wasmtimeFrameName'

// Write the compiled fragment to `fragmentPath` — `fragmentSource` and nothing else, so the bytes
// on disk are the bytes the golden shows (see `fragmentSource`).
function writeFragment(fragmentPath FilePath, test SpecTest) returns bool
	try File.writeText(fragmentPath, content: fragmentSource(test)) otherwise return false
	return true
end 'writeFragment'

// THE ONE REWRITE, and both sides of a compilerError comparison go through it: every path a
// diagnostic names for THIS test becomes the `<fragment>` token, and — for a multi-file test — its
// line number becomes the MERGED fragment's.
//
// ONE routine because the two sides must mean the SAME thing by "normalized". Written twice — the
// obvious shape, since the two callers hold different paths — the copies would be free to drift on
// the token, on the renumbering, or on what else gets stripped, and a drift there does not read as
// a bug at either site: it reads as a test that mysteriously stopped matching.
//
// The MULTI-FILE half is what makes a ported `/specs` expectation work at all. The compiler
// compiled `…/app/main.maxon` and reported `main.maxon:2:9`; the expectation in the corpus says
// `specs/fragments/export-keyword/error.non-exported-function-cross-file.test:11:9`, because that
// is where the line sits in the merged block a reader is looking at. The C# reference does exactly
// this translation (`TestRunner.RewriteMultiFileErrorPaths`), and a spec expectation is not a claim
// about where a temp file lived, so neither side of it should decide a test.
function normalizeCompilerOutput(text String, paths FragmentPathMappingArray, stdlibRoot String) returns String
	var out = text
	for mapping in paths 'eachPath'
		out = rewriteDiagnosticPaths(out, path: mapping.compiledPath, lineOffset: mapping.lineOffset, token: mapping.replacementToken)
	end 'eachPath'

	// LAST, and the order is load-bearing: a `// --- stdlib-overlay:` section's file lies UNDER the very
	// root this rewrites and is also a staged source, so it has to become `<fragment>` above — with the
	// renumbering its section is owed — before anything general about the library is applied to it.
	return rewriteStdlibPaths(out, stdlibRoot: stdlibRoot)
end 'normalizeCompilerOutput'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SECOND FILE A DIAGNOSTIC CAN NAME, AND UNTIL ARRH NOTHING COULD PIN IT.** A refusal raised
// inside a LIBRARY body — `stdlib/Array.maxon:145:32`, `Array.clone`'s own `managed.slice(0, len)` — is as
// much a claim about the language as one raised in the program, and it is the shape EVERY retirement from
// the `Array`/`String` surface rosters produces: the refusal stops being the parser's and becomes the
// stdlib body's. But the compiler locates its library by walking up from its own EXECUTABLE
// (`StdlibSource.locateStdlibDir`), so what it prints is `C:\Users\…\<whoever's checkout>\stdlib\Array.maxon`
// and a `maxoncstderr` block carrying that string passes on exactly one machine. Rewritten to the
// REPO-RELATIVE form the checkout's own layout already spells, the file and the line — the two things such
// a case actually asserts — survive, and the host drops out.
//
// ⚠ **THE C# REFERENCE NEEDS NO COUNTERPART TO THIS, AND ITS ABSENCE THERE IS NOT A SECOND SPELLING.** The
// bootstrap's compiler renders EVERY diagnostic path relative to the project root with forward slashes
// before printing it (`CompileError.Format`), so it EMITS this exact form rather than normalizing one —
// MEASURED (ARRH): a broken `stdlib/Sleep.maxon` under a copied checkout reports `stdlib/Sleep.maxon:1:35`.
// The two runners therefore agree on ONE spelling for a stdlib expectation, reached from two ends; a
// rewrite added to the reference could never fire and would be the second copy of the rule.
//
// ⚠ **A LINE PINNED HERE IS THE LIBRARY'S, and an expectation naming one moves when `stdlib/` gains a line
// above it.** That is a real cost, deliberately accepted: it is the same one the four `/specs` cases
// pinning `Array.maxon:413`'s runtime panic already pay, and the alternative — normalizing the number away
// — would delete the half of the assertion that says WHICH construct in the library refused.
function rewriteStdlibPaths(text String, stdlibRoot String) returns String
	if not text.contains(stdlibRoot) 'namesNoLibraryFile'
		return text
	end 'namesNoLibraryFile'

	let pieces = text.split(stdlibRoot)
	var out = try pieces.get(0) otherwise panic("rewriteStdlibPaths: pieces.get(0) failed on a {pieces.count()}-part split of a text that contains '{stdlibRoot}'")
	for i in 1 upto pieces.count() 'eachOccurrence'
		let tail = try pieces.get(i) otherwise panic("rewriteStdlibPaths: pieces.get({i}) failed on a {pieces.count()}-part split")
		out = "{out}{StdlibDirName}{portableLibraryTail(tail)}"
	end 'eachOccurrence'
	return out
end 'rewriteStdlibPaths'

// What follows a library root, with the PATH part put in the corpus's own forward-slash spelling and
// everything from `:<line>:<col>` on left exactly as the compiler wrote it.
//
// ⭐ **THE CUT IS THE FIRST `:`, AND ON THIS SIDE OF THE ROOT THAT IS UNAMBIGUOUS.** A Windows drive
// letter's colon sits inside the root, which has already been consumed, and no path segment may contain
// one — so the first colon after the root is always the one that ends the file name. Rewriting the
// separators matters as much as the prefix does: `…\stdlib\helpers\sort\smallSort.maxon` and
// `…/stdlib/helpers/sort/smallSort.maxon` are the same library file on two hosts, and a prefix swap alone
// would leave the host's separator inside the token so that no ONE expectation could pass on both.
function portableLibraryTail(tail String) returns String
	// A library named with no position after it — nothing bounds the path, so only the root is rewritten.
	// `rewriteDiagnosticLine` declines in the same shape and for the same reason: guessing where a path
	// ends is worse than leaving what follows it alone.
	let cut = try tail.findFirst(PositionSeparator) otherwise return tail

	let relative = tail.slice(tail.startIndex(), endIndex: cut)
	let position = tail.slice(cut, endIndex: tail.endIndex())
	return "{relative.replace(WindowsPathSeparator, with: PathSeparator)}{position}"
end 'portableLibraryTail'

// The single mapping an EXPECTED text needs: the path a `/specs` expectation names for this test,
// and NO renumbering (its lines are already the fragment's). An shv2-native expectation names
// `<fragment>` already and passes through untouched.
function referencePathMapping(specName String, testName String) returns FragmentPathMappingArray
	var paths = FragmentPathMappingArray.create()
	// No directory prefix: an expectation ported from `/specs` carries its own (`dir_b/<fragment>`) in
	// the corpus text, ahead of the fragment path this replaces, and passes through untouched.
	paths.push(FragmentPathMapping.create(referenceFragmentPath(specName, testName: testName), lineOffset: NoLineOffset, sourceDirPrefix: ""))
	return paths
end 'referencePathMapping'

// Rewrite every `<path>:<line>:<col>` to `<token>:<line + lineOffset>:<col>`, where `token` is the
// mapping's `<dir>/<fragment>` (see FragmentPathMapping).
//
// With no offset the path is all that moves, and a whole-text replace does it — that is every
// single-file test, and it is the path this function had before multi-file tests existed.
function rewriteDiagnosticPaths(text String, path String, lineOffset LineIndex, token String) returns String
	if lineOffset == NoLineOffset 'linesAlreadyMatch'
		return text.replace(path, with: token)
	end 'linesAlreadyMatch'

	// An explicit if/else, NOT `rewritten if first else "{out}\n{rewritten}"`. Both arms happen to be
	// owned here so the ternary would not leak — but the construct is a known bootstrap trap the
	// moment one arm is a borrowed field read (OPEN.md #8b), and it is not worth keeping an instance
	// of it around for a reader to copy.
	var out = ""
	var first = true
	for line in text.split(LineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		let rewritten = rewriteDiagnosticLine(line, path: path, lineOffset: lineOffset, token: token)
		if first 'firstLine'
			out = rewritten
			first = false
		end 'firstLine' else 'moreLines'
			out = "{out}{LineSeparator}{rewritten}"
		end 'moreLines'
	end 'eachLine'
	return out
end 'rewriteDiagnosticPaths'

// Rewrite ONE diagnostic line: `<anything><path>:<line>:<col>:<message>`.
//
// Done by SPLITTING ON THE PATH — shv2 has no regex, and it needs none: `split` says precisely
// what this needs to say, and it keeps the whole routine free of index arithmetic. Anything that
// does not have the expected shape falls back to replacing the path and nothing else: a line that
// names the file without a `line:col` has no line number to renumber, and guessing one is worse
// than leaving it.
function rewriteDiagnosticLine(line String, path String, lineOffset LineIndex, token String) returns String
	if not line.contains(path) 'noPath'
		return line
	end 'noPath'

	let halves = line.split(path)
	if halves.count() != PathSplitHalves 'notNamedExactlyOnce'
		return line.replace(path, with: token)
	end 'notNamedExactlyOnce'

	let prefix = try halves.get(0) otherwise panic("rewriteDiagnosticLine: halves.get(0) failed on a {halves.count()}-part split")
	// `:<line>:<col>:<message>` ⇒ ["", line, col, message…]
	let tail = try halves.get(1) otherwise panic("rewriteDiagnosticLine: halves.get(1) failed on a {halves.count()}-part split")

	let fields = tail.split(PositionSeparator)
	if fields.count() < MinPositionFields 'notPositioned'
		return line.replace(path, with: token)
	end 'notPositioned'

	let beforePosition = try fields.get(0) otherwise panic("rewriteDiagnosticLine: fields.get(0) failed on a {fields.count()}-field split")
	if not beforePosition.isEmpty() 'pathNotFollowedByPosition'
		return line.replace(path, with: token)
	end 'pathNotFollowedByPosition'

	let lineText = try fields.get(1) otherwise panic("rewriteDiagnosticLine: fields.get(1) failed on a {fields.count()}-field split")
	let sourceLine = try int.fromString(lineText) otherwise return line.replace(path, with: token)
	let column = try fields.get(2) otherwise panic("rewriteDiagnosticLine: fields.get(2) failed on a {fields.count()}-field split")

	// The message can itself contain the separator (`type mismatch: 'cannot compare int with bool'`),
	// so everything from the message field on is re-joined with the separator it was split on rather
	// than taken as one field and truncated.
	var message = ""
	for i in MessageFieldStart upto fields.count() 'rejoinMessage'
		let field = try fields.get(i) otherwise panic("rewriteDiagnosticLine: fields.get({i}) failed on a {fields.count()}-field split")
		message = "{message}{PositionSeparator}{field}"
	end 'rejoinMessage'

	return "{prefix}{token}{PositionSeparator}{sourceLine + lineOffset}{PositionSeparator}{column}{message}"
end 'rewriteDiagnosticLine'

// The path a /specs expectation names for this very test — RECONSTRUCTED, not pattern-matched
// (shv2 has no regex, and needs none: the runner already knows the spec and test names).
//
// This is what makes a /specs ```maxoncstderr block portable byte-for-byte. The reference actually
// compiles from a TARGET-SUFFIXED directory (`specs/fragments-x64-windows/<spec>/<test>.test`) and
// prints that path in every diagnostic — but before comparing, its runner rewrites
// `specs/fragments-<target>/` back down to a generic `specs/fragments/` (`NormalizeStderr`,
// `TestRunner.cs:1380`). So the generic form is what every expectation in the corpus was AUTHORED
// with, and the generic form is what is reconstructed here. shv2 never reads or writes this
// directory; it only has to recognize the string.
//
// `FragmentExtension` is shared with shv2's own golden path deliberately — `.test` is one
// convention, held by both runners, not two that happen to agree.
function referenceFragmentPath(specName String, testName String) returns String
	return "{ReferenceFragmentDir}/{specName}/{testName}{FragmentExtension}"
end 'referenceFragmentPath'

// Best-effort removal of everything this test wrote: its staged source file(s), the produced exe,
// the IR sidecar and the metrics TSV. A delete failure — e.g. the exe/IR/metrics were never produced
// because the compile failed — is ignored. Correctness does not rest on this succeeding: a multi-file
// test's directory is PURGED before it is written (`stageSourceFiles`), and the metrics file is
// deleted before the compile that would write it (`compileStagedTest`), so a leftover can never join a
// later build or be read as a later compile's cost.
function cleanup(staged StagedTest, exePath FilePath, irPath FilePath, metricsPath FilePath)
	for path in staged.writtenFiles 'eachSource'
		try File.delete(path) otherwise ignore
	end 'eachSource'
	try File.delete(exePath) otherwise ignore
	try File.delete(irPath) otherwise ignore
	try File.delete(metricsPath) otherwise ignore
end 'cleanup'
